    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147218869": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education",
      "description": "Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. \n \n Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Education and Social Practice within Te Pūtahi Mātauranga | Faculty of Arts and Education is seeking a Senior Lecturer in Sport, Health and Physical Education to join our collegial academic team. This is an opportunity to contribute to teaching, research and service across the discipline, including the education of future teachers. You will contribute to the delivery and development of our programmes, supervise postgraduate students and develop an active programme of research, while building collaborative relationships across the School, Faculty and wider community. We are particularly interested in applicants whose teaching and research expertise will complement the School's existing strengths in areas such as anatomy, exercise physiology, Health and Physical Education curriculum, games education or sport pedagogy. Key responsibilities include: Delivering high-quality teaching, contributing to curriculum development and supervising postgraduate students. Developing and maintaining an active programme of research, including scholarly publications, research collaborations and external funding opportunities. Building collaborative relationships across the School, Faculty and wider community to support teaching, research and service. This is a  full-time  (40 hours per week)  permanent  position (subject to continuation). The workload split is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. The salary range for this role is $99,788 - $119,204 per annum. Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and is aligned with our  Academic Standards policy. For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the  Position Description. \n \n He kōrero mōu | About you You are a collaborative academic with expertise in Sport, Health and Physical Education and a passion for educating future teachers. You bring a thoughtful, inclusive approach to your work and are committed to making a positive contribution through teaching, research and engagement. To be successful in this role, you will bring: A PhD in Sport, Health or Physical Education. Experience teaching Sport, Health and Physical Education at the tertiary level. A developing or established research profile, with high-quality research outputs and the potential to contribute to collaborative research and external funding. Experience applying socially critical, inclusive and culturally responsive approaches within teaching, research or professional practice. Strong communication, collaboration and relationship-building skills. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits. \n \nAuckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. \n \n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Sunday 20 September  to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter outlining your interest in and suitability for the role. Please direct enquiries to Rod Philpot (Programme Leader) via r.philpot@auckland.ac.nz. Please note while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications via email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Arts and Education"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147140529": {
      "title": "Innovation Programmes Adviser - Māori Engagement",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  Ko te pae tawhiti, whāia kia tata; ko te pae tata, whakamaua kia tīna. This role combines hands-on programme delivery with a particular focus on strengthening Māori and Pacific participation in innovation and entrepreneurship. As the Innovation Programmes Adviser – Māori Engagement, you’ll manage a portfolio of innovation and entrepreneurship programmes while building connections with Māori and Pacific students, researchers, staff and communities. You’ll help ensure CIE’s programmes are engaging, culturally responsive and create meaningful pathways for greater participation in innovation and entrepreneurship.  Manage the planning, coordination, delivery and continuous improvement of assigned innovation and entrepreneurship programmes.   Build trusted relationships with Māori and Pacific students, staff, communities and relevant networks.   Create high-quality participant experiences and support progression through CIE’s innovation and entrepreneurship pathways.   Work with academics, facilitators, practitioners and other contributors to deliver engaging and effective programmes.   Take particular responsibility for Māori and Pacific-focused programmes and activities within CIE’s programme portfolio.   Use participant feedback, programme data and stakeholder insights to continuously improve programme delivery and participant experience.   Support recruitment, engagement and outreach activities that increase participation from Māori and Pacific communities.   Contribute to a connected programme portfolio that develops innovation and entrepreneurial capability across the University.   This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent role.  For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description .  Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team  The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is Waipapa Taumata Rau’s platform for developing the innovators, entrepreneurs and changemakers of the future. We work across the University to help students, researchers and staff build innovation and entrepreneurial capability, turn ideas into action, develop ventures, and create social, cultural, environmental and economic impact for Aotearoa and the world.   CIE is entering an exciting new chapter. With an ambitious new strategy, we are significantly increasing the reach and impact of innovation and entrepreneurship across the University, helping realise Waipapa Taumata Rau’s ambition to be a global powerhouse of innovation, creativity and entrepreneurship, and strengthening the University’s contribution to a more innovative and prosperous Aotearoa.   We are building a team of people who are curious, collaborative, ambitious for impact and committed to turning ambition into action. If you want to help shape how a world-class research-led university develops innovative and entrepreneurial people, we would love to hear from you.   He kōrero mōu | About you  You are passionate about creating opportunities for others and have strong programme delivery skills. You’re organised, proactive and comfortable taking responsibility for managing and coordinating programmes, working with a range of contributors and creating engaging experiences for participants. You’re also confident building trusted relationships with Māori communities and bring a collaborative and culturally responsive approach to creating environments where people feel welcomed, supported and able to participate.  Experience managing and delivering programmes, projects or initiatives in education, innovation, entrepreneurship, community development or a related field.   Strong programme management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple activities, contributors and priorities.   Demonstrated experience engaging with Māori communities and an understanding of tikanga Māori and culturally responsive practice.   Ability to engage effectively with Pacific communities and support participation and relationship-building initiatives.   Strong relationship-building, communication and participant engagement skills.   Experience working with facilitators, educators, practitioners, academics or community partners to deliver successful programmes.   Initiative and sound judgement, with a practical approach to getting things done in a busy and varied environment.   Ability to use feedback, data and insights to improve programme quality and participant experience.   A genuine interest in innovation and entrepreneurship and in making opportunities accessible and relevant to diverse participants.   What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary ($78,000 - $90,000pa) reflecting experience with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits   \n\n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of  Sunday 13th September, 2026  to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter highlighting how you meet the skills and experience outlined above, what attracts you to this opportunity, and what you would bring to CIE and our work with Māori and Pacific communities.  If you would value an informal kōrero about the role, the team or the kaupapa before applying, please contact Josh Chapman j.chapman@auckland.ac.nz Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s pre-eminent University, with a turnover of $1.6bn, including research revenue of over $300m generated across eight diverse faculties and two research institutes. With over 9,000 staff and 50,000 students, the University of Auckland is one of New Zealand’s most significant and complex entities.",
      "department": "Research and Innovation"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147096468": {
      "title": "Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Services Adviser - part-time (18.75 hrs pw)",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is a people-centered role with a strong focus on building trusted relationships. You’ll be someone staff can approach when they’re looking for guidance, connection, or a pathway forward. You’ll listen carefully, understand their needs, provide appropriate information and support, and confidently connect them with specialist services when matters sit outside your role. You’ll combine empathy and sound judgment with strong organisation, clear communication, and the ability to turn ideas into action. Working closely with staff networks and internal partners, you’ll help build strong network cultures, support effective ways of working, and deliver meaningful initiatives that strengthen our staff community. You’ll also play an important role in building organisational capability by helping staff and managers develop their understanding and confidence in inclusive workplace practices. Through your work, you’ll support managers and staff communities in creating and maintaining workplaces where people feel respected, supported, connected, and included. Nga Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Acting as a trusted first point of contact for staff seeking information, guidance or support relating to diversity, equity, inclusion and belonging. Support staff with sensitivity and sound judgement, understanding individual needs, maintaining confidentiality and professional boundaries, and appropriately referring people to specialist services and support. Work alongside staff networks and diverse communities, helping strengthen connection, participation and sustainable network cultures while supporting networks to achieve their goals. Build organisational capability by developing and sharing accessible guidance and resources on inclusive workplace practices Facilitate workshops, information sessions and group discussions that are engaging, inclusive and practical to support meaningful learning. Coordinate initiatives, budgets, timelines and competing priorities, ensuring commitments are delivered effectively and within agreed timeframes. Build strong partnerships across the University, working collaboratively with staff, managers, networks and specialist services to strengthen inclusive practice and the overall staff experience. This is a part-time (18.75 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $78,601 to $90,700 per annum, depending on skills and experience, and will be pro-rated. For more detailed information, please refer to the  Position Description   He kōrero mōu | About you You’ll bring high emotional intelligence, empathy, and sound judgment, combined with confidence, clarity, and a strong focus on delivery. You’ll be comfortable supporting people through sensitive or complex conversations while maintaining appropriate professional boundaries, recognising the limits of your role, and knowing when to seek advice or connect people with specialist support. You will bring: A tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline such as communications, education, human rights, social work or community development, or an equivalent Demonstrated commitment to equity, inclusion and Te Tiriti o Waitangi, through your contribution to inclusive practices and equitable outcomes for diverse communities. Minimum two years' experience working with diverse or underrepresented communities, ideally in a community, tertiary, organisational or service environment. Excellent interpersonal and cross-cultural communication skills, with the ability to establish trust and build relationships across diverse groups. Experience supporting or working alongside communities and networks, with an understanding of the importance of connection, belonging and community-led approaches. The ability to identify issues, analyse information and develop practical, inclusive solutions to complex or ambiguous situations. Strong facilitation skills, with the ability to deliver workshops, training and group discussions that encourage participation and support practical learning. Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage budgets, timelines, competing priorities and stakeholder expectations. A high level of confidentiality, discretion and professional integrity. The confidence to know when to listen, when to provide guidance, when to challenge constructively and when to connect someone with specialist support Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and several other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 20th September 2026, and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions about this role, please get in touch with Sarah Krieg (Equity Comms & Marketing Adv) via sarah.krieg@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential discussion. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n  Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties and 11 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.  Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability .",
      "department": "Human Resources"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147094829": {
      "title": "Sustainability Communications Adviser",
      "description": "With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, we're boosting our strategic communications capabilities to help shape and enhance the reputation of the University in the eyes of its most important audiences.    Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our  Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement  functions.     Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity   As our Sustainability Communications Adviser, you’ll have the opportunity to influence how sustainability is understood and communicated across one of New Zealand's largest and most complex organisations.   Working as part of the Strategic Communications team, you'll partner with sustainability leaders, researchers, academic staff and professional teams from across the University to develop compelling content that supports engagement, reputation and understanding.  From sustainability reporting and impact stories to rankings submissions and strategic communications campaigns, you'll help bring complex topics to life for a wide variety of audiences.  Key areas of focus:   Provide communications advice to sustainability stakeholders across the organisation  Research, write and edit engaging sustainability content across digital, web, social, internal and external channels   Translate complex sustainability topics into clear, accessible and compelling communications   Identify and develop stories showcasing sustainability initiatives, research, partnerships and outcomes  Support sustainability reporting and impact reporting initiatives   Contribute to SDG reporting, rankings submissions and external benchmarking communications   Develop content that supports sustainability engagement, understanding and reputation  This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $87,000 - $118,000 per annum    We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $93,201 – $107,500 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our  commitment to pay equity .   For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the  position description   kōrero mōu | About you   You'll be a strong communicator and storyteller who enjoys taking complex information and transforming it into engaging content that people can understand and connect with.  You'll bring:  Proven experience developing high-quality communications content across multiple channels  Exceptional writing and editing skills  Strong stakeholder engagement and relationship-building capability  The ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines  A commitment to plain language, accessibility and audience-focused communications  Strong attention to detail and organisational skills  A collaborative and proactive approach  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer   Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world.    Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University.   We also offer:   A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns   A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier   Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme,    Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces   A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance.    The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff   The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply   Please submit an application online by the closing date of 3 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience.   Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications.   Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements.   \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147093218": {
      "title": "Pouako|Lecturer - Earth Science - Te Kura Mātai Taiao - School of Environment ",
      "description": "Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the school The School of Environment is home to a vibrant and diverse community of students, academics and professional staff pursuing research and teaching across the social and natural sciences. Alongside Earth Science, the School brings together internationally recognised disciplinary programmes in Geography, Geology, Geophysics and Geographical Information Science, as well as interdisciplinary programmes in Environmental Management, Environmental Science. This creates a highly collaborative environment where researchers can connect across disciplines and explore new approaches to some of the most important environmental challenges facing our world. Earth Science is an integral part of this broader community, providing opportunities to collaborate nationally and internationally and to contribute to research that connects past, present and future Earth system processes. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a researcher and educator with a PhD in Earth Science, Geography, Environmental Science, We are seeking a paleoenvironmental science researcher and educator with expertise in one or more of the following areas: paleoclimate, climate, paleoceanography, paleoecology, paleontology, palynology, and environmental change, as well as related fields such as biogeochemistry and planetary geology. The candidate will hold a PhD in Earth Science, Geological Science, Environmental Science, Evolutionary Biology or a related discipline, and a developing record of high-quality research publications. This is an exciting opportunity for an academic who is passionate about research, enjoys working collaboratively and wants to contribute to the development of the next generation of Earth and environmental scientists. We welcome researchers who use field, laboratory, analytical, computational, and/or modelling approaches and who are excited by opportunities to work across disciplines. Ngā Haepapa Matua | Key Responsibilities (not limited to): Contribute to the teaching and delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate Earth Science courses, particularly relating to Earth History, paleoenvironments, and environmental change. Conduct research and contribute to the development of an independent research programme in Earth Science. Supervise postgraduate research students in Earth Science and related areas, supporting their academic and research development. Contribute meaningfully to the School's collegial working environment by working collaboratively with staff, students, and relevant external and professional organisations. For more detailed information review this  position description   This is a full-time (40 hrs per week) permanent role The remuneration is between $99,788 - $109,496 per annum, dependent on skills and experience  He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for an engaged, forward-thinking, and collegial academic who is excited about research and enjoys collaborating with colleagues and students. You will hold a PhD in Earth Science, Geological Science, Geography, Environmental Science, Evolutionary Biology, or a closely related discipline. You will also bring: a track record of research demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications, appropriate to your career stage; research expertise in Earth Science, with alignment to paleoclimate and/or climate-linked Earth system science; experience in, or a strong interest in tertiary level teaching; excellent communication and interpersonal skills; the ability to work effectively as part of a diverse and collaborative team; a collegial approach and enthusiasm for engaging with colleagues and students across disciplines; and the potential to develop an internationally recognised research programme. Teaching experience is preferred, as is experience demonstrating the ability to contribute to Earth Science teaching and research programs. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave Opportunity for Research and Study Leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 25th September 2026  to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV, highlighting how you can meet the aforementioned skills and experience. Your CV should include a list of refereed publications and a list of courses you have taught, clearly indicating whether you were giving lectures or tutorials and the level of independence in your teaching. Also, if you have recent student evaluations of your teaching or other teaching reviews, please upload them. If needed, you may reach out to Lorna Strachan (Associate Professor) via l.strachan@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science  at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world.  Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tuākana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae .",
      "department": "Faculty of Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146893670": {
      "title": "Learning Management System (LMS) Coordinator",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Looking for a role where your work has tangible impact? As IMAC LMS Coordinator, you'll help power the learning systems behind New Zealand's national immunisation education courses, enabling healthcare professionals across the country to access essential training that supports confident, effective vaccination practice. The Immunisation Advisory Centre (IMAC) is New Zealand's national immunisation education provider, based within Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. IMAC provides independent, evidence-based information on vaccine-preventable diseases and immunisation, drawing on both New Zealand and international scientific research. To learn more about IMAC, visit www.immune.org.nz . In this varied and rewarding position, you will: Contribute to the management of IMAC’s online courses from end-to-end Analyse issues and troubleshoot system errors Have a key role in continuous quality improvement across IMAC’s digital systems Support IMAC’s digital transformation projects For more information on the role, please view the  Position Description . This is a part time (22.5h – 30h per week) fixed term position until 30 July 2027 The salary range is between $68,000 - $75,000 (pro-rated) per annum, dependent on skills and experience He kōrero mōu | About you You’re an enthusiastic team player and ready for challenge. Have experience in managing customers and eye for detail to help resolve learning management system issues. To be successful in this role, you'll: Have a tertiary degree or equivalent professional qualification in a relevant discipline, or equivalent related experience.  Have Computer / e-learning certification or demonstrated equivalent ability Experience of providing training or support to staff and colleagues, preferably in an educational context.  Experience managing and/or supporting integrated information systems (e.g. Learning Management Systems (LMS), Customer Relationship Management (CRM) systems, Student Information Systems (SIS), or similar enterprise/business systems). Experience troubleshooting and resolving technical or system-access issues in a customer-facing or service-desk capacity. Previous experience with learning management systems. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer \nThe University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently ranked among the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 14 September 2026  to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV, highlighting how you can meet the skills and experience detailed above. Please reach out to Theo Brandt, IMAC Information Manager via  t.brandt@auckland.ac.nz if you have questions about the role. Please note applications are not accepted via email.     #LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences is a diverse faculty with more than 10,000 students studying across six schools, led by world-leading researchers.  We are committed to improving the health of our local, national and global communities through excellence in teaching, research, service and engagement. \n \nOur mission and values place a strong emphasis on both our student body and our scholarly community, and we work with both community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the peoples of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to pursuing sustainability.",
      "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146878839": {
      "title": "Senior Digital Marketing Specialist",
      "description": "Lead and optimise digital acquisition campaigns across Google, Meta and LinkedIn Bring strong technical expertise in digital analytics, attribution and performance optimisation Enjoy hybrid work, a 37.5-hour week, and inclusive benefits that support wellbeing and caring commitments \n  Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, we create globally transformative impacts through world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships across Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. We are dedicated to creating knowledge, transforming lives and shaping the future. This opportunity sits within Marketing and Recruitment , a core enabling function within the Strategic Engagement portfolio. The function provides specialist marketing and recruitment services across areas including brand and market positioning, market research, targeted performance campaigns, recruitment activity, and partner and channel management. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for an experienced Senior Digital Marketing Specialist to join our Digital & Performance team. This is a hands-on role focused on delivering and optimising digital acquisition campaigns across the University’s priority portfolios. You will bring strong technical expertise across digital channels including Google, Meta and LinkedIn, using performance data, testing and analytics to improve campaign outcomes. You’ll work across a broad range of campaigns, collaborating with colleagues across Marketing and Recruitment and stakeholders across the University. You will also provide technical guidance to other digital practitioners and work with media agencies, platform representatives and technology partners. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity currently based at our Newmarket campus. The full salary range for this role is $98,700 - $133,500 - Band H . We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . This role offers a 37.5-hour working week, flexible start and finish times, and a hybrid working pattern, supporting a strong commitment to work/life balance. For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description. Ngā haepapa matua | Key focus areas Planning, executing and optimising paid digital campaigns across Google, Meta, LinkedIn and other digital channels Managing campaign performance and budgets to improve conversion and cost efficiency Using analytics, attribution and performance data to identify insights and opportunities Designing and implementing testing across audiences, creative and channels Providing technical guidance and quality assurance to other digital practitioners Collaborating with internal stakeholders and external digital partners to deliver effective campaigns He kōrero mōu | About you You’re an experienced digital marketing specialist who can step into the role and make an immediate impact. You’ll bring strong technical capability, sound judgement and a collaborative approach, with the ability to work at pace and take ownership of campaigns. You’ll bring: Extensive experience delivering and optimising multi-channel digital campaigns across paid search, paid social, programmatic, display and remarketing Strong technical knowledge of digital analytics, attribution, testing and performance optimisation Experience managing significant digital budgets and delivering measurable results The ability to turn performance data into clear insights and recommendations Experience providing technical guidance or mentoring to other digital practitioners, with strong stakeholder engagement and communication skills The ability to work autonomously, manage competing priorities and adapt to changing needs Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Flexible employment practices, including hybrid working and flexible start and finish times Up to 6.75% employer superannuation scheme Five weeks’ annual leave and a 37.5-hour working week Gender-neutral parental leave policy, including up to nine weeks’ full pay On-site, discounted childcare centres, including Kōhanga Reo Confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services In addition, we offer broad professional development opportunities, active staff networks , and a range of other staff benefits to support you to thrive in and outside of work. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of Wednesday 16th September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Applications will be assessed on a rolling basis. Shortlisted candidates may be contacted before the advertised closing date, and the position may close earlier if a suitable candidate is identified. We encourage interested applicants to not delay their application.  Please reach out to Milena Cooper on milena.cooper@auckland.ac.nz with any questions. \n \n \n#LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Deputy VC (Education)"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146843709": {
      "title": "Event Technician ",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an Event Technician to join our Event Services team to coordinate and deliver audio-visual and logistical support across a wide range of University of Auckland events, faculties and departments. Based across our campuses, this is a hands-on role supporting both in-person and online events. In this varied and practical role, you will be responsible for setting up and operating technical and event equipment, delivering logistics support, and ensuring high-quality service to internal clients. Your work will include supporting live events, webinars and streamed content, as well as managing event setups and pack downs. You will work both independently and as part of a collaborative team in a fast-paced environment. This role requires flexibility, with evening and weekend work required as part of event delivery. Your responsibilities will include: Delivering audio-visual and technical support, including setup, operation and troubleshooting of equipment Supporting online and hybrid events, including webinars, live streaming and video management Coordinating event logistics, including equipment setup, furniture and resource planning Managing bookings, schedules and enquiries to ensure smooth event deliver Maintaining health and safety standards, including risk assessment and compliance requirements Supporting team operations, including supervising casual staff and maintaining organised storage areas This is a permanent full-time position (37.5 hours per week). The salary range for this role is $60,000 to $70,000 (pro-rated, for part time), negotiable depending on skills and experience. A full description of your duties and the role are provided in the position description linked HERE . He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced Event Technician with a strong background in audio-visual support and event delivery. You are confident working with a range of technical equipment and enjoy being hands-on in delivering high-quality events, including live and online formats. You bring a proactive and positive approach, with strong interpersonal skills that enable you to work effectively with a variety of stakeholders. You are organised, adaptable and comfortable working under pressure, whether independently or as part of a team. You are also physically capable of manual setup and pack down and take pride in delivering reliable, professional service. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking Relevant qualification in audio-visual production, lighting or a related field, or equivalent experience At least five years’ experience providing audio-visual support for events and functions Demonstrated experience operating audio-visual and event production equipment Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to prioritise effectively High level of digital capability, including Microsoft Office applications A full driver’s licence and willingness to work flexible hours, including evenings and weekends Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave. Discounted public Transport  for perm and long term fixed term staff  Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75%. Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions. Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on‑site amenities . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Thursday, 17 September 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Anthony Brandon, Events Technical Team Leader at a.brandon@auckland.ac.nz  #LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Campus Environment"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146841029": {
      "title": "Facilities and Services Manager ",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Facilities and Services Manager to join our team in the Faculty of Engineering and Design. You will lead the facilities team in managing the provision of functional spaces to all academic departments and administrative functions in the faculty. You will be the primary liaison to Campus Environment to ensure the adequate provision of maintenance, cleaning, access control, space planning and minor capital works projects. You will also ensure that health and safety, containment, sustainability, and environmental requirements are met. Key responsibilities include: Provide leadership, mentoring and direction to the to the Facilities team. Responsible for the implementation of operational plans for Facilities and Services Co-ordinate and manage Minor Capital Works projects for the faculty. Liaise with Property Services for Space Planning Develop and maintain relationships with other Facilities and Services Managers and departments throughout the University. Manage and monitor service delivery of all Facilities and Services activities. This is a full-time, 37.5 hours per week role, permanent. The salary range for this role is $87,000 - $107,500 per annum depending on experience and qualifications. For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description.   Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team Our faculty has a strong and innovative vision for the future, delivering learning and research excellence to enable our students to thrive and succeed and become graduates who make a positive impact on their community and world around them.   He kōrero mōu | About you Our suitable candiate will meet the following requirements: 5 years’ experience in operational or facilities roles demonstrated skills and experience in leading a small team of staff. Demonstrated experience working with maintaining health & safety compliance. Demonstrated knowledge of reporting, administration and general information systems. Demonstrated skills and experience in team building, change management and working co-operatively To be successful in this role you will need to be a self-starter with strong skills in relationship development and stakeholder engagement who is comfortable working within an ambiguous and complex environment.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits .   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 16/9/2026   to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the abovementioned skills and experiences. Please note that we may close the advert earlier if we find a suitable candidate. Please reach out to Stephen Warrington via s.warrington@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.",
      "department": "Engineering and Design"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146831129": {
      "title": "Professional Teaching Fellow - Humanities (TFC)",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga Mahi | The opportunity The He Ara Takatū – Tertiary Foundation Certificate (TFC) team is seeking two Professional Teaching Fellows in Humanities to support students preparing for tertiary study and to deliver teaching that makes a meaningful impact. The roles are being recruited in response to growing student enrolments and increasing demand for Tertiary Foundation Certificate (TFC) English courses , with teaching at both our City and Te Tai Tonga (Manukau) campuses. One position is permanent and one is a three-year fixed term role. As a Professional Teaching Fellow in TFC English, you’ll be at the heart of: Teaching foundation-level English tailored to the diverse learning needs of TFC students and preparing them for undergraduate study in the humanities and across the University Coordinating and teaching TFC English courses across in-person, asynchronous and blended delivery modes Providing high-quality, research-informed learning and assessment, alongside pastoral support that is essential to enabling student success Supporting students one-on-one, helping them build confidence and capability as they prepare for tertiary study Contributing to course and curriculum development as TFC English continues to grow and expand across campuses and delivery modes Contributing to a strong and collegial team culture across TFC, Humanities and English The workload allocation for these positions is 80% teaching and 20% service. There are two positions available: One full-time (40 hours per week), permanent position One full-time (40 hours per week), three-year fixed-term position If you are only interested in one of the contract types, please indicate your preference in your covering letter. Otherwise, we will assume you are open to being considered for both positions. Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and will be aligned with our  Academic Standards policy . It will be in the range of $105,791-$116,929.  For more information about this role, please refer to the  Position Description. \n\n He kōrero mōu | About you The ideal candidate will hold a Masters or PhD in English or a related discipline and have experience teaching English in a high-school, college or university setting. We particularly value experience teaching in foundation-level or related programmes.  You will be confident coordinating courses and using a learning management system such as Canvas and enjoy working with learners at varying levels and from diverse backgrounds. You will communicate clearly, collaborate effectively and bring a positive, student-centred approach to your teaching. You are also keen to innovate, adapt and contribute to research-informed teaching and the continued development of TFC English. \n\n Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent teaching, research and campus facilities 5 weeks annual leave  Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and several discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Sunday 13 September 2026 (11.59pm) . Please include your cover letter and CV, including a teaching statement, teaching record, and (if applicable) summarised teaching evaluations. Please reach out to Andrew Dawson (TFC Director) or   Lisa Bailey (Acting HoS Humanities) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ranked #47 in the QS Ranking for Arts & Humanities Ranked 5th in Oceania and 28th globally for sustainability (QS Sustainability Rankings 2026) Support our growing Tertiary Foundation Certificate course offerings The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Situated within Te Pūtahi Mātauranga | The Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna Aronui | Humanities is Aotearoa’s leading centre for humanities scholarship, home to the largest concentration of humanities academics in the country. The School includes English, Drama and Comparative Literature alongside Art History, Asian Studies and Languages, History, Philosophy, Media and Screen Studies, and more. He Ara Takatū | Tertiary Foundation Certificate is New Zealand's largest foundation programme for domestic students. It provides an alternative entry to university for those who require further preparation for the degree and/or subjects they aspire to study, with a particular focus on on literacy and numeracy. TFC particularly focuses on providing access to Māori, Pasifika, students from low-socio economic backgrounds, students with disabilities, and students from refugee backgrounds.",
      "department": "Arts and Education"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146823049": {
      "title": "Media & Communications Partnering Lead (Education, Faculty)",
      "description": "With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures.   As part of our newly established Communications function, we've created the role of Media and Communications Partnering Lead (Education and Faculty) to head up a new team of embedded Media and Communications Partners.  This is a rare opportunity for an experienced and people-focused communications leader, to help shape the future direction of communications at New Zealand's leading University.  Find out more about how we’re reshaping how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions  Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement      Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  Reporting to the Director of Communications, you’ll lead a team of five trusted Media and Communications Partners. Together you’ll create a new partnering model to deliver high-quality strategic advice and communications support for the University’s faculties (Law, Business & Economics, Arts & Education, Science, Engineering & Design and Medical and Health Sciences).  As a trusted advisor to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Education and the Education Office, you’ll support strategic communications planning and delivery, stakeholder engagement and reputation-sensitive activity relating to the University’s education portfolio.   With a strong media focus, your team will proactively tell the University’s most compelling stories, shaping reputation and contributing to our social licence. We want to talk about not only what the University is good at, but what it’s good for.    Key areas of focus:  Lead, coach and develop a team of communications professionals, embedding a future-focussed partnering model built on expertise and collaboration.  Provide strategic communications counsel and reputation management expertise to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Education, Education Office and other senior leaders.   Oversee communications planning, messaging, and delivery, helping identify the audiences that matter most, the channels to reach them and the stories that will resonate.  Support communications responses during emerging issues and crisis situations.   This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $114,400 - $169,800 per annum.  We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $122,501 – $155,000 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our  commitment to pay equity .  For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the  position description   He kōrero mōu | About you  We're particularly interested in people who bring:  Significant experience in strategic communications, media relations, communications business partnering or public relations within a large and complex organisation.  Proven people leadership experience, including coaching, mentoring, performance development and leading teams through change.  Experience managing reputationally sensitive communications issues and supporting organisational priorities in dynamic environments.  Sound understanding of New Zealand’s changing media landscape and the impacts of AI on how audiences are engaging with content.   Cultural competency and experience in shaping communications for diverse audiences, with a strong understanding of Te Ao Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi.  Strong stakeholder engagement and influencing skills, with the ability to build trusted relationships across all levels of an organisation.  Excellent planning, prioritisation and problem-solving abilities.  Confidence operating in ambiguity and balancing strategic oversight with hands-on support when required.  A commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi, equity, inclusion and culturally responsive practice.    Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer  Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world.   Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community.   We also offer:  A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns  A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier  Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme,   Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces  A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance.   The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff  The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations    Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Please submit an application online by the closing date of 1 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience.  Please reach out to Verity Collins  verity.collins@auckland.ac.nz  with any questions.  Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications.  Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements.  \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146821950": {
      "title": "Strategic Communications Lead",
      "description": "With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, we've created the role of Strategic Communications Lead to help shape and enhance the reputation of the University in the eyes of its most important audiences.   Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our  Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement  functions.  Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity   We're seeking an exceptional strategic communications leader to help shape and enhance the reputation of the University and build trust with our key audiences. This is a pivotal leadership role for a communications professional who is adept in using data and insights to identify our important audiences, understand their perceptions, shape our narrative, and translate organisational priorities into compelling communication strategies that create measurable impact. Reporting to the Director of Communications, you'll lead the Strategic Communications function, guiding the development of the University's institutional narrative, strategic positioning, executive communications, and reputation-building activity.  You'll be responsible for helping answer some of the University's most important communications questions:  How do we want the University to be known and understood?  What do our audiences think about us today?  What needs to change to achieve our strategic ambitions?  Working across a broad range of portfolios and stakeholders, you'll ensure communication strategies are insight-led, audience-focused and aligned to long-term organisational outcomes.  Key areas of focus include:   Shape and evolve the University's institutional narrative and strategic messaging   Use audience data and insights to develop communications strategies that strengthen reputation, trust and stakeholder engagement   Lead communications approaches that support the University's global standing and strategic priorities   Build audience-led messaging frameworks and positioning approaches   Provide strategic communications leadership during issues, incidents and reputationally sensitive situations  Partner with the Vice-Chancellor's Office and senior leadership team on complex communications matters   Support major institutional announcements, initiatives and change programmes    This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $114,400 - $169,800 per annum. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $122,501 – $155,000 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our  commitment to pay equity .   For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the  position description   He kōrero mōu | About you   You are an accomplished communications leader and experienced in operating in complex, high-profile environments where reputation and stakeholder trust matter.  We're particularly interested in people who bring:   Significant experience in strategic communications, corporate affairs or reputation management   Experience advising senior executives and organisational leaders   Strong understanding of audience insight, segmentation and messaging strategy   Proven success developing organisational narratives and positioning frameworks   Experience managing issues, risk and crisis communications   Exceptional writing, storytelling and communication capability   Strong leadership experience with a track record of building high-performing teams   Cultural competency and experience in shaping communications for diverse audiences, with a strong understanding of Te Ao Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi.  The ability to influence, challenge constructively and build trusted stakeholder relationships  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer   Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world.    Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University.   We also offer:   A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns   A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier   Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme,    Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces   A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance.    The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff   The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations      Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply   Please submit an application online by the closing date of 1 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience.   Please reach out to Verity Collins  verity.collins@auckland.ac.nz  with any questions.       Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications.   Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements.   \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146603007": {
      "title": "Strategic Communications Adviser ",
      "description": "With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, we're boosting our strategic communications capabilities to help shape and enhance the reputation of the University in the eyes of its most important audiences.     Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our  Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement  functions.     Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity   We're looking for a talented Strategic Communications Adviser to join the University of Auckland's Communications team. This is an opportunity to work on some of the University's most important communications priorities, working closely with the Strategic Communications Lead to shape how we communicate our impact and strengthen our reputation both nationally and internationally  Working alongside experienced communications professionals, you'll turn complex information into clear, engaging stories, support strategic initiatives, and help manage communications in high-profile and time-sensitive situations.  Key areas of focus:   Taking a data and insights led approach to developing strategic communications to support the University's priorities, reputation, and long-term positioning.   Creating high-quality content including leadership communications, institutional announcements, and strategic messaging.   Supporting communications related to the University's global reputation and rankings.   Contributing to issues and crisis communications activities, helping ensure timely, accurate, and coordinated responses.   Partnering with subject matter experts and stakeholders across the University to develop compelling communications solutions.   Helping ensure communications are clear, consistent, accessible, and aligned to organisational standards.    This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $87,000 - $118,000 per annum    We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $93,201 – $107,500 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our  commitment to pay equity .   For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the  position description   kōrero mōu | About you   You’ll be someone who is motivated in using communication to make complex ideas accessible, build understanding, and help tell stories that matter.  We're particularly interested in people who bring:   Exceptional writing, editing, and storytelling skills, with the ability to translate complex, technical, strategic, or sensitive information into clear and engaging communications.   Experience developing content for senior leaders and stakeholders, with a strong understanding of audience, tone, and messaging.   Demonstrated ability to support issues management and crisis communications, remaining calm and effective under pressure while managing competing priorities and tight deadlines.   Strong stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to build trusted relationships and work collaboratively with a diverse range of subject matter experts, leaders, and teams.   Experience in using data and insights to inform communications planning, messaging and measurement.  Cultural competency and experience in shaping communications for diverse audiences, with a strong understanding of Te Ao Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi.  Sound judgement and attention to detail, with a commitment to accuracy, consistency, and delivering work to a high professional standard.  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer   Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world.    Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University.   We also offer:   A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns   A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier   Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme,    Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces   A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance.    The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff   The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations      Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply   Please submit an application online by the closing date of 1 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience.     Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications.   Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements.   \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146582360": {
      "title": "Exam Supervisor ",
      "description": "The opportunity | Te Whiwhinga mahi We are seeking to recruit exam room invigilators to provide supervision of students during end of semester exams. You will be responsible for supervising and ensuring compliance during exam sessions to ensure the security and integrity of the exam environment and you will play an integral role in assisting students during a particularly stressful part of their studies. You will hold integrity in high regard, pay close attention to detail and be able to remain vigilant for prolonged periods. You will be able to adapt to mixed modes of exam delivery, including on campus digital exams, traditional paper based exams and remote, online invigilation. You will be responsible for: Setting up the exam room by placing exam papers, stationery and equipment at desks in accordance with procedures and spacing requirements. Implementing exam rules and regulations and remaining attentive throughout the exam, monitoring student behaviour. Assisting before, during and after the exam by directing students to their seats, advising them about possessions permitted in the exam room, and handling queries raised. Supervising carefully, making sure that students do not communicate with one another during the exam, and responding to any irregularities immediately. Checking attendance during exams and recording details of late arrivals. Escorting candidates on washroom breaks as required. Detecting any unauthorized materials inside the exam room and ensuring that academic integrity is upheld at all times. Delivering and collecting exam scripts carefully at the start and end of the exam in accordance with procedures. Be able to adapt to different modes of exams throughout the day and/or exam period. Support exam processes You will need to be available as needed throughout the exam period. Semester One exams run from 29 October through to 16 November. You must be available for in-person training prior to the exam period. For more detailed information, please refer to our webpage. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 11/09/2026 to be considered. However, do not wait to submit your application as we may begin hiring sooner. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Henry Bell via henry.bell@auckland.ac.nz for more information. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email.     #LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Assessment Services oversee the successful delivery of exams for students.",
      "department": "Student and Scholarly Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146576759": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Postgraduate Recruitment",
      "description": "Auckland | Permanent | Full-time Hybrid working environment (mix of on-site / work from home) Marketing & Recruitment, Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland $134,900 - $202,500 per annum (Band J), based on experience   Lead New Zealand's largest postgraduate recruitment function Own the strategy, partnerships and performance that drives domestic postgraduate recruitment growth Balance ambitious work with flexibility - hybrid work, 37.5-hour week, and inclusive benefits that recognise life beyond work   Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We're looking for a commercially minded leader to shape and deliver the University's domestic postgraduate student recruitment function supporting working professionals, postgraduate, and doctoral students. This is a dynamic senior leadership role that will build, develop and oversee high-performing teams responsible for lead management, prospective conversion, new customer acquisition and partnerships at scale. Reporting to the Director of Marketing and Recruitment, you'll set the strategic direction and operational framework for domestic postgraduate recruitment and lead an integrated team spanning student recruitment, business development and partnership growth. You'll be accountable for performance across direct-to-student (B2C) and employer-led (B2B) channels, building a sustainable pipeline of professional and postgraduate learners while strengthening the University's strategic relationships with employers, industry and government. An integral part of the Marketing and Recruitment senior leadership team, you’ll share accountability for outcomes that extend beyond your portfolio and contributing to the development of a newly established function designed for long-term impact. Find out more about our future-focused Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions and vision. Key areas of focus include: Defining and leading the domestic postgraduate recruitment strategy and operational framework across key market segments Driving measurable growth across the full recruitment funnel, from awareness through to enrolment Building and scaling strategic partnerships with employers, industry bodies and government organisations Leading B2C and B2B acquisition activity, including lead, conversion and pipeline management Establishing robust forecasting, reporting, lead scoring and performance frameworks Leading service improvement, conversion optimisation and recruitment innovation initiatives Identify and develop business requirements for new technology and AI-enabled tools to bolster optimisation Building a high-performing team culture focused on accountability, growth and continuous improvement Managing budgets, investment decisions and the commercial performance of the postgraduate portfolio Providing strategic advice, market insight and growth recommendations to senior stakeholders across the University. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $134,900 - $202,500 per annum (Band J). We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Salary decisions for individual appointments are made based on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description .   He kōrero mōu | About you You could be leading customer acquisition, business development, sales, partnerships, growth or commercial performance within a large organisation. Experience in education sectors is not necessary, as this role is about establishing growth and acquisition methodologies and funnel logic on a significant scale, so you may come from sectors like SaaS, telco, subscription organisations, financial services or marketplace businesses where revenue, pipeline, customer acquisition and relationship management sit at the centre of success. You've likely led teams with performance targets, built strategic partnerships, improved pipeline quality and managed growth initiatives that require coordination across multiple stakeholders and functions. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Significant experience leading management and acquisition, sales, partnership or commercial growth functions Demonstrated success delivering YoY business growth Experience leading high-performing teams and developing capability Strong commercial and financial acumen Experience using CRM, automation, analytics and reporting to improve outcomes Strong stakeholder engagement, influencing and negotiation skills A data-driven and customer-centred mindset Experience operating within complex organisations and navigating multiple stakeholder groups Resilience, judgement and a strong bias toward action and results.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Few organisations can offer the scale, complexity and opportunity available in this role. This is a chance to shape one of the University's most important growth portfolios and influence how professional and postgraduate education are positioned, marketed and delivered across Aotearoa. You'll have the opportunity to influence strategy, partnerships, customer experience, technology and growth while building a function designed for sustainable long-term impact. For the right person, this is an opportunity to achieve in a few years what may take considerably longer within a more mature organisation. You'll help define the future state, build capability, develop talent and leave a lasting impact on an organisation whose purpose extends far beyond commercial outcomes. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of  Sunday 27th September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Faith Pallant, Recruitment Manager on f.pallant@auckland.ac.nz with any questions.   Reasonable accommodations  - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people  - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Marketing and Recruitment"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146573500": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Performance and Digital",
      "description": "Auckland | Permanent | Full-time Hybrid working environment (mix of on-site / work from home) Marketing & Recruitment, Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland $134,900 - $202,500 per annum (Band J), based on experience   Own the strategy, governance and performance of a University-wide digital and performance marketing capability Purpose over products – ours are creating knowledge, transforming lives, shaping futures Balance ambitious work with flexibility - hybrid work, 37.5-hour week, and inclusive benefits that recognise life beyond work   Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is a senior leadership role for someone who can set the strategic and operational agenda for performance marketing and digital at university scale. Someone who still wants to be close enough to the work to know how it actually gets delivered. Reporting to the Director of Marketing and Recruitment, you'll set the strategic direction, governance and operating framework for the University's performance marketing and digital capability, and lead three specialist managers; Web, Digital and Content, and Digital Marketing, through to delivery. You'll own significant media and technology budgets, shape a connected digital experience across every channel and touchpoint in the prospective student journey, and manage the agency and platform partnerships that support it. You'll be part of the Marketing and Recruitment senior leadership team, sharing accountability for outcomes that go beyond your own portfolio; driving the digital and performance maturity of the wider University. Find out more about our reimagining of digital, web and performance, and our broader Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions. Key areas of focus include: Setting and leading the University's performance marketing strategy across all paid digital channels Driving acquisition, conversion and pipeline quality across the full student recruitment funnel Providing strategic leadership across web, digital platforms and marketing automation Building advanced measurement, attribution and ROI frameworks, and translating them into decisions senior stakeholders can act on Establishing enterprise-level governance for digital and performance investment, risk and compliance Leading and growing a high-performing team, and lifting digital capability and ways of working across the University Running experimentation, CRO and customer-journey optimisation programmes Managing agency and partnerships while building in-house capability over time Championing data-informed decision-making and performance transparency across the function This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $134,900 - $202,500 per annum (Band J). We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Salary decisions for individual appointments are made based on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description .   He kōrero mōu | About you You could be leading performance marketing, digital or growth for a large, complex organisation, or leading a portfolio of clients at an agency. Either way, you understand how modern acquisition ecosystems work end to end, and you know how to lead the people and functions that run them. You've likely built acquisition programmes from the ground up, scaled marketing technology platforms, led digital optimisation initiatives, or grown high-performing performance marketing teams. What matters most is that you understand how digital performance actually works, and that you can build a function that's sustainable, ethical and built to keep improving. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Significant experience leading performance marketing at scale, in a large or complex organisation Deep expertise across paid search, paid social, programmatic and digital acquisition Experience owning a marketing technology, automation and analytics stack Strong commercial and analytical judgement A track record of leading digital transformation and capability uplift Proven people leadership, including through change A track record of using data to sharpen marketing performance and investment decisions A genuine appetite for continuous improvement and innovation   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Few organisations can offer the scale, complexity and opportunity available in this role. This is a chance to join at the beginning of a significant transformation and shape the future of digital marketing for one of New Zealand's most recognised organisations. You'll have the opportunity to influence strategy, technology, capability and ways of working while building a function designed for long-term success. For the right person, this is an opportunity to achieve in a few years what may take considerably longer within a more mature organisation. You'll help define the future state, build capability, develop talent and leave a lasting impact on an organisation that influences thousands of people every day. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of  Sunday 27th September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Faith Pallant, Recruitment Manager on f.pallant@auckland.ac.nz with any questions.   Reasonable accommodations  - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people  - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Marketing and Recruitment"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146573719": {
      "title": "Executive Communications Adviser",
      "description": "With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, we're boosting our strategic communications capabilities to help shape and enhance the reputation of the University in the eyes of its most important audiences.    Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our  Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement  functions.  Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity   We're looking for an exceptional writer and communicator to join the University of Auckland's Communications team as Executive Communications Adviser. This is a unique opportunity to work at the heart of the University's leadership environment, partnering closely with the Vice-Chancellor and other senior leaders to deliver high-quality communications that support strategic priorities, elevate reputation, and engage audiences across Aotearoa and beyond.  This is a position perfect for an outstanding writer who thrives in complex, high-profile environments and is skilled at crafting messages that make an impact.  Key areas of focus:   Development and delivery of executive communications for the Vice-Chancellor and senior leadership team.   Drafting speeches, messages, briefings, statements, talking points, presentations, and other high-profile communications.   Working directly with senior leaders to shape messaging, tone, and communication approaches.   Supporting executive communications during issues, incidents, and crisis situations.   Helping ensure executive messaging is aligned with the University's strategic priorities, institutional narrative, and reputation objectives.   Building trusted relationships with senior leaders and subject matter experts across the University.   Managing a dynamic workload that balances planned communications with emerging priorities and time-sensitive requests.  This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $87,000 - $118,000 per annum    We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $93,201 – $107,500 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our  commitment to pay equity .   For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the  position description   kōrero mōu | About you   You'll be a highly capable communications professional with the confidence, judgement, and credibility to work alongside senior leaders in a complex environment. You'll be an exceptional writer who can quickly understand context, distil complexity, and develop compelling communications that resonate with diverse audiences.  We're particularly interested in people who bring:   Proven experience delivering executive, corporate, strategic, or senior leadership communications within a large and complex organisation.   Exceptional writing, editing, and storytelling skills, with the ability to adapt style, tone, and messaging for different stakeholders, audiences and situations.   Demonstrated experience drafting speeches, leadership messages and other executive communications.   Strong judgement and political awareness, with the ability to navigate sensitive, confidential, and high-risk issues with professionalism and discretion.   Cultural competency and experience in shaping communications for diverse audiences, with a strong understanding of Te Ao Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi.  Strong stakeholder management skills, including the ability to build trusted relationships with senior leaders, subject matter experts, and key partners.     Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer    Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world.    Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University.   We also offer:   A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns   A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier   Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme,    Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces   A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance.    The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff   The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations      Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply   Please submit an application online by the closing date of 1 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience.     Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications.   Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements.      \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146565767": {
      "title": "Internal Communications Lead",
      "description": "With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, we've created the role of Internal Communications Lead to help transform the way we engage with our staff, building connection and belonging.  Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our  Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement  functions.  Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity   We're looking for an experienced and inspiring Internal Communications Lead to shape how we connect, engage and communicate with our staff and students across New Zealand's leading University. This is an opportunity to make a genuine impact on organisational culture, engagement and employee experience by helping our people understand where we're heading, why it matters, and the role they play in achieving our aspirations.  Reporting to the Director of Communications, you'll lead a team of internal Communications professionals, providing strategic direction and fresh approaches to how we communicate, engage and connect with our people.  This role offers exceptional breadth and visibility across one of New Zealand's largest and most complex organisations. You'll partner with the Executive Leadership, Deans, Directors, senior leaders and other stakeholders across faculties and service divisions to shape communication outcomes that impact thousands of staff and students.  Key areas of focus:   Develop and deliver enterprise-wide internal communications strategies and plans, supporting organisational culture, engagement and employee experience  Keep us ahead of the game by identifying and developing the channels and media that maximise reach and effectiveness   Help us understand and segment our audiences, develop measurement frameworks and insights to evaluate communication effectiveness and engagement  Provide trusted advice to senior leaders and support the continuing professional development of your team    This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $114,400 - $164,800 per annum. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $122,501 – $150,000 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our  commitment to pay equity .   For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the  position description   He kōrero mōu | About you   This is a role for someone who understands that great internal communication is about more than sharing information, it's about building trust, creating connection, strengthening culture and helping people feel proud of where they work.  We're particularly interested in people who bring:   Significant experience in internal communications within a large and complex organisation   Experience developing and implementing internal communications strategies for diverse audiences  Strong stakeholder engagement, relationship-building skills and the credibility to influence at a senior level  Proven cultural competency, and understanding of Te Ao Māori principles  A modernising, audience-centred approach  A passion for employee engagement, culture and people experience   Proven experience leading and developing teams   A finger on the pulse of digital technology advances and trends in internal communications  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer   Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world.    Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University.   We also offer:   A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns   A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier   Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme,    Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces   A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance.    The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff   The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply   Please submit an application online by the closing date of 1 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience.   Please reach out to Verity Collins  verity.collins@auckland.ac.nz  with any questions.   Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications.   Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements.      \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146403299": {
      "title": "Kaiārahi ",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an experienced and passionate Kaiārahi to join the Auckland Bioengineering Institute (ABI) in a newly created role that will help shape the future of Māori participation and success in bioengineering research. This role has a strong future focus. You will lead initiatives that create a sustainable pipeline of Māori students into postgraduate study, summer internships, and ultimately research careers. Through meaningful engagement with communities, students, and key University partners, you'll help shape pathways that support Māori success while strengthening ABI's connection to Māori communities and aspirations. Working across the University and within the community, you will collaborate with academic and professional staff to build awareness of ABI's world-leading research, identify opportunities for engagement, and encourage Māori learners to explore pathways into research and postgraduate study. You will also work closely with key networks including the Office of the Pro Vice-Chancellor Māori , MAPAS , Tuākana , SPIES , and other Māori support and engagement groups across the University. Key areas of focus include: Building a sustainable pipeline of Māori students into postgraduate study, internships, and research careers. Creating pathways that connect Māori learners with ABI's research opportunities and programmes. Supporting culturally responsive engagement and Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments across ABI. This is a part-time (18.75 hours per week, 0.5 FTE), fixed-term position for 36 months , based at our City Campus, 70 Symonds Street, Auckland. This is a band H role with a salary range from $98,700 - $121,900 per annum, pro-rated based on part-time hours, depending on skills, experience, and qualifications. For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review this position description. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Auckland Bioengineering Institute (ABI) is a large-scale research institute of Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. ABI excels in developing innovative bioengineering approaches to solve complex problems and translating technologies into meaningful health, social, and economic impact. The Institute brings together engineers, scientists, clinicians, and translational experts in a highly collaborative and collegial environment He kōrero mōu | About you You are a trusted relationship builder who is passionate about creating opportunities for Māori and supporting long-term educational and research outcomes. You thrive on bringing people together, building meaningful partnerships, and creating pathways that make a lasting difference. You will bring: Experience building trusted relationships with Māori communities, iwi, hapū, kura, schools, or Māori organisations. Strong understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and tikanga Māori. Experience in tertiary education, student engagement, recruitment, community engagement, research, or a related environment. Proven ability to collaborate and co-design initiatives with diverse stakeholders. Excellent communication (written and verbal), influencing, and relationship management skills. The ability to work independently while maintaining strong connections across teams, communities, and networks. You will be approachable, open-minded, culturally competent, and motivated by the opportunity to help shape a role that contributes to Māori success in higher education and research. \n\n Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland - is New Zealand's leading university and offers an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Five weeks' annual leave Career development and professional learning opportunities Generous parental leave provisions Access to a range of staff benefits, wellbeing initiatives, and discounts The opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to Māori student success and research engagement within a world-class institution. For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 9th of September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter outlining your interest in the role and relevant experience and your current curriculum vitae (CV) For any questions related to the role, please reach out to the Recruitment Team on recruitment.team@auckland.ac.nz  Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland is committed to upholding Te Tiriti o Waitangi and fostering an inclusive environment where Māori can thrive. We welcome applications from candidates who bring strong relationships, lived experience, and a genuine commitment to supporting Māori success in education, research, and community engagement. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Auckland Bioengineering Institute (ABI) | Waipapa Taumata Rau, University of Auckland Shape the future of Māori participation and success in bioengineering research Build pathways for Māori students into postgraduate study, summer internships, and research careers Join a world-leading research institute in a newly created 0.5 FTE, fixed-term role The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.",
      "department": "Bioengineering Institute"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146390609": {
      "title": "Dean, Faculty of Arts & Education | Manukura Pūtahi Mātauranga",
      "description": "Contribute to the strategic leadership of Aotearoa New Zealand’s top-ranked, proudly comprehensive and research-intensive university as a member of the executive team. Lead and enable the future for our exceptional and diverse scholars, staff and students. The University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau is seeking an inspirational leader with outstanding interpersonal skills and a commitment to positively contributing to society through world-class research and education to become our Dean, Faculty of Arts and Education Manukura Pūtahi Mātauranga. Candidates who are not already familiar with New Zealand’s bicultural context will be expected to engage with Māori culture and systems of knowledge, and to develop a sound understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and New Zealand’s place in the Pacific. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The Dean will demonstrate the insight, experience and commitment to provide continued leadership to equity, diversity and inclusion. A key member of the University Executive, reporting to the Vice-Chancellor, the Dean will balance the role of being the Faculty’s leader and greatest advocate internally and externally, while contributing to the success of the whole University, collaborating constructively with Deans, Directors and other university leaders. The Dean will provide visionary leadership to inspire, relate and ensure relevance of the Faculty’s mission. The Dean’s academic eminence will be combined with the belief that people are the Faculty’s greatest asset. The ideal candidate will bring the leadership experience to foster a culture of collegiality, collaboration, trust and excellence in both research and teaching, ensuring all staff are encouraged and enabled to achieve at the highest level. Ambitious for people and their endeavours in the Faculty and beyond, the Dean will apply strategic, management and financial acumen to create an environment of excellence, innovation and inclusion where our 530 staff and more than 7,000 students are empowered and challenged to flourish, thus ensuring the Faculty’s lasting success. The Dean will partner with others within and beyond the Faculty to explore and enhance opportunities that strengthen the Faculty’s future impact and sustainability, including growing the philanthropic base while deepening relationships with an existing portfolio of major donors, trusts and corporates. An accomplished relationship builder, the Dean will have expertise in developing meaningful engagement with government, industry, global and local arts and education focused communities, international institutional and educational partners and wider communities, building a pipeline of opportunities for future generations.   The University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and one of the Asia-Pacific region's most internationally connected institutions. We are ambitious in our scholarship, confident in our public role, and deeply committed to helping shape the future of Aotearoa New Zealand. As a comprehensive research-intensive university, we seek to combine global excellence with a deep commitment to the society we serve. Applications must be submitted by 11 October 2026 (further detail in the Candidate booklet ). For a confidential discussion, please contact the University of Auckland as below:  Clare Litten, email: c.litten@auckland.ac.nz Kathrin Soehnel, email: k.soehnel@auckland.ac.nz Position Reference: REF47374B \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Arts and Education"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146374207": {
      "title": "Media & Communications Partners",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  This is a rare opportunity to help build and shape a newly established Communications function at New Zealand's largest and most comprehensive university. Working alongside senior academic and professional leaders, you'll partner with some of the country's most influential educators, researchers and innovators to tell stories that inform, inspire and create impact well beyond our campuses. From breakthrough medical research and scientific discovery to innovation, entrepreneurship and education, you'll help tell stories that have the power to inform public debate, influence policy and improve lives.  Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our  Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement  functions. Embedded across the university and working closely with faculty Deans and research centre Directors, you’ll translate organisational priorities into proactive communication plans, engaging audiences, enhancing reputation and supporting strategic objectives.   You’ll have a strong risk radar and be able to advise senior leaders, academics and researchers how to navigate sensitive issues and build trust with audiences and stakeholders.   There’s no shortage of good stories to tell and you’ll be supported by a central hub of communications expertise spanning external communications, strategic communications, internal communications, digital storytelling and publishing to help get messages out across a range of channels.   Our available portfolios span some of the University's most strategically important and intellectually exciting areas, including our world-leading Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, Faculty of Science, Large Scale Research Institutes (The Liggins Institute and Auckland Biomedical Engineering Institute), Research & Innovation, and Innovation & Entrepreneurship.   These are portfolios rich with stories of breakthrough research, societal impact, educational excellence and global collaboration.  In these roles, you’ll be:  Building trusted relationships with senior leaders, researchers and stakeholders across your portfolio  Providing strategic communications advice and identifying opportunities to enhance reputation and visibility  Developing strategies and plans to strengthen employee engagement, recognition and belonging  Identifying, developing and delivering proactive media opportunities, managing media enquiries, and training and supporting media spokespeople  Translating complex topics into engaging and compelling content for internal and external audiences  Identifying and managing emerging issues, reputational risks and opportunities  Supporting continuous improvement through planning, evaluation and stakeholder feedback    This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is 98,700 - $133,500 per annum (Band H).  We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $105,701 – $121,900 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity .    He kōrero mōu | About you  We're looking for experienced communications professionals who can connect people, priorities and stories — helping ensure the University's communications activity delivers meaningful impact for our students, staff, researchers, partners and communities.  You may come from a communications, media relations, journalism, public relations, corporate affairs or strategic communications background. What matters most is your ability to build trusted relationships, identify opportunities and translate complex information into compelling communications.  We're particularly interested in people who bring:  Experience providing strategic communications advice at a senior level within large, complex organisations  Strong media relations, external communications or public affairs experience  Exceptional writing, storytelling and digital content development capability  Demonstrated stakeholder management skills with strong judgement and the credibility to influence decision making  Curiosity, adaptability and comfort working within evolving, ambiguous environments  A collaborative and service-oriented mindset  Cultural competency, knowledge and understanding of te ao Māori and experience communicating with diverse audiences   Experience in STEM, research, medical and health communications environments an advantage  Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences and see the position description for each of the portfolios for more detailed information on each of the roles:  Media and Communications Partner – Large Scale Research Institutes  Supporting two world-leading research centres, you’ll be communicating about the real-life impact of groundbreaking research improving health outcomes for communities around the world.  Auckland Bioengineering Institute (ABI) aims to improve medical diagnosis and treatment of injury and disease through the application of engineering sciences and technical innovation to medicine and human physiology.  See the  position description The Liggins Institute studies how early life events, nutrition, and environment, from before conception through childhood, shape lifelong health, aiming to give every child a healthy start.  Media and Communications Partner – Faculty of Science  The largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand, brings together more than 7,000 students across 10 diverse schools and departments. From climate change and sustainability to biodiversity, artificial intelligence and advanced technologies, Faculty of Science students, academic and researchers are tackling some of the world's most significant challenges.   See the  position description Media and Communications Partner – Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences A large and diverse faculty with more than 4,000 students studying across six schools, FMHS is committed to improving the health of local, national and global communities through excellence in teaching, research, service and engagement. We’re developing the next generation of health care practitioners and researchers to answer the big questions around human health and wellbeing.  See the  position description Media and Communications Partner – Research & Innovation  This portfolio sits at the heart of the University's research strategy, supporting world-class researchers, flagship research initiatives and innovation programmes that create impact beyond academia. The biggest advances rarely come from one field, one discipline, or one way of seeing the world. They happen when different areas of expertise come together. We bring together world-class research with iwi, communities, industry, government and global partners, to turn our greatest strengths into the world’s next breakthroughs.  See the  position description Media and Communications Partner – Innovation & Entrepreneurship  The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is a community that provides opportunities for students and staff across all faculties to develop entrepreneurial mindsets and innovative capability. By developing our people for innovative careers or start-up creation, we’re also building a future of prosperity for Aotearoa New Zealand and creating a world of opportunity, so far igniting 293 ventures, creating 4,427 jobs and raising $8.2 billion in capital.   See  position description Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer  As a comprehensive university and Aotearoa’s largest research organisation, our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University.  We also offer:  A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns  A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier  Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme,   Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces  A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance.   The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff  The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations    Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Please submit an application online by the closing date of 25 September 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience.  Please reach out to Verity Collins verity.collins@auckland.ac.nz  with any questions.    Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications.  Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146358409": {
      "title": "Senior Research Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Taumata Teitei, the University’s Vision 2030 and Strategic Plan, focuses on four impact areas; sustainability; health and wellbeing; advancing just, cultured, and engaged communities; and ethical innovation and technology. Research centres are a critical enabler of these strategic priorities and initiatives, bringing together stakeholders working towards common research goals. Following a review in 2022, the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences renewed its commitment to Six Faculty Research Centres. The Eisdell Moore Centre (EMC) for hearing and balance research is one of the six Faculty Research Centres. The EMC is dedicated to reducing the impact of hearing and balance disabilities in New Zealand and the Pacific Region. EMC also houses the WHO Collaborating Centre for Indigenous and Pacific Ear and Hearing Care, engaging and maintaining relationships with communities in Aotearoa and the Pacific Islands. Find out more by visiting our website https://www.emcentre.ac.nz/ We are seeking a dynamic and highly organised Senior Research Operations Coordinator to provide administrative and operational support to the Directors and Research Operations Manager. This role is crucial in supporting key initiatives, including outreach, communications, events, funding rounds, and digital presence, helping to strengthen connections, visibility, and impact. As the Senior Research Operations Coordinator, you will collaborate closely with a diverse team of academics, professional staff, external stakeholders, and the public. Your strong communication and relationship management skills will be essential. You will be ready to hit the ground running with excellent organisational abilities, capable of efficiently juggling multiple tasks. This is a part time (22.5 hours per week), fixed term role ending 31 December 2029. Remuneration:  $73,400 - $83,000 dependent on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the  Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You will have experience navigating policies and procedures in a large, complex organisation, with an understanding of research management and the research funding environment. You will be skilled in project, people and time management, with the ability to balance multiple priorities effectively and coordinate multiple tasks simultaneously. You will have strong written and oral communication skills, with experience coordinating research-related events such as symposiums, workshops and seminars, developing communication materials and engaging with diverse stakeholders. You will be confident working both independently and collaboratively, identifying opportunities and developing innovative ways to overcome challenges. It is preferable that you have experience with website management, including updating and creating content. You will be proficient in coordinating communication across various platforms, including social media, email, newsletters and membership lists. You will be capable of engaging stakeholders at all levels, including senior management, academia, government providers and community stakeholders. You will be proficient in Microsoft Office, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Teams for managing administrative tasks. Ideally you will have some experience engaging with Māori and Pacific communities Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and several other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 4 September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to  Jacqui Grey  via  jacqui.grey@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email.     #LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences is a diverse faculty with more than 10,000 students studying across six schools, led by world-leading researchers.  We are committed to improving the health of our local, national and global communities through excellence in teaching, research, service and engagement.",
      "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146109609": {
      "title": "Innovation Programmes Lead ",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  This is an exciting time to join innovation and entrepreneurship at Waipapa Taumata Rau.  As Innovation Programmes Lead, you will lead CIE’s portfolio of programmes that develop innovation and entrepreneurial capability in students, researchers and staff. You will shape how people from across disciplines develop the mindsets, skills and practical capabilities to recognise opportunities, navigate uncertainty, experiment and create meaningful change.  This is a people and portfolio leadership role. You will lead a multidisciplinary team, including CIE’s Technology & Prototyping function, and bring coherence and quality across a diverse portfolio of experiential programmes, professional development and hands-on experimentation.  You will build on strong existing programmes while making informed choices about what should be strengthened, evolved, scaled, developed or retired to deliver CIE’s strategy. The focus is developing people and innovation capability at scale; CIE’s dedicated Venture & Founder Development team leads the pathway for those who choose to progress ideas into venture creation.  As a member of the CIE Leadership Team, you will also share responsibility for CIE’s overall strategy, performance, culture and organisational development.  You will:  Lead the strategic development, delivery and continuous improvement of CIE’s innovation and entrepreneurship programme portfolio.   Lead and develop a multidisciplinary team and create the conditions for high-quality, disciplined delivery.   Ensure programmes provide high-quality experiential learning for diverse audiences across the University.   Build strong relationships with academics, practitioners and University partners, drawing on relevant expertise and leading practice.   Use evidence, participant insight and evaluation to inform portfolio decisions and continuously improve quality and impact.   Strengthen connections across programmes and with other opportunities across CIE and the wider University.   This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent role. This is a salary band I role with a salary range from $114,400 pa up to $140,000 pa depending on skills and experience. The nature of CIE’s programmes and events means the role requires flexibility to work outside normal business hours, including evening work as required and occasional weekend work.  For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description .  Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team  The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is Waipapa Taumata Rau’s platform for developing the innovators, entrepreneurs and changemakers of the future. We work across the University to help students, researchers and staff build innovation and entrepreneurial capability, turn ideas into action, develop ventures, and create social, cultural, environmental and economic impact for Aotearoa and the world.  CIE is entering an exciting new chapter. With an ambitious new strategy, we are significantly increasing the reach and impact of innovation and entrepreneurship across the University, helping realise Waipapa Taumata Rau’s ambition to be a global powerhouse of innovation, creativity and entrepreneurship, and strengthening the University’s contribution to a more innovative and prosperous Aotearoa.  We are building a team of people who are curious, collaborative, ambitious for impact and committed to turning ambition into action. If you want to help shape how a world-class research-led university develops innovative and entrepreneurial people, we would love to hear from you.  He kōrero mōu | About you  You are an experienced people and programme leader who combines strategic thinking with exceptional planning, organisation and execution. You thrive in a fast-paced and varied environment and can lead a busy portfolio serving diverse audiences, while establishing the clarity, priorities and operating rhythm that enable your team to perform at its best.  You think ahead, anticipate dependencies and pressure points, and know how to translate strategy into a coherent portfolio of high-quality programmes and experiences. You remain close enough to delivery to understand what is working, where quality can be strengthened and when intervention is needed, while creating space for your team to lead and succeed.  You are an influential relationship builder who can work credibly with academics, University leaders, practitioners and external partners. You understand different perspectives and needs, can influence across organisational boundaries, and know how to turn opportunities and relationships into practical outcomes.  You are also a collaborative organisational leader. You bring your expertise and perspective to collective decisions, value different viewpoints and take shared responsibility for the success of CIE as a whole.  You will bring:  Significant experience leading innovation, entrepreneurship, learning, professional development or capability-building programmes.   Demonstrated success leading and developing people and building high-performing, collaborative teams.   Exceptional forward planning and portfolio-management capability, with experience leading multiple programmes or workstreams in complex, fast-moving environments.   Strong innovation and entrepreneurship capability and an understanding of experiential approaches to learning and capability development.   Outstanding stakeholder engagement and influencing skills, with the credibility to work effectively with academics, University leaders, practitioners and external partners.   Strategic judgement and the ability to translate organisational priorities into high-quality execution, using evidence and insight to inform decisions and continuous improvement.   The confidence to lead a specialist Technology & Prototyping function, including appropriate oversight of physical infrastructure, health and safety and operational risk, while drawing on the technical expertise within the team.   Experience managing budgets, resources and operational planning.   A relevant tertiary qualification in innovation, entrepreneurship, business, education, management or a related field.   Experience in a tertiary education, research, innovation or entrepreneurship environment would be advantageous. An understanding of education within a research-led university context would be particularly valuable, including an appreciation of educational quality, research and scholarship, and the different contexts and motivations of students, researchers and academic staff  \n\n What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through:  Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours)  Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme  A competitive salary ($114,400 pa up to $140,000 pa) reflecting experience with five weeks’ annual leave  In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.   For more information, please visit Staff Benefits     \n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of  Sunday 6th September 2026  to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter highlighting how you meet the skills and experience outlined above, what attracts you to this opportunity, what you would bring to CIE and its leadership team, and how your experience has prepared you to lead a complex and diverse programme portfolio within a University context.  Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s pre-eminent University, with a turnover of $1.6bn, including research revenue of over $300m generated across eight diverse faculties and two research institutes. With over 9,000 staff and 50,000 students, the University of Auckland is one of New Zealand’s most significant and complex entities.",
      "department": "Research and Innovation"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146109099": {
      "title": "Venture and Founder Development Lead",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  This is a unique leadership opportunity to shape the University’s founder and venture development pathway and build the team that will deliver it.  Working at the intersection of innovation, entrepreneurship and commercialisation, you’ll lead a busy year-round portfolio that helps students, researchers and staff explore opportunities, validate ideas, develop as founders and progress early-stage ventures towards appropriate next-stage support.  You will:  Lead the strategic development, delivery and continuous improvement of CIE’s founder and venture development portfolio.   Shape a coherent pathway from early idea and exploration through validation, venture development and connection to appropriate next-stage support.   Build and lead a new Venture & Founder Development team, creating clear priorities, strong ways of working and consistently high-quality delivery.   Lead a diverse portfolio of founder and venture initiatives, ensuring programmes, cohorts, mentors, practitioners and external dependencies are well planned and coordinated.   Build trusted relationships with founders, researchers, academics, mentors, investors, industry, commercialisation colleagues and ecosystem partners.   Strengthen connections between CIE’s venture initiatives and wider opportunities for research translation, commercialisation, investment and venture support.   Use evidence, founder insights and emerging venture practices to inform portfolio decisions and continuously improve quality and founder outcomes.   Contribute as a member of CIE’s Leadership Team to the Centre’s strategy, performance, culture and organisational development.   This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent role.  This is a salary band I role, with a salary range from $114,400 pa to $140,000 depending on skills and experience. The nature of CIE’s programmes and events means the role requires flexibility to work outside normal business hours, including evening work as required and occasional weekend work.  For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description .    Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team  The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is Waipapa Taumata Rau’s platform for developing the innovators, entrepreneurs and changemakers of the future. We work across the University to help students, researchers and staff build innovation and entrepreneurial capability, turn ideas into action, develop ventures, and create social, cultural, environmental and economic impact for Aotearoa and the world.   CIE is entering an exciting new chapter. With an ambitious new strategy, we are significantly increasing the reach and impact of innovation and entrepreneurship across the University, helping realise Waipapa Taumata Rau’s ambition to be a global powerhouse of innovation, creativity and entrepreneurship, and strengthening the University’s contribution to a more innovative and prosperous Aotearoa.   We are building a team of people who are curious, collaborative, ambitious for impact and committed to turning ambition into action. If you want to help shape how a world-class research-led university develops innovative and entrepreneurial people, we would love to hear from you.   He kōrero mōu | About you  You are an experienced people and portfolio leader who combines strong venture and founder-development expertise with exceptional organisation, forward planning and execution. You understand what founders and early-stage ventures need as they move from an initial idea through exploration, validation and venture development, and you know how to turn that understanding into high-quality programmes and pathways.  You thrive in a fast-paced and varied environment and can lead a complex portfolio while building a new team and establishing the clarity, priorities and ways of working that enable people to perform at their best. You think ahead, anticipate dependencies and pressure points, and remain close enough to delivery to understand what is working, where quality can be strengthened and when intervention is needed.  You bring strong commercial judgement and can work credibly across different venture contexts, including student-led and research-derived ventures. You understand research translation and commercialisation and can work effectively with founders, researchers, academics, commercialisation professionals, investors, industry and the wider entrepreneurial ecosystem.  You are also a collaborative organisational leader. As a member of CIE’s Leadership Team, you will bring your expertise and perspective to collective decisions, work effectively across portfolio boundaries and take shared responsibility for CIE’s strategy, performance, culture and overall success.  You will bring:  Significant experience in venture and founder development, entrepreneurship, innovation, commercialisation or a related field.   Demonstrated success leading and developing people and building high-performing, collaborative teams.   Exceptional forward planning and portfolio-management capability, with experience leading multiple programmes or initiatives in complex, fast-moving environments.   Strong understanding of founder journeys and early-stage venture development, including opportunity exploration, validation and progression towards appropriate next-stage support.   Strong commercial judgement and an understanding of research translation and commercialisation.   Outstanding stakeholder engagement and influencing skills, with the credibility to work effectively with founders, researchers, academics, commercialisation professionals, investors, industry and ecosystem partners.   Experience coaching, mentoring or supporting founders as they navigate uncertainty, test assumptions and develop opportunities.   Strategic judgement and the ability to translate organisational priorities into coherent pathways, high-quality delivery and measurable outcomes.   Experience managing budgets, resources and operational planning.   A relevant tertiary qualification in business, innovation, entrepreneurship, science, engineering or a related discipline.   Experience in a tertiary education, research, innovation or entrepreneurship environment would be advantageous. An understanding of a research-led university environment, including the different contexts and motivations of students, researchers and academic staff, would be particularly valuable. \n\n What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through:  Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours)  Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme  A competitive salary ($114,400 pa to $140,000 pa ) reflecting experience with five weeks’ annual leave  In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.   For more information, please visit Staff Benefits     \n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 6th September, 2026 to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter highlighting how you meet the skills and experience outlined above, what attracts you to this opportunity, what you would bring to CIE and its leadership team, and how your experience has prepared you to lead a complex and diverse founder and venture development portfolio within a University context.    Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s pre-eminent University, with a turnover of $1.6bn, including research revenue of over $300m generated across eight diverse faculties and two research institutes. With over 9,000 staff and 50,000 students, the University of Auckland is one of New Zealand’s most significant and complex entities.",
      "department": "Research and Innovation"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000146081824": {
      "title": "Turner/Fitter/Welder",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for a Teaching Technician to support our Engineering Design programmes. We want our students to practically apply their theoretical knowledge; learn the value of prototyping, reviewing and iterating; nurture their curiosity; and to build the skills that will make them effective Engineers. Our Fabricating and Prototyping Workshop is one place where students can get this hands-on experience. The Teaching Technicians are responsible for running this workshop, which includes: overseeing all practical learning, teaching, and research activity conducted in the workshop building a safe and welcoming environment for students of all mechanical skill levels helping students learn safe equipment use as they develop their practical skills working with academic colleagues to plan out practical learning and teaching activities working with colleagues to plan further workshop development and expansion mentor and teach alongside our team of Graduate Teaching Assistants (postgraduate students hired to support teaching in the workshop) making sure the labs themselves are well stocked, the equipment is maintained, and the highest levels of safety are upheld You’ll have the opportunity to help shape the tools, projects, and culture in the space that will be used by over 1000 students per year. The successful candidates will become part of a larger team of Teaching Technicians responsible for supporting student learning across the Faculty’s Multidisciplinary Learning Spaces (MDLSs), labs, and workshops. The salary range is $63,600 – $79,300 per annum, depending on experience and qualifications. This is a full-time, 37.5-hour-per-week role on a fixed-term contract for 6 months. For more detailed information about the responsibilities and requirements of the role, please review the Position Description.   He kōrero mōu | About you Our ideal candidates will: have a welcoming attitude towards novice machinists understand the importance of empowering students to learn and create through experience and exploration have a trade qualification or significant relevant experience in a technical engineering role (in an industry setting) have demonstrated safe and effective mentorship of novice technicians in mills, lathes, MIG welding, and/or workshop tools have experience implementing and managing Health & Safety protocols and practices relevant to engineering workshops have very good verbal communication skills and the ability to communicate well with people from a range of backgrounds, experience levels and cultures.   Mō tō mātou rōpū | About us Our faculty has a strong and innovative vision for the future, delivering learning and research excellence to enable our students to thrive and succeed and become graduates who make a positive impact on their community and world around them. The faculty also enjoys a, purpose built Engineering building, housing state of the art labs, workshops and facilities. The Faculty of Engineering and Design is large and multi-faceted, and maintains a strong culture of excellence in research and student-centric learning. The faculty is also committed to Mātauranga Māori - the unique Māori way of viewing the world – encompassing traditional knowledge and culture – and actively seeking meaningful ways to reflect this in our teaching, research, and how we work together.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave We also offer a 6.75% superannuation scheme, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, discounted onsite childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information please visit our Staff Benefits page.   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 6/9/2026 and include an up to date CV outlining your experience. If you have any questions about this role, please get in touch with Ashveen Nand, Technical Manager, for a confidential discussion on ashveen.nand@auckland.ac.nz. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.",
      "department": "Engineering and Design"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145890109": {
      "title": "Digital and Generative AI Communications Lead",
      "description": "With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, the Digital & Generative AI Communications Lead will play a pivotal role in helping the University adopt safe, effective and audience-focused approaches to Generative AI, digital communications and emerging technologies. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Working closely with the communications and digital leadership teams, you’ll provide strategic leadership, advice, and coaching on the use of Generative AI, workflow optimisation, digital content technologies, and AI-augmented communications approaches. You'll act as a bridge between communications practice and digital innovation, providing practical tools, guidance, workflows and capability building. Your work will help the communications team reach wider audiences and get the most out of their story-telling capabilities. Find out more about our reimagining of how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement . This role offers a rare opportunity to shape the future of communications in an organisation that helps shape the future of Aotearoa. Key areas of focus include: Leading the development and implementation of digital and Generative AI communications capability across the Communications function Identifying opportunities to improve communications effectiveness and audience engagement through digital tools and AI-enabled workflows Developing guidance, standards, training and resources to support responsible adoption of Generative AI Keeping up with emerging trends in AI, digital communications, content discovery and audience behaviour Contributing to innovation, experimentation and continuous improvement across the Communications function. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $110,700 - $149,800 per annum (Band HP). We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $118,501 – $136,700 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you We're looking for someone who combines communications expertise with demonstrated technical capabilities in digital innovation and emerging technologies. You may come from a digital communications, public relations, or agency background, what matters most is your ability to translate emerging technologies into practical comms solutions that improve how teams work, communicate and engage with audiences. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Significant experience in digital communications, content operations, digital publishing or related communications environments Practical experience applying Generative AI or emerging technologies within communications, content or publishing environments The ability to translate complex digital or AI concepts into practical, audience-focused guidance Strong stakeholder engagement and influencing capability across complex organisations Curiosity, adaptability and a commitment to continuous improvement A collaborative leadership style and passion for helping others succeed. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As part of a newly established Communications function, you'll have the opportunity to influence how digital communications, content operations and Generative AI capability evolve across the University. You'll work alongside communications leaders, digital specialists, strategists and storytellers while helping build capability that benefits the entire organisation. Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of 27 September 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Verity Collins verity.collins@auckland.ac.nz with any questions.   Reasonable accommodations  - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people  - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements.   \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Communications and Engagement"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145887999": {
      "title": "Research Nurse",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Liggins Institute is seeking a Research Nurse to support the delivery of clinical research studies within its Gut Bugs research programme. This 9   month fixed-term position (until 30 June 2027) combines clinical nursing responsibilities with study coordination, providing support across participant recruitment, assessments, treatment delivery, data collection, and study administration. You will help ensure that clinical trials are conducted in accordance with approved protocols, ethical requirements, and Good Clinical Practice standards. The role involves regular interaction with research participants, healthcare providers, and internal stakeholders, and offers the opportunity to contribute to research focused on improving long-term health outcomes. This is a full-time opportunity suited to a registered nurse with strong organisational skills, an interest in clinical research, and the ability to work across both participant-facing and administrative aspects of study delivery. Key responsibilities: Coordinate participant recruitment, screening, enrolment and follow-up activities. Conduct clinical assessments and support treatment delivery within research studies. Act as a key point of contact for participants, families and healthcare providers. Ensure accurate data collection, documentation and quality assurance. Support biological sample collection and study-related procedures. Assist with clinical trial logistics, scheduling and study administration. Promote adherence to Good Clinical Practice, study protocols and ethical requirements. The salary range for these roles are $73,400 - $101,900 depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are a registered nurse who thrives in a collaborative environment and enjoys building trusted relationships with participants and colleagues alike. You bring exceptional organisational skills, attention to detail and a genuine interest in advancing healthcare through research. You'll be motivated by the opportunity to contribute to work that makes a real difference. Our ideal candidate will bring: Nursing or medical degree and at least 2 years’ experience Confidence performing clinical procedures, including phlebotomy. Strong organisational and time-management skills. Excellent verbal and written communication abilities. Ability to work independently while managing competing priorities. A commitment to high-quality participant care and research excellence Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Liggins Institute is a Large-Scale Research Institute at the University of Auckland. We are one of the world’s leading centres for research on fetal child and adolescent health and well-being, nutrition, development, epigenetics and translational and implementation science. Our vision is “a healthy start for a healthy life” and our mission is to improve lifelong health through excellent research. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of  11 September 2026  and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Steffi Freudlsperger via steffi.freudlsperger@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and health researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students.",
      "department": "Liggins Institute"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145676440": {
      "title": "Academic Services Manager",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  This position offers an excellent opportunity to provide leadership for ensuring effective faculty-based academic services to students and academic leaders across the faculty’s academic programmes (including doctoral and sub-doctoral research). It is responsible for managing faculty’s relationships with the central Student and Scholarly Services function and the School of Graduate Studies to ensure delivery of a high quality, seamless and holistic service and student experience for the University’s students.  Success in this role will require a balance of strategic thinking and strong leadership. You will lead a team of experienced professionals, navigate a complex environment and ensure faculty priorities are delivered through strong planning, collaboration and execution.  Key accountabilities include:  Leading and developing a high-performing team, building capability, accountability and a culture of continuous improvement  Assuming responsibility for the implementation of faculty and University strategic and annual plans as they relate to academic and student services in the faculty.  Ensuring accurate and appropriate advice is available to the Student and Scholarly Services function, particularly Student Hubs and central Academic Services relating to admissions, academic progress and programme completion matters relating to the faculty’s academic programmes and courses.  Building and maintaining strong stakeholder relationships with both central service divisions and faculty teams, to ensure effective collaboration and alignment.  Fostering strong working relationships with central admissions and enrolment teams to optimise admissions and enrolment outcomes, support faculty recruitment objectives, and enhance the overall student experience.  Playing a leading role in the planning and delivery of ongoing strategic projects and service improvements, including initiatives such as Undergraduate Health programme changes and Clinical Admissions process enhancements  This is a  fulltime (37.5 hours per week) permanent role .  The salary range is between  $98,700 - $121,900  based on experience and qualifications.   For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description.  Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team   This role works closely with the Faculty Student Support and Engagement, Marketing and Communication, and Student Hubs teams at Grafton Campus, and the Faculty academic leadership. This role is pivotal in ensuring effective faculty-based academic services are provided to students and academic leaders across the faculty’s academic programmes.  He kōrero mōu | About you | Our ideal candidate  You will be a strategic and people-focussed leader with a demonstrated ability to translate organisational objectives into operational outcomes. You will have successfully led teams through change, influenced senior stakeholders, and delivered improvements in complex environments. Our ideal candidate will bring:  Significant experience in a management role with proven experience in operational planning and implementation within a large, complex organisation  Demonstrated skills and experience in team building, change management and working collaboratively  Extensive knowledge of trends and best practice in customer service including service design, digital delivery and self-service  Demonstrable understanding of Te ao Māori and experience of developing cultural competency within a team.  Experience and understanding of academic and student services and other university related information systems.  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.   The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through:  Flexible employment practices  Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme  A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave  In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.   For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits .  Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of  6 September 2026  to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above.  Please reach out to Johanna Beattie via  j.beattie@auckland.ac.nz . Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email.  \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau   | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have eight academic faculties and 11 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences is a diverse faculty with more than 10,000 students studying across six schools, led by world-leading researchers, teachers, and practising health professionals.  Our Vision is to courageously lead and advance hauora across generations in Aotearoa.  As such, we are committed to lead and advance the mana of Aotearoa as a global centre of Health learning, research, and innovation.",
      "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145616309": {
      "title": "Admissions Adviser",
      "description": "The opportunity | Te Whiwhinga mahi  We are on the lookout for an Admission Adviser to join our team.  This is a pivotal role in the University’s admissions process.  You’ll be responsible for assessing applications ensuring they meet established criteria, processing associated documentation within agreed timeframes. While this is primarily a behind-the-scenes position, you will also provide accurate and timely advice to prospective studies, agents and internal stakeholders – mostly via email and online systems – about course and entry requirements. The role is full-time, 37.5 hours per week.  The salary range for this role is $63,600 - $68,800 per annum depending on experience and qualifications. For more information, please view the  Position Description.   About You | He kōrero mōu You will be comfortable working in a high-volume environment, all while not sacrificing the quality of the interactions with your customers and stakeholders. You genuinely enjoy working with people and know how to balance great customer experiences with sound decision-making that aligns with our policies and procedures. If you’re unsure if you tick all the boxes but have a fantastic attitude and can learn skills quickly, please submit an application – we’re interested in speaking with you to see if we could support and train you.   Important Skills | Nga Pūkenga Motuhake  Customer and stakeholder service: the driving force behind your decisions and activities. Interpersonal effectiveness: clear and constructive communication and behaviours influencing others and inspiring cooperation and achievement. Change and innovation: Initiate, facilitate, and support our work. Decision Making: Asking questions, gathering and analysing information, generating solutions, making decisions, and taking action is what we do.   About the team | Mō tō mātou rōpū  Our leaders are committed to ensuring you can grow and develop your skillset and career. We place great emphasis on ensuring that we foster a culture that, although busy, is supportive and enjoyable. Working with a wide variety of stakeholders across the University means that you’ll be able to grow your relationships and understanding.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information please visit  Staff Benefits .   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 9/09/2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Sau Kraay via  sau.kraay@auckland.ac.nz  for more information. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.",
      "department": "Student and Scholarly Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145614509": {
      "title": "Finance Business Partner",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity As Associate Finance Business Partner, you will support the Finance Lead and wider business with financial planning, reporting, and management to ensure accurate financial information and processes are maintained. This is a 12-month fixed-term role focused on supporting the effective transfer and finalisation of financial reporting responsibilities for UniServices business units that have transitioned to new entities. Key responsibilities include: Partnering with business units to provide financial insights and actionable recommendations. Assisting with budgeting, forecasting, and variance analysis. Preparing monthly management reports and commentary for decision-making. Maintaining accurate financial records and reconciling accounts. Supporting compliance with internal controls, financial policies, and audit requirements. Building strong relationships with University of Auckland shared services and finance teams. Identifying opportunities to streamline reporting and analysis processes. Contributing to dashboards and tools for improved financial visibility. For more information, please see Position Description This is a 12-Month Fixed-Term, full-time role (40 hours per week, Monday to Friday) with a salary range of $96,000-$104,000 per annum plus Incentives and benefits, depending on experience. He kōrero mōu | About You You are a proactive and detail-oriented finance professional who can translate data into meaningful insights and build strong relationships across teams. You thrive in a collaborative environment and have a strong foundation in financial analysis and reporting. What you’ll bring: A tertiary qualification in Finance, Commerce, Accounting, Economics, or Business. Working towards or completion of a professional accounting qualification (CA, CPA, or CIMA). 2–3 years’ experience in finance or accounting, ideally in budgeting, forecasting, and management reporting. Strong Excel skills, including modelling and data analysis. Experience with Workday Adaptive is an advantage. Commercial acumen and ability to interpret financial data effectively. Excellent attention to detail and ability to prioritise work to meet deadlines. Strong relationship-building and communication skills   What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou We offer a comprehensive range of benefits designed to support your wellbeing and professional growth. You’ll enjoy five weeks of annual leave and up to 6.75% employer contribution to our superannuation scheme. We provide eye-testing and glasses subsidies, along with access to discounts on health insurance and gym memberships. Long-service recognition is part of our culture, and we’re proud to offer a supportive, culturally diverse workplace with a blended approach to office and home-based work. Flexible working arrangements are available, and we’re committed to your ongoing professional development so you can thrive in your role and beyond. \n\n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  \nApplications should be submitted on our website, before Sunday 6th September , 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above.   Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email.  \n\n Uniservices | Waipapa Taumata Rau   | The University of Auckland UniServices is the University of Auckland’s research commercialisation and growth team. We sit at the intersection of research, innovation, and industry, with a mission to grow and commercialise the University’s vast footprint of research expertise.",
      "department": "UniServices"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145607599": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor in Economics",
      "description": "Ranked #67 in the QS World University Rankings 2027 Triple-crown accredited Business School (AACSB, EQUIS and AMBA) The Business School is a community of 6,885 EFT students across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, supported by the strength of 265 FTE academic staff and 105 FTE professional staff \n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. \n \nJoin New Zealand’s top-ranked and triple-crown accredited Auckland Business and Economics School, with an international reputation for shaping the future of research and teaching excellence, and be part of its continued growth as you embark on the next phase of your academic career. With strong growth in student numbers and sustained investment across the Faculty of Business and Economics, this is an exciting time to join as it continues to strengthen and expand its academic community. \n \n Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity As the Faculty continues to grow, the Department of Economics is seeking an ambitious, collegial, and research-active Lecturer, (i.e. tenure-track Assistant Professor), Senior Lecturer or Associate Professor specialising in applied econometrics, microeconometrics and/or macroeconometrics. The successful candidate will demonstrate strong research potential and a commitment to excellent, research-informed teaching, enhanced by appropriate learning technologies. You will contribute to an exceptional learning experience for undergraduate and postgraduate students, teaching in your specialist area and more widely at introductory levels, while developing your academic profile through research, publication and supervision of research students. You will be encouraged to build research partnerships, seek external funding and engage with professional, academic and non-academic communities. You will contribute to an intellectually vibrant academic environment through interdisciplinary collaboration, knowledge exchange and departmental service, with opportunities to work across and beyond the Department. All candidates will bring the potential to contribute service and citizenship within and beyond the University, as well as to actively participate in Department, Faculty and University committees and activities. The faculty attracts high-calibre students to our world-class undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. We take pride in the excellence demonstrated by our internationally recognised scholars, who lead a vibrant research ecosystem supported by interdisciplinary research centres and a global network of visiting experts who contribute to both teaching and research. This is a  full-time  (40 hours per week)  permanent  position (subject to continuation). Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and will be aligned with our  Academic Standards policy . It will be within the following ranges: Lecturer (L): NZ$99,788 - NZ$119,204 \nSenior Lecturer (SL): NZ$126,863 - NZ$158,904 \nAssociate Professor (AP): From NZ$165,808 For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description. \n \n He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a collegial academic with excellent communication skills and the ability to clearly articulate your ideas, research, and future plans. The successful appointee will have a PhD in Economics (or be near completion), with demonstrated excellence in research and a commitment to high quality research-informed teaching. You will have a research programme that complements and builds upon existing areas of strength within the Department. These areas include, but are not limited to: International Trade International Finance Open Economy Macroeconomics Financial Economics Artificial Intelligence and the Macroeconomy Fintech Energy Economics Public Economics Labour Economics Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes and mentoring support Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. \n \nFor more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of  Sunday 06 September 2026 (11.59pm NZT) .  \n \nPlease include a cover letter outlining your interest and ability to relocate if required, CV, statement of your teaching philosophy, teaching evaluations and a statement of the direction and ambition of your research programme, with a full publication, PG supervision and research grant record. \n \nPlease direct enquiries to Prasanna Gai, Head of Department, via p.gai@auckland.ac.nz. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but applications must be made online.  \n \n#LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Faculty of Business and Economics"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145476968": {
      "title": "Technician - Chemical Research Labs - Te Kura Mātai Taiao - School of Environment",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This role is responsible for providing technical support for the activities, equipment, and infrastructure of chemistry research laboratories, primarily within the School of Environment. The role ensures the efficient operation and day-to-day functionality of laboratory spaces, as well as equitable technical support to a wide range of academic staff and postgraduate students, primarily from the School of Environment, but also the wider Faculty and University. The appointed candidate will be providing technical support for analytical equipment such as an Elemental CNS analyser, MP-AES, TOC/N, UV/VIS Spectrophotometer, and Ion Chromatograph. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. Salary range is   $65,000 - $70,000, dependent on experience For more detailed information, please refer to the  Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you \nOur ideal candidate will have   a Bachelor’s Degree in Chemistry, or an equivalent tertiary qualification, and a minimum of 2-3 years’ experience working in general chemistry laboratories. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team \nThe successful candidate will work alongside a diverse team of technical staff who support the School of Environment in environmental chemistry, electron microprobe analysis, GIS, geography, earth science, lapidary preparation, sedimentology, hydrology and field operations be strongly engaged with the team supporting the School of Chemical Sciences. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer We offer an opportunity within New Zealand’s leading University that will have a positive impact on the lives of students. We also offer: Five weeks’ annual leave per annum Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Over 300 different career development courses and programme  Discounted parking and various leisure and recreation services A diverse and inclusive working environment For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . He kōrero mōu | About you \nOur ideal candidate will have   Bachelor’s Degree in Chemistry, or an equivalent tertiary qualification and a minimum 2-3 years’ experience working in general chemistry laboratories. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team \nThe successful candidate will work alongside a diverse team of technical staff who support the School of Environment in environmental chemistry, electron microprobe analysis, GIS, geography, earth science, lapidary preparation, sedimentology, hydrology and field operations be strongly engaged with the team supporting the School of Chemical Sciences. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer \nWe offer an opportunity at New Zealand’s leading University that will positively impact students' lives. We also offer: Five weeks’ annual leave per annum Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Over 300 different career development courses and programme  Discounted parking and various leisure and recreation services A diverse and inclusive working environment For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 13th September 2026  to be considered.  Please include your cover letter and CV, highlighting how you meet the skills and experience detailed above. If you have any questions about this role, please get in touch with David Jenkinson (Technical Services Manager) via  d.jenkinson@auckland.ac.nz  for a confidential discussion. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science  at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including  Tuākana ,  Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network  and our  Waipapa Marae .",
      "department": "Faculty of Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145426188": {
      "title": "3 x Health & Safety Adviser – Science and Engineering  – 12-month fixed term ",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for three Health & Safety Advisers to join our Science and Engineering faculties at the University of Auckland: One Health & Safety Adviser – Science One Health & Safety Adviser - Medical and Health Sciences One Health & Safety Adviser – Engineering These are broad, hands-on roles where you will work alongside academics, researchers, technicians, professional staff, and students to help create safe, compliant, and well-managed environments across complex teaching, research, laboratory, and engineering settings. This is much more than a compliance role. You will be a trusted adviser who understands the needs of the business, provides practical advice, challenges when required, and helps people find solutions that enable teaching and research to happen safely. Working closely with the relevant Health, Safety, and Wellbeing Manager and Faculty stakeholders, you will provide specialist advice and guidance across a broad range of health Nga Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Provide practical, health and safety advice and guidance across complex environments Support the identification and management of hazards and risks, including reviewing risk assessments, implementing appropriate controls, and supporting higher-risk laboratory and engineering activities. Lead and support incident response and investigations, corrective actions, and organisational learning, while identifying opportunities for continuous improvement. Undertake safety walks, inspections, and audits, while coordinating health and safety documentation, training, records, reporting, and other compliance requirements Build strong relationships with managers, academics, technicians, researchers, and students, providing training and guidance Using health and safety systems and data to identify emerging risks and contribute to practical improvements that strengthen safety across the Faculty. Supporting compliance with relevant legislation, standards, and University requirements. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), 12-month fixed-term position. The salary range is $87,000 to $107,500 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the  Position Description   He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for someone who combines sound technical health and safety knowledge with strong interpersonal skills and a practical, people-focused approach. You will bring: A relevant tertiary qualification and demonstrated experience in health and safety, preferably within a complex, technical or higher-risk environment. For the Science positions: a degree in Science, Environmental Science, Occupational Health & Safety or a related discipline. A Chemistry qualification or background would be advantageous . For the Engineering position: a degree in Engineering, Mechanical Engineering or a related discipline. Approximately 3–4 years’ relevant health and safety experience, ideally within a complex operational, laboratory, research, engineering, or other technical environment Strong knowledge of risk management, hazard identification, incident management and health and safety compliance. Experience supporting incident investigations, corrective actions and continuous improvement, as well as developing, reviewing and maintaining risk assessments, procedures and health and safety documentation. Experience undertaking audits, inspections, safety walks and other assurance activities, with strong attention to detail and the ability to manage multiple compliance and administrative requirements. Excellent communication, relationship-building and influencing skills, with the confidence to work with people at all levels, challenge constructively, provide appropriate pushback and build practical, solutions-focused relationships. The ability to work autonomously, exercise sound judgment, and take ownership, supported by strong organisational and administrative skills, Proficiency in Microsoft Excel and experience using large or complex health and safety management systems - Sidetrack and/or Ideagen (previously Damstra) would be advantageous. If you have a background in science, engineering, manufacturing or laboratory environments, and a passion for health, safety and wellbeing, we’d love to hear from you. \n  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and several other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 24th September 2026, and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. Please indicate your preferred position – Science, Engineering, or either – in your cover letter If you have any questions about this role, please get in touch with Sharon Kishore (Recruitment Consultant) via sharon.kishore@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential discussion. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n  Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.  Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability .",
      "department": "Human Resources"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145241069": {
      "title": "Senior Brand Activation Specialist",
      "description": "Auckland | Permanent | Full-time Hybrid working environment Marketing & Recruitment, Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland $87,000 - $118,000 per annum (Band G) depending on experience \n  Lead major brand activations, sponsorships and audience experiences for Aotearoa New Zealand's leading university Shape how one of Aotearoa's most recognised brands evolves and engages with students, communities and partners Enjoy hybrid work, a 37.5-hour week, and inclusive benefits designed to support wellbeing and caring commitments With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau, the University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to creating knowledge, transforming lives and shaping the future. As part of a newly established Marketing & Recruitment operating model, our Brand team is responsible for strengthening the University's reputation, growing brand awareness and creating meaningful engagement with audiences across Aotearoa and beyond.   Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is a highly visible role responsible for bringing the University brand to life through impactful activations, sponsorships and audience experiences. Working closely with the Brand Manager and a wider network of specialists across brand, digital, content, recruitment, communications and media production, you'll design and deliver activation programmes that strengthen brand awareness, increase audience engagement and support the University's strategic objectives. This role goes beyond event delivery – far beyond. We're looking for someone who can identify opportunities, develop creative activation concepts and turn ideas into experiences that resonate with audiences. From flagship student recruitment events and sponsorship activations to promotional campaigns and brand experiences, you'll play a key role in shaping how audiences connect with the University. In this newly established team and function, you'll work alongside an experienced Brand Manager, dedicated Māori and Pacific Brand Specialists, Brand Advisers and a network of creative, digital and content experts. Together, you'll help shape how one of Aotearoa’s most recognised brands engages with its audiences on a scale and breadth that few organisations can offer. This role offers a rare opportunity to combine strategic thinking, creativity and hands-on delivery while contributing to a brand with national and global reach. Key areas of focus include: Designing and delivering brand activations, sponsorship activations and promotional experiences Creating audience-centred activation concepts that build brand awareness and engagement Leading activation programmes from planning and promotion through to delivery, evaluation and reporting Managing sponsorship activity to maximise audience engagement and brand value Working with agencies, suppliers and partners to deliver high-quality experiences and outcomes Coordinating content capture, creative assets, merchandise and promotional materials Using audience insights and performance data to improve activation effectiveness Building strong relationships with stakeholders across the University and external partners. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $87,000 - $118,000 per annum – Band G. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $93,201 – $107,500 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description .   He kōrero mōu | About you We're looking for an experienced marketer who can combine strategic thinking, creativity and delivery excellence to create experiences that connect audiences with our brand. You may come from a brand activation, experiential marketing, sponsorship, events, audience engagement or broader marketing background. What matters most is your ability to turn ideas into action and create experiences that deliver measurable outcomes. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Experience delivering brand activations, experiential marketing, sponsorship programmes or large-scale promotional activity The ability to develop activation concepts that align audience needs with organisational objectives Experience managing multiple projects and campaigns from concept through to evaluation Strong stakeholder engagement and relationship management capability Experience working with agencies, suppliers and external partners Campaign management, paid and owned activity A strong understanding of brand, audience engagement and experiential marketing principles Experience managing budgets and measuring performance Excellent project management, communication and influencing skills Curiosity, creativity and a proactive approach to identifying new opportunities.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer This is a rare opportunity to help shape a new function while working with one of Aotearoa’s most recognised brands. This new function gives you access to internal expertise and resources commensurate with our scale and ambition, including an in-house design studio, media production teams, audience specialists, web and digital teams, AI and automation expertise, and dedicated Māori and Pacific specialists across brand, media, comms and engagement. Together, these teams help bring ideas to life through integrated campaigns, content and experiences. Few organisations have the breadth of audiences, partnerships, events and engagement opportunities that exist at the University. From flagship recruitment events and sponsorship activations to community engagement initiatives and reputation-building campaigns, you'll have the opportunity to deliver work that reaches audiences across Aotearoa and beyond. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa’s most diverse and complex organisations   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of  Sunday 6th September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Faith Pallant, Recruitment Manager on f.pallant@auckland.ac.nz with any questions.   Reasonable accommodations  - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people  - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Marketing and Recruitment"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145227741": {
      "title": "Marketing & Recruitment Business Partners",
      "description": "Auckland | Permanent | Full-time Hybrid working environment Marketing & Recruitment | Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland $98,700 - $133,500 (Band H) depending on experience \n  Join a newly established team redefining how marketing and recruitment supports the University Partner with senior leaders to drive strategy, prioritisation and growth Enjoy hybrid work, a 37.5-hour week, and inclusive benefits designed to support wellbeing and caring commitments With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau, the University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to creating knowledge, transforming lives and shaping the future. As part of a newly established Marketing & Recruitment operating model, our Business Partnering team sits at the centre of strategic decision-making, helping faculties, research institutes and service divisions connect organisational priorities with meaningful marketing and recruitment outcomes.   Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is an influential, strategic partnering role working closely with senior leaders, faculty leadership teams and key stakeholders to translate organisational priorities into integrated marketing and recruitment plans. Rather than delivering campaigns directly, you will help shape direction, set priorities, facilitate planning and ensure the University's marketing and recruitment investment delivers the greatest impact. You'll be a trusted adviser, connecting your portfolio to a central hub of marketing, brand, digital, content, recruitment and communications expertise. Through evidence-based planning, stakeholder engagement and strategic advisory work, you'll help drive sustainable student recruitment and experience, strengthen reputation, and support research, innovation and community impact. In this newly established team and function, we are recruiting multiple Marketing & Recruitment Business Partners to support portfolios across the University. These portfolios are: Arts & Education Business & Law Engineering and Design Medical Health Sciences, FMHS (appointed) Science Campus Experience Research and Innovation Education Large-Scale Research Institutes (LSRIs) Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions. This role offers a rare opportunity to influence outcomes at scale while remaining connected to the people and communities your work supports. Key areas of focus include: Developing and maintaining integrated annual marketing and recruitment plans for assigned portfolios Translating University and portfolio strategies into clear, prioritised objectives Partnering with Deans, senior leaders and professional services stakeholders to align priorities and outcomes Providing strategic marketing and recruitment advice informed by evidence, insight and best practice Managing demand, prioritisation and briefing processes into the Marketing & Recruitment Hub Identifying opportunities for collaboration and shared solutions across portfolios Supporting governance, planning and continuous improvement activities that enhance effectiveness across the University. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $98,700 - $133,500 - Band H. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $105,701 – $121,900, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description .   He kōrero mōu | About you We're looking for experienced strategic partners who can connect people, priorities and ideas — helping ensure our marketing and recruitment investment drives meaningful outcomes for students, staff, faculties, research institutes and communities. You may come from a marketing, communications, consulting, agency account management, business partnering background or another strategic advisory environment. What matters most is your ability to build relationships, influence stakeholders and connect organisational priorities to meaningful action. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Experience developing strategic plans, business plans or customer strategies, creating a clear line of sight for your stakeholders and priorities for specialist teams Demonstrable stakeholder/client engagement and relationship management capability to support maximum impact for your portfolio, high-quality delivery for internal teams, and a culture of collaboration Confidence working with senior leaders in complex environments, beyond pure advising to a valued partnership, while managing competing priorities and projects across your collaborating partners and the marketing hub A solid understanding of the tertiary marketing and/or student recruitment landscape to translate the needs of your portfolio into actionable outcomes Excellent facilitation, communication and influencing skills A collaborative and service-oriented mindset Curiosity, adaptability and comfort working within evolving environments.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer This is a rare opportunity to contribute to a new function, vision and culture while working within an organisation that operates at significant scale. This new function gives you access to internal expertise and resources commensurate to our complexity and commitments including an in-house design studio and media production teams, audience specialist teams, web and digital teams, AI and automation expertise, and Māori and Pacific specialists across brand, media, comms and engagement. As a comprehensive university, our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. Whether your interests lie in education, research, innovation, reputation building, community engagement or student growth, you'll have the chance to contribute across a wide range of portfolios, projects and strategic priorities while continuing to grow your own career. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa’s most diverse and complex organisations   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of Sunday 13th September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Faith Pallant, Recruitment Manager on f.pallant@auckland.ac.nz with any questions.    Reasonable accommodations  - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people  - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Marketing and Recruitment"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145194690": {
      "title": "Venture and Founder Development Specialist",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  This role is ideal for someone who enjoys working directly with founders and early-stage ventures. As Venture & Founder Development Specialist, you'll design and deliver founder programmes that help students, researchers and staff explore opportunities, test ideas and progress towards venture creation.  Working closely with the Venture & Founder Development Lead, this is a highly hands-on role focused on managing and delivering high-quality programmes, managing founder cohorts and creating practical learning experiences that help early-stage ventures progress.  Manage the design, delivery and continuous improvement of designated founder and venture development programmes.   Design and facilitate practical founder-development experiences that support customer discovery, experimentation, validation and early-stage venture development.   Manage founder cohorts and create engaging learning experiences that support venture progression.   Coordinate mentors, facilitators and ecosystem contributors to give founders access to relevant expertise and support.   Build strong relationships with researchers, students, founders, academics and external startup ecosystem partners.   Support researchers and research-derived opportunities to navigate early-stage venture development and connect with appropriate commercialisation pathways.   Use programme data, participant feedback and emerging venture practices to improve programme quality and outcomes.   Connect founders with appropriate next-stage pathways, including commercialisation, investment opportunities, accelerators and other venture support.  This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent role.  This is a band G role with a salary range from $87,000 pa to $107,500 depending on skills and experience. \n  \nFor more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description   \n   Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team  The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is Waipapa Taumata Rau’s platform for developing the innovators, entrepreneurs and changemakers of the future. We work across the University to help students, researchers and staff build innovation and entrepreneurial capability, turn ideas into action, develop ventures, and create social, cultural, environmental and economic impact for Aotearoa and the world.   CIE is entering an exciting new chapter. With an ambitious new strategy, we are significantly increasing the reach and impact of innovation and entrepreneurship across the University, helping realise Waipapa Taumata Rau’s ambition to be a global powerhouse of innovation, creativity and entrepreneurship, and strengthening the University’s contribution to a more innovative and prosperous Aotearoa.   We are building a team of people who are curious, collaborative, ambitious for impact and committed to turning ambition into action. If you want to help shape how a world-class research-led university develops innovative and entrepreneurial people, we would love to hear from you. .  He kōrero mōu | About you  You are passionate about entrepreneurship and enjoy working directly with founders as they navigate uncertainty, test ideas and develop early-stage ventures. Combining strong programme management and delivery skills with practical startup and venture development knowledge, you’re comfortable working with diverse founder cohorts and managing complex programmes involving multiple contributors and stakeholders.  Experience designing and delivering entrepreneurship, founder development, venture support or innovation programmes.   Strong understanding of startup ecosystems, customer discovery, experimentation, venture validation and early-stage venture development.   Experience working directly with founders, researchers or innovators through early-stage venture development.   Strong programme management and organisational capability, with the ability to manage multiple programmes, cohorts, priorities and relationships simultaneously.   Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to work effectively with founders, researchers, academics and external contributors.   Strong problem-solving skills and the ability to adapt programmes based on participant needs, feedback and insights.   Experience coordinating mentors, facilitators, industry experts and ecosystem partners to provide founders with access to relevant expertise.   Relevant qualification or equivalent professional experience in entrepreneurship, innovation, commercialisation or a related field.   Experience in startup or venture development, research commercialisation or a university environment would be advantageous. An understanding of research-derived opportunities and the different pathways they may take towards commercialisation and venture creation would be particularly valuable.  \n\n What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through:  Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours)  Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme  A competitive salary ($87,000 pa to $107,500 pa) reflecting experience with five weeks’ annual leave  In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.   For more information, please visit Staff Benefits     \n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 6th September, 2026 to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter highlighting how you meet the skills and experience outlined above, what attracts you to this opportunity, and how your experience has prepared you to manage complex founder and venture development programmes and work effectively with early-stage founders within a University context.  Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s pre-eminent University, with a turnover of $1.6bn, including research revenue of over $300m generated across eight diverse faculties and two research institutes. With over 9,000 staff and 50,000 students, the University of Auckland is one of New Zealand’s most significant and complex entities.",
      "department": "Faculty of Business and Economics"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145136142": {
      "title": "Clinical Case Manager",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for an experienced and compassionate Clinical Case Manager to join our Te Papa Manaaki | Campus Care team. This is a rewarding opportunity to make a genuine difference to students who may be experiencing complex personal, wellbeing, or mental health challenges. As a Clinical Case Manager, you’ll work directly with students, staff, and members of the University community to understand concerns, assess needs, and connect students with the right support. Using a case management approach, you’ll develop individual support plans, provide advocacy, and facilitate warm transfers to specialist services both within the University and in the wider community. You’ll help make what can sometimes feel like a complicated University environment easier to navigate, ensuring students receive timely, appropriate, and respectful support. This role is ideal for an experienced practitioner who brings strong clinical or wellbeing expertise, excellent judgement, and a genuine commitment to supporting young people. Key responsibilities include: Managing and triaging referrals and concerns from students, staff, and members of the University community. Gathering information, assessing risk, and developing appropriate support plans and referral pathways. Connecting students with the right support, from self-help resources through to specialist internal and external services. Providing advocacy and ongoing case management for students with multiple or complex needs. Responding to sensitive and potentially high-risk situations with professionalism, care, and sound judgement. Maintaining accurate case notes, reports, and confidential records while ensuring information is handled appropriately. Building strong relationships with University teams and community support providers to ensure students can access the right help. Contributing to continuous improvement by identifying trends, barriers, and opportunities to strengthen the service. This is a full-time, 37.5 hours per week, on a fixed-term contract for 18 months. Remuneration is $87,000- $107,500 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more information, please review the Position Description.   He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced practitioner who genuinely enjoys supporting young people and helping people navigate challenging circumstances. You bring a calm, empathetic, and professional approach, with the confidence to work with sensitive information and make sound decisions when situations are complex or require timely action. You'll be comfortable listening to students, understanding their individual circumstances, and working collaboratively to develop practical pathways forward. You also enjoy building relationships and working alongside colleagues and external providers to ensure students receive holistic support. Most importantly, you'll bring a strong commitment to student wellbeing, equity, culturally responsive practice, and supporting students to overcome barriers to success.   Ngā pūkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience A relevant tertiary qualification in social work, occupational therapy, mental health nursing, or a similar allied health field, together with current registration under the Health Practitioners Competence Assurance Act 2003. Proficiency in Te Reo Maori or Pacific languages is a must-have in this role At least five years' experience working with or providing mental health support to students or young people. Demonstrated experience using a case management approach to support individuals with a range of needs. Strong understanding of issues affecting students and young people, with the ability to work confidently with sensitive matters. Excellent communication, relationship-building, listening, and problem-solving skills. Cultural competence and an understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its implications for professional practice in Aotearoa New Zealand. Confidence using Microsoft Office and learning new University systems. The following would be beneficial: Experience working within a tertiary environment and an understanding of student needs. Knowledge of relevant University and community-based health, wellbeing, and support services.   Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team Te Papa Manaaki | Campus Care coordinates personal and wellbeing support for students. It provides early and holistic support with the aim of preventing further escalation, reducing the burden on the student of accessing help, minimising duplication and gaps in service provision across support providers, and minimising the burden on academic and other non-support staff of assessment and referral requirements. \n  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer \n \nThe University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices  Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . \n  Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply \n \nApplications must be submitted online, by the closing date of  7/09/2026  to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Chanel Tafa via chanel.tafa@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. \n \n We will begin reviewing applications as they come in and may close this advert early if a suitable candidate is found.  \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting more than 46,000 students, we are one of New Zealand’s largest employers. Within Te Kāhu Tauira | Student & Scholarly Services, Te Papa Manaaki | Campus Care provides early intervention and personal and wellbeing support to students. Our team helps students navigate challenges, connect with the right support, and overcome barriers that may affect their wellbeing and success.",
      "department": "Student and Scholarly Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145143269": {
      "title": "Health Promotion Adviser",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for an enthusiastic and experienced Health Promotion Adviser to join our Student Success and Wellbeing team. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in creating a healthier, more connected, and supportive university environment for our students. Working under the Oranga Tauira | Student Wellbeing Strategy, you will develop, coordinate, and deliver a range of student wellbeing programmes, campaigns, events, training, and activities designed to enhance student health and wellbeing across the University. A key part of the role will be ensuring that student voice is at the heart of our wellbeing initiatives. You will work closely with student leaders, clubs, and groups, coordinate listening hui and focus groups, and create opportunities for students to shape the programmes and initiatives that affect them. You'll also develop and deliver training, contribute to wellbeing communications and campaigns, undertake research into best practice, and evaluate programmes to ensure they are making a meaningful difference. This is a varied and rewarding role for someone who enjoys combining health promotion, project management, communications, training, research, and relationship building to create positive outcomes for young people. Key responsibilities include: Developing, planning, and delivering student wellbeing programmes, campaigns, events, activities, and resources. Coordinating student voice through focus groups, listening hui, student leaders, and student organisations. Developing and delivering wellbeing training and supporting student ambassadors to deliver training to their peers. Working collaboratively with Communications & Marketing to develop engaging and effective wellbeing communications. Researching best-practice approaches to student wellbeing and using evidence to inform programmes and messaging. Managing projects, budgets, risks, evaluations, and reporting to ensure initiatives are delivered effectively. Building strong relationships with students, University teams, and external organisations to support successful wellbeing initiatives. Identifying opportunities to continuously improve programmes and enhance the student experience. This is a full-time, 37.5-hour-per-week role on a permanent basis. The salary range for this role is $73,400 - $90,700 per annum. For more information, please review the position description.   He kōrero mōu | About you You are a passionate health promotion or wellbeing professional who enjoys working with young people and creating initiatives that make a genuine difference. You are confident managing multiple projects and stakeholders and can turn ideas into engaging programmes, events, training, and communications. You bring strong interpersonal skills and are comfortable connecting with a diverse student population, building trust, and working collaboratively across a complex organisation. You'll also be someone who enjoys using research, data, and feedback to understand what works and identify opportunities to do things better. Most importantly, you bring empathy, creativity, initiative, and a genuine commitment to student wellbeing and equity.   Ngā pūkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience A relevant degree in social work, community development, health promotion, public health, or a related field, and/or equivalent experience. At least five years' relevant experience, including experience working with young people in a health promotion capacity. Demonstrated experience coordinating multiple projects and activities involving a range of stakeholders. Experience developing, facilitating, and delivering training programmes for young people. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage effectively with a diverse student population. Experience analysing data, evaluating programmes, and using insights to make recommendations and improve outcomes. Strong project management, planning, coordination, and relationship-building skills. Knowledge of health promotion theory and practice and a commitment to continuous improvement. The following would be highly beneficial: Experience developing health promotion resources, communications, activities, and programmes. Experience working in a tertiary environment or with student clubs and organisations. Experience developing and delivering education and training activities.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits .   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 6/9/2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you meet the skills and experience required. Please note that we may close the role earlier if we find suitable candidates before the end date. Please reach out to Alejandra Gonzalez Campanella - alejandra.gonzalez@auckland.ac.nz with any questions about the role. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting more than 46,000 students, we are one of New Zealand’s largest employers. Within Te Kāhu Tauira | Student & Scholarly Services, our teams work across the student journey to create an inclusive, supportive, and engaging university experience. Our health and wellbeing programmes play an important role in helping students thrive both academically and personally.",
      "department": "Student and Scholarly Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145047839": {
      "title": "Medical Lead",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an experienced and inspiring Medical Lead to provide clinical leadership and strategic direction within our University Health and Counselling Service. This is a unique opportunity to combine clinical excellence with leadership in a purpose-driven environment that places student wellbeing at the heart of everything we do. As Medical Lead, you will play a key role in shaping the delivery of primary healthcare services for a diverse student population while supporting a team of dedicated clinicians to provide outstanding patient-centred care. Working closely with the General Manager and senior leadership team, you will contribute to service planning, clinical governance, quality improvement initiatives, and the ongoing development of a positive, collaborative, and high-performing clinical culture. This role offers the opportunity to make a meaningful impact on student health outcomes while helping shape the future of healthcare delivery within one of New Zealand’s leading universities. Key responsibilities include: Providing clinical leadership and support to medical practitioners within UHCS Promoting excellence in clinical practice, patient safety, and quality improvement initiatives Supporting clinical governance activities and ensuring compliance with relevant professional standards and legislation Contributing to service planning, operational decision-making, and strategic initiatives Working collaboratively with multidisciplinary teams including nurses, counsellors, psychologists, and administrative staff Supporting workforce development, mentoring, and professional growth within the medical team Maintaining a high standard of clinical care through direct patient consultations Supporting the development and implementation of evidence-based healthcare practices Building strong relationships with internal stakeholders and external healthcare providers Contributing to a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, and student-centred service delivery This role combines clinical leadership with hands-on practice, allowing you to continue delivering care while helping drive service excellence and positive health outcomes for students. This is a full-time , 37.5 hours per week role on a permanent contract. For more information, please review the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced and respected medical practitioner who is passionate about delivering high-quality healthcare and supporting the development of others. You bring strong clinical leadership skills, sound judgement, and the ability to foster positive relationships across multidisciplinary teams. You are committed to continuous improvement, patient-centred care, and creating an inclusive and supportive environment where both students and colleagues can thrive. Most importantly, you are motivated by the opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to student wellbeing while helping shape the future direction of a trusted and highly valued health service. Ngā pūkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience Current registration with the Medical Council of New Zealand and a current practising certificate Significant experience working in General Practice or primary healthcare Demonstrated clinical leadership experience and the ability to support and mentor clinicians Strong understanding of clinical governance, quality improvement, and patient safety principles Excellent communication and relationship-building skills Experience working effectively within multidisciplinary healthcare teams A commitment to culturally responsive practice and equitable health outcomes The following would be beneficial: Vocational registration in General Practice Experience working with young adults or within tertiary health services Experience leading service improvement and healthcare innovation initiatives Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland provides a supportive, people-centred working environment where your wellbeing and professional development are genuinely valued. As part of our team, you’ll enjoy: Flexible employment practices Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Five weeks’ annual leave A wide range of professional development opportunities Generous parental leave provisions Access to staff discounts, recreation facilities, wellbeing initiatives, and discounted parking The opportunity to combine clinical practice with leadership in a rewarding university healthcare environment The chance to make a lasting impact on the health, wellbeing, and success of thousands of students Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online to be considered. Please submit your application by 14 September 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV outlining how your skills and experience align with this role. For a confidential conversation about the role, please contact Tania Abernethy-Jones - tania.abernethy-jones@auckland.ac.nz. Please note that applications sent via email will not be accepted. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Within Te Kāhu Tauira | Student & Scholarly Services, the University Health and Counselling Service (UHCS) provides high-quality, accessible healthcare and wellbeing support to students across the University. Our multidisciplinary team is committed to delivering exceptional care, promoting wellbeing, and helping students thrive throughout their university journey.",
      "department": "Student and Scholarly Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000145045549": {
      "title": "Lead Technologist – Genomics - Te Kura Mātauranga Koiora | School of Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Biological Sciences is seeking an experienced genomics professional to lead the scientific and operational delivery of Auckland Genomics. Auckland Genomics is a core research platform, providing next-generation sequencing, genomics and related technical expertise to researchers from the University of Auckland and the wider research community. As Lead Technologist, you will lead a small team of specialist staff and oversee the day-to-day operation and continued development of the facility. You will work closely with researchers to understand their scientific questions, design appropriate experimental approaches and deliver high-quality genomics services. This is a hands-on leadership role. Alongside managing people and facility operations, you will contribute directly to complex genomics projects, introduce new methods and technologies, and help shape the future capability of Auckland Genomics. In this role, you will: Lead, support and develop the Auckland Genomics technical team. Provide expert advice to researchers on experimental design, sample requirements, sequencing approaches and project feasibility. Design and deliver projects involving next-generation sequencing, transcriptomics, genotyping and related genomic technologies. Maintain high standards of scientific quality, reproducibility, documentation and customer service. Evaluate emerging technologies and develop new or bespoke methods in response to researchers’ needs. This is a full-time permanent role (37.5 hours per week) The salary range is $98,700 to $121,900 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You will be an experienced genomics scientist or technical specialist who combines strong scientific judgement with practical laboratory expertise and effective people leadership. You will be comfortable discussing a research question with an academic or external client, translating it into a technically robust sequencing project, and guiding your team through its successful delivery. You will also understand the operational requirements of running a busy shared research facility, including workflow planning, quality systems, equipment management, budgets and competing client priorities. Our preferred candidate will have: A PhD in molecular biology, genomics or a related discipline, or significant equivalent relevant experience Substantial post-qualification experience in genomics, including experience providing specialist advice and technical support at a senior level. Demonstrated expertise in next-generation sequencing library preparation and associated molecular biology workflows. Experience operating, troubleshooting and maintaining high-value genomics instrumentation. Strong knowledge of experimental design, quality control and the technical factors affecting sequencing outcomes. Experience leading, supervising or mentoring technical or scientific staff. Strong communication and relationship-management skills, including the ability to work effectively with researchers from varied disciplines and levels of technical experience. Bioinformatics skills (e.g., bash scripting, sequencing QC; familiarity with High performance computing such as NESI/REANNZ advantageous) A proactive approach to developing new methods, improving workflows and responding to changes in sequencing technology. Experience with a range of short-read, long-read, single-cell and high-throughput genomic technologies would be advantageous. Experience contributing to facility budgets, cost-recovery models, equipment procurement or business cases would also be valuable. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 23rd September 2026 , and should include: A cover letter outlining your interest in the role and how your experience meets the key requirements. A current curriculum vitae. Please reach out to the Technical Manager Jo Dodd at jr.dodd@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n  Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science  at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tuākana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae . Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on both our student body and our scholarly community, and we work with both community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the peoples of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to pursuing sustainability.",
      "department": "Faculty of Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000144683469": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Finance",
      "description": "Ranked #67 in the QS World University Rankings 2027 Triple-crown accredited Business School (AACSB, EQUIS and AMBA) The Business School is a community of 6,885 EFT students across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, supported by the strength of 265 FTE academic staff and 105 FTE professional staff \n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. \n \nJoin New Zealand’s top-ranked and triple-crown accredited Auckland Business School, with an international reputation for shaping the future of research and teaching excellence, and be part of its continued growth as you embark on the next phase of your academic career. With strong growth in student numbers and sustained investment across the Business School, this is an exciting time to join as it continues to strengthen and expand its academic community. \n \n Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity As the Faculty continues to grow, we are seeking an ambitious, collegial, and research-active Lecturer or Senior Lecturer specialising in Finance to join the Business School's  Accounting and Finance Department .  We are seeking scholars with expertise in finance who will contribute to excellence in research and teaching. The successful candidate will contribute to finance teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels, bringing strong theoretical knowledge and research expertise in their area of specialisation. We are open to candidates with expertise in a range of areas within finance and welcome applications from scholars whose research contributes to contemporary finance scholarship and practice. In addition to growing your academic profile and reputation, you will also be committed to shaping and delivering an exceptional learning experience for students, contributing to an innovative teaching environment using a variety of methods. All candidates will bring the potential to contribute service and citizenship within and beyond the University, as well as to actively participate in Department, Faculty and University committees and activities. The Business School attracts high-calibre students to our world-class undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. We take pride in the excellence demonstrated by our internationally recognised scholars, who lead a vibrant research ecosystem supported by interdisciplinary research centres and a global network of visiting experts who contribute to both teaching and research. \n \nThis is a  full-time  (40 hours per week)  permanent  position (subject to continuation). Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and will be aligned with our  Academic Standards policy . It will be within the following ranges: Lecturer (L): NZ$99,788 - NZ$119,204 \nSenior Lecturer (SL): NZ$126,863 - NZ$158,904 For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description.   \n \n He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a collegial academic with excellent communication skills and the ability to clearly articulate your ideas, research, and future plans. You will hold a PhD in Finance, Financial Economics or a related discipline (or be nearing completion), along with: Potential (Lecturer) or proven (Senior Lecturer) track record of: conducting high-quality, independent research and contributing to publications in leading international journals successfully delivering engaging, research-informed teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels designing engaging learning activities, assessments and/or curriculum supervising Honours, Master's and, where appropriate, PhD students to completion developing research collaborations, contributing to competitive research funding applications, and engaging with industry, professional or other external partners. Industry or practical experience in finance, as well as engagement with professional networks, would be advantageous. \n \n Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes and mentoring support Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. \n \nFor more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . \n \n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of  Thursday 01 October 2026 (NZT) .  \n \nPlease include a cover letter outlining your interest and ability to relocate if required, CV, statement of your teaching philosophy, teaching evaluations and a statement of the direction and ambition of your research programme, with a full publication, PG supervision and research grant record. \n \nPlease direct enquiries to Prasanna Gai, Head of Department, via p.gai@auckland.ac.nz. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but applications must be made online.  \n \n#LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Faculty of Business and Economics"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000144680409": {
      "title": "General Practitioner",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are currently seeking an experienced General Practitioners to join our supportive and collaborative team. In this role, you will provide high-quality, patient-centred primary health care to a diverse student population. Working within a multidisciplinary team of doctors, nurses, counsellors, and psychologists, you will support students’ physical and mental wellbeing, helping them navigate the unique challenges of university life.  This is a rewarding opportunity to work in a wellbeing-focused environment where preventative care, early intervention, and holistic support are at the heart of the service we provide. Your work will make a meaningful difference to students at a pivotal stage of their lives. Please note that we offer 20 minute appointments to our patients. Key responsibilities include: Providing skilled health assessment, diagnosis, and treatment services in line with current best practice and professional standards Delivering patient-centred care that supports both physical and mental wellbeing Working collaboratively with nurses, counsellors, and other health professionals to provide integrated care Managing referrals, documentation, follow-up care, and case management in a timely and professional manner Supporting preventative health initiatives, including screening programmes, vaccinations, and health education Providing travel health advice and vaccinations where required Assessing aegrotat applications and providing appropriate academic health advocacy Building strong relationships with internal and external healthcare providers and referral agencies These are permanent positions, and we are flexible with hours — we welcome applications from both full-time and part-time practitioners, depending on your availability. For more information, please review the Position Description.   He kōrero mōu | About you You are a compassionate and experienced clinician who enjoys working with young adults and supporting diverse communities. You bring strong clinical judgement, excellent communication skills, and a calm, professional approach to patient care. You will bring: Registration as a medical practitioner in New Zealand with a current NZMC Practising Certificate and indemnity insurance Fellowship of the Royal New Zealand College of General Practitioners. At least two years’ experience in General Practice, ideally including primary or emergency care Current CPR or Advanced Life Support certification Experience working in a multidisciplinary healthcare environment Proven experience working with young adults and diverse communities Confidence using clinical systems such as MedTech or similar practice management software Experience working within a university or student-focused health service would be an advantage but is not essential.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits .   How to apply | Me pēhea te tuku tono  Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 17/09/2026 to be considered. For any job-specific questions, please reach out to Tania Abernethy-Jones - tania.abernethy-jones@auckland.ac.nz. Please include your cover letter and/or CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above.  \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland \nThe University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.",
      "department": "Student and Scholarly Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000144485329": {
      "title": " Research Fellow - Te Kura Mātauranga Koiora | School of Biological Sciences - 13 month fixed term ",
      "description": "Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team \n Te Kura Mātauranga Kiora/The School of Biological Sciences is the largest School within the Faculty of Science.  We are a lively, interdisciplinary School with around 100 academic staff and 4000 students across 38 undergraduate and 34 postgraduate courses. We have international research leaders across a range of fields, organised into three groupings; Ecological and Evolutionary Biology; Cellular, Molecular and Physiological Biology; Biomedical and Human Biology.   We are served by   well-developed research facilities including controlled environment plant growth facilities, several scientific reserves and access to  shared equipment centres  for genomics and mass spectroscopy, amongst others.  The School prides itself on its strong links with research, commercial, and community organisations nationally and internationally. SBS is also home to the University of Auckland’s first and oldest Tuākana programme (est. 1991) which focuses on Māori and Pacific student academic learning success. We are in the heart of Auckland/Tāmaki Makaurau, a harbour-side city with rich cultural diversity, beaches, islands, iconic volcanoes, and lush forests. Auckland is consistently ranked in the top tier in global quality of life surveys.   Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity \nIn this role you will undertake: Research and provide technical support on a Marsden funded project aiming to understand the patient response to immunotherapies. With a focus on melanoma, we aim to identify mechanisms that underlie T cell memory and response to immune checkpoint inhibitors – to provide knowledge that can be exploited to improve all forms of immunotherapy. I n vitro studies using primary human cancer and T cells and techniques including CRISPR based gene editing, next generation sequencing and analysis, ddPCR, flow cytometry, immunocytochemistry, and western blotting. In vivo studies using mouse models. additional responsibilities are: Undertake an active, appropriate, and viable research, with oversight from the named investigators Writing up results of research and contributing to the publication of results, including in high-quality peer-reviewed scientific journals Deliver progress reports as required Develop and maintain an up-to-date academic knowledge in the relevant research field Maintain lab book and accurate sample inventories in timely manner Essential Requirements : PhD in Molecular Biology/Medical Science or related disciplines Prior laboratory research experience Computer: Word, Excel, PowerPoint This is a full-time (40 hours per week), 13-month fixed-term contract The salary range is RF1 - $99,788 per annum For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 19th September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Dr. Hilary Sheppard via h.sheppard@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n  Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science  at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tuākana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae .",
      "department": "Faculty of Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000144451429": {
      "title": "Laboratory Animal Technician",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Laboratory Animal Technician to join our busy research unit within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences. This role plays a critical part in supporting world‑leading research by ensuring the highest standards of animal care, welfare, and compliance. You will be part of a friendly, highly skilled technical team who take pride in their work and in supporting researchers to deliver meaningful outcomes that improve lives in Aotearoa New Zealand and beyond. In this role, you will be responsible for: Providing high-quality animal husbandry, hygiene, and breeding support while maintaining excellent animal health and welfare standards Supporting research staff and programmes through routine and specialist animal care procedures Monitoring animal welfare and ensuring all activities comply with Animal Ethics Committee approvals and relevant legislation Maintaining accurate records, databases, and documentation for animal care and operational activities Supporting containment, security, and hygiene standards across assigned facilities Assisting with the operation, maintenance, and appropriate use of equipment within the facility Working collaboratively with colleagues, researchers, and students in a busy research environment This role includes weekend and public holiday rostered work. This is a  fulltime (37.5h per week ),  permanent  position. The salary range for this role is  $63,600 - $79,300 , depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please review the  position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are someone who is genuinely passionate about animal welfare and takes pride in delivering high standards of care. You enjoy working as part of a team, communicate clearly, and are comfortable balancing routine tasks with more specialised procedures. You bring a practical, calm approach to your work, are confident handling animals, and understand the importance of compliance, accuracy, and professionalism in a research setting. A reasonable level of physical fitness is required for this role. You will bring: Qualification in animal care or relevant practical experience Experience working with animals, ideally within a laboratory or research environment Knowledge of, or ability to learn, New Zealand animal welfare and regulatory requirements Confidence handling both small and large animals Strong communication skills and the ability to work both independently and as part of a team Good computer literacy and record‑keeping skills Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 4 September 2026 . To be considered, applications must include both a cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please note we do not accept applications by email.       #LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau   | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities.",
      "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000143928019": {
      "title": "Dean, Faculty of Science | Manukura Pūtaiao",
      "description": "Lead the Faculty and champion the academic pursuit of Science, across all its diversity. Contribute to the strategic leadership of Aotearoa New Zealand’s top-ranked (#67 QS 2027) research-intensive university as a member of the executive team. Waipapa Taumata Rau, the University of Auckland is seeking an inspirational leader with outstanding interpersonal skills and a commitment to discovery and innovation through world-class research and education to become our Dean, Faculty of Science | Manukura Pūtaiao. A key member of the University Executive reporting to the Vice-Chancellor, the Dean will balance the role of being the Faculty’s leader and greatest advocate, while contributing to the success of the whole University, collaborating constructively with Deans, Directors and other university leaders. The Dean will be ambitious for the people and their endeavours in the Faculty and beyond. Applying strategic, management and financial acumen, the Dean will create an environment of excellence, innovation and inclusion where our 900 staff and 9000 students  are empowered and challenged to flourish, inclusive, thus ensuring the faculty’s lasting success. The Dean will draw on their academic credentials to provide visionary leadership to enhance our reputation as New Zealand’s largest and most diverse Faculty of Science. Academic eminence will be combined with the belief that people are the Faculty’s greatest asset. The ideal candidate will bring the leadership experience to foster a culture of excellence in both research and teaching, collegiality, collaboration and trust, ensuring all staff are encouraged and enabled to achieve at the highest level. Candidates who are not already familiar with New Zealand’s bicultural context will be expected to engage with Māori culture and systems of knowledge, and to develop a sound understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and New Zealand’s place in the Pacific. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The Dean will demonstrate the insight, experience and commitment to provide continued leadership to equity, diversity and inclusion. The Dean will partner with others within and beyond the Faculty to explore and enhance opportunities that strengthen the Faculty’s future impact and sustainability, including growing the philanthropic base while deepening relationships with an existing portfolio of major donors, trusts and corporates. An accomplished relationship builder, the Dean will have expertise in developing meaningful engagement with government, industry, global and local science communities, international institutional and educational partners and wider communities, building a pipeline of opportunities for future generations.   Waipapa Taumata Rau, the University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading university and one of the Asia-Pacific region's most internationally connected institutions. We are ambitious in our scholarship, confident in our public role, and deeply committed to helping shape the future of Aotearoa New Zealand. As a comprehensive research-intensive university, we seek to combine global excellence with a deep commitment to the society we serve. \n\n Applications must be submitted by 27 September 2026 (further detail in the Candidate booklet ).  For a confidential discussion, please contact the University of Auckland as below:  Clare Litten, email: C.litten@auckland.ac.nz   Position Reference: REF47368M Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Faculty of Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000143705059": {
      "title": "Director e-Research",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity   Lead the strategic direction of e-Research at Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland and help position the University at the forefront of digital research enablement. As Director e-Research, you will play a pivotal role in shaping the future of research infrastructure, services and capabilities that support world-class research across all disciplines.  This is an exciting time to join the University as we continue to strengthen and evolve our e-Research capability to meet the rapidly changing needs of researchers. You will help drive the next phase of research enablement, advancing capabilities across research AI, advanced computing, research data services and digital research platforms, while shaping strategic priorities and investment decisions that will support research excellence for years to come.  Working in partnership with academic leaders, researchers and professional staff, you will strengthen the University's position within the national and international e-Research ecosystem and help unlock new opportunities for collaboration, innovation and research impact.  As a senior member of the Digital Services leadership team, you will also contribute to the University's broader digital strategy and organisational priorities.  For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description . Please note, this is a full-time (37.5hours/ week), permanent position based in Auckland, New Zealand. He kōrero mōu | About you  You are a strategic and collaborative leader who understands how technology and digital capability can accelerate research excellence. You bring people together, build trusted relationships and thrive in complex environments where innovation, influence and partnership are key.  To be successful in this role, you will bring:  Significant leadership experience within a complex research, technology, innovation or digital environment, including leading multidisciplinary teams and organisational capability development.  Experience developing and delivering strategy, transformation initiatives and investment roadmaps that deliver sustainable outcomes.  Strong understanding of digital infrastructure, advanced computing, research technologies, AI-enabled research, research data services or related domains.  Exceptional stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence senior leaders, work effectively within governance environments and build partnerships across academic, technical and executive communities.  Commercial and financial acumen, combined with a passion for advancing research excellence through innovation, collaboration and impactful service delivery.  Experience within the tertiary sector would be advantageous; however, we welcome applications from leaders across research organisations, government, science and innovation environments, digital infrastructure providers and related sectors.  Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team Digital Services provides the technology, platforms and digital capabilities that enable the University to teach, learn, research and operate effectively. The team delivers secure, reliable and innovative services that support staff and students across the University.   The e-Research function enables world-class research through specialist expertise in advanced computing, research data management, digital research infrastructure and emerging research technologies. Working across disciplines and faculties, the team helps researchers leverage technology to achieve greater research impact and discovery.  \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university. We employ more than 5,400 academic and professional staff to support over 41,000 students, making us one of NZ’s largest employers.    Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer  The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.   The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through:  Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours)  Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme  A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave  In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.   For more information please visit  Staff Benefits .  Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply  Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 13th September 2026 (NZST) to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above.   Please reach out to the Recruitment Team via directoreresearch@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Enable the future of research at Waipapa Taumata Rau   Research is at the heart of the University of Auckland | Waipapa Taumata Rau. As Director e-Research, you will lead the University's e-Research capability, shaping the digital infrastructure, services and partnerships that enable world-class research across all disciplines.  As the University continues to invest in its research ecosystem, we are evolving our approach to e-Research to create a more connected, strategically aligned capability that supports researchers across faculties, large-scale research institutes and the University's six Signature Research Areas . This newly created leadership role represents an exciting opportunity to bring together previously distributed expertise, services and ways of working into a cohesive University-wide capability, helping accelerate discovery, foster collaboration and amplify the impact of our research.  This is a unique opportunity to influence the future of research technologies, advanced computing, AI-enabled research and research data services at New Zealand's leading university.",
      "department": "Digital Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000143440279": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Counselling ",
      "description": "Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Education and Social Practice within Te Pūtahi Mātauranga | Faculty of Arts and Education is seeking a Lecturer in Counselling to contribute to our postgraduate Counsellor Education Programme. This is an opportunity to contribute to the education of future counselling professionals through a programme that combines counselling theory, research and supervised professional practice. You will contribute to postgraduate teaching, research supervision and curriculum development, while maintaining strong connections with the counselling profession and wider community. We are particularly interested in applicants with expertise in integrative counselling practice and professional supervision. Expertise across the wider counselling curriculum is also advantageous, particularly in areas such as trauma-informed practice, counselling children and families, or culturally and contextually responsive practice. Key responsibilities include: Teaching and contributing to the development of postgraduate counselling courses. Supervising Master's and doctoral research students and contributing to curriculum development and scholarship. Developing an active research profile and contributing to collaborative research. Building strong relationships with the counselling profession and wider communities, and contributing to the academic and collegial life of the Programme, School and Faculty. This is a full-time (40 hours per week) permanent position (subject to continuation). The workload split is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. The salary range for this role is $99,788 - $119,204 per annum. Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and is aligned with our Academic Standards policy. For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description. He kōrero mōu | About you You are a qualified and experienced counselling professional with expertise in integrative counselling practice and professional supervision. You bring a strong commitment to counsellor education and the continued development of counselling practice, together with a thoughtful, reflexive and relational approach to working with students and diverse communities. To be successful in this role, you will bring: A completed doctorate, relevant counselling qualifications and professional counselling experience. Membership of the New Zealand Association of Counsellors or a comparable professional counselling organisation. Experience teaching and/or supervising at postgraduate level, with the ability to contribute to high-quality curriculum delivery and development. An active or developing research profile, with demonstrated potential for continued research productivity. Strong knowledge of integrative counselling practice and professional supervision, alongside an understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its implications for professional practice. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Sunday 13 September to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter outlining your interest in and suitability for the role. Please direct enquiries to Jay Marlowe (Associate Head of School) via jm.marlowe@auckland.ac.nz. Please note while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications via email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Arts and Education"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000143438219": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Social Studies ",
      "description": "Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Education and Social Practice within Te Pūtahi Mātauranga | Faculty of Arts and Education is seeking a Lecturer in Secondary Social Studies Education to help shape the next generation of secondary Social Studies teachers and contribute to the ongoing development of the discipline. Drawing on your experience in both secondary and tertiary Social Studies education, you will contribute to the delivery and development of our teacher education programmes, supervise postgraduate students and develop a programme of high-quality research with scholarly and societal impact. Key responsibilities include: Teaching and contributing to curriculum development across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Supervising postgraduate students and contributing to the scholarship of teaching and learning. Developing and disseminating high-quality research, and pursuing opportunities for research collaboration and funding. Contributing to course and programme leadership, academic service and relationships across the wider education community. This is a full-time (40 hours per week) permanent position (subject to continuation). The workload split is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. The salary range for this role is $99,788 - $119,204 per annum. Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and is aligned with our Academic Standards policy. For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description. He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced Social Studies educator with a strong understanding of secondary education and a commitment to preparing future teachers. You bring experience teaching and leading Social Studies in secondary schools, alongside tertiary teaching experience and a proven record of high-quality research. To be successful in this role, you will bring: A PhD in Social Studies or a closely related discipline Experience teaching and leading Social Studies and related subjects, such as History or Classics, at secondary school level. Experience teaching Social Studies education at the tertiary level. A proven record of high-quality research and publications, with demonstrable scholarly and societal impact. Experience teaching or undertaking research in culturally and/or linguistically diverse communities. Strong communication, collaboration and relationship-building skills. Current New Zealand teacher registration, familiarity with national curriculum and education policy, and knowledge of evidence-informed approaches such as culturally responsive, inclusive or digital pedagogies will be advantageous. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Sunday 13 September to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter outlining your interest in and suitability for the role. Please direct enquiries to Rebecca Jesson (Co-Associate Head of School) via r.jesson@auckland.ac.nz. Please note while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications via email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Arts and Education"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000143407849": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor in Psychiatry",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The University of Auckland (Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences) is seeking to appoint an Associate Professor/ Senior Lecturer in Psychiatry.  Are you looking to further your career in Psychiatry?  This part-time academic position will ideally be combined with a part-time clinical component, tailored to the successful candidate, and will ideally suit and be a great opportunity for an enthusiastic psychiatrist who wants to combine a clinical appointment with a university career. This role is particularly suitable for an early to mid-career academic psychiatrist. The anticipated start date for this position is early December 2026. This opportunity is for a permanent role, part time for 16 hours per week (Associate Professor) or 20 hours per week (Senior Lecturer). The salary range for each academic level are: Senior Lecturer: $126,863 - $145,741 (fulltime equivalent) Associate Professor: $165,808 (fulltime equivalent) Your salary will be determined based on your experience, qualifications, and the level at which you are appointed. For more detailed information, please refer to the  Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you Candidates must hold a medical qualification registrable in New Zealand, must have a higher qualification in psychiatry equivalent to the Fellowship of the Royal Australian and New Zealand College of Psychiatrists (FRANZCP) and be eligible for vocational registration by the Medical Council of New Zealand as a Specialist Psychiatrist. Candidates must have experience in research and ideally hold an MD/PhD or equivalent.   Essential: Registered with the New Zealand Medical Council. Hold a current Annual Practising Certificate Have vocational registration in Psychiatry (FRANZCP or equivalent) Hold professional liability insurance Preferred: MD/PhD Psychiatry if no MD/PhD, the candidate should be prepared to undertake doctoral studies part-time if requested by and with the support of the HOD and Clinical Director of relevant service. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The School of Medicine is one of the six schools within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences. The hub is based at Grafton Campus with other clinical campuses in South Auckland, Waitematā, Waikato and Tauranga. In addition, there are clinical sites in Northland, Whakatāne, Rotorua, Taranaki and Hawera.   The School of Medicine coordinates staff and resources within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences to: Deliver the undergraduate medical programme Facilitate and encourage clinical and applied research in clinical departments Develop and deliver postgraduate programmes in clinical areas covered by the school Work in partnership with the School of Medical Sciences, School of Population Health and Medical Programme Directorate to deliver the medical programme Our school is comprised of eight academic departments: Anaesthesiology Centre for Medical and Health Sciences Education Medicine Obstetrics and Gynaecology Ophthalmology Paediatrics: Child and Youth Health Psychological Medicine Surgery A key priority for the School of Medicine is the provision of clinical undergraduate training for almost half of New Zealand’s medical students, as well as postgraduate training for a range of health care workers. We have large and successful research programmes and a strong record of postgraduate research supervision. Many of the our academics contribute to New Zealand’s health outcomes through both direct clinical service and service on many national and international organisations related to health care, including specialist colleges and government agencies. Our school has a strong focus to build the Māori and Pacific workforce. The interprofessional opportunities within the faculty are one of our great strengths and we are committed to working with the wider faculty and external providers in advancing this objective.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours, with some on-campus attendance required) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 13 September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Professor Trecia Wouldes (Head of Department – Psychological Medicine), via t.wouldes@auckland.ac.nz , for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa  New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students.",
      "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000141834348": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer – Biomedical Science - Te Kura Mātauranga Koiora | School of Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are inviting applications from scientists interested in Biomedical Science to join our team in the Te Kura Mātauranga Koiora | School of Biological Sciences for one position at Lecturer/Senior Lecturer level. The successful applicant will be expected to establish a competitive and innovative research programme to attract external research funding. This position will involve contributing to our undergraduate and postgraduate teaching programmes, supervising postgraduate students and developing inter-institutional research projects. Teaching may be required at international partner institutions, including in China, for a period of approximately one month at a time. The role includes contributing to service-related activities (University and community).  The anticipated start date for this position is early-2027 or as soon as reasonably possible. For more information on the role, please view the Position Description   This is a full-time (40 hrs per week), permanent role. The remuneration ranges for each academic level are outlined below. Your final appointment and salary will be determined based on your experience, qualifications, and the level at which you are appointed. Lecturer: $99,788 - $119,204 Senior Lecturer: $126,863  He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for a person with a strong academic background in biomedical science with the aim of improving health outcomes for New Zealanders. Research expertise may span fundamental, applied, or translational biomedical science, including molecular and cellular mechanisms through to integrated physiological systems, and the development of approaches that translate scientific discoveries into tangible benefits for New Zealand communities. A PhD in a relevant discipline and a demonstrated history of research excellence, with peer-reviewed publications and impact, are necessary. Applicants at the lecturer level should demonstrate potential for excellence in teaching and supervision, as well as strong international or community links. Applicants at the Senior Lecturer level are expected to have demonstrated high-quality teaching and supervision abilities, along with strong international and/or community links, plus academic citizenship, service, and evidence of growing leadership. Staff at the University of Auckland are expected to contribute to upholding Te Tiriti o Waitangi in their work and enacting the principles outlined in Taumata Teitei, the University’s strategic plan. You will need to be passionate about student development and willing to support and mentor our students at all levels. The main purpose of the role will be to: (i) teach and coordinate courses at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels; (ii) undertake original research and secure external funding; (iii) to supervise research students towards Honours, Masters and Doctoral degrees; and (iv) to work collegially to participate in the organisation and activities of SBS. Communicating science, outreach and the application of research to address challenges relevant to New Zealanders is expected. The successful applicant will demonstrate synergy or complementarity with other research groups within SBS, to enhance the research output, impact, teaching quality, and reputation of the University. Preference will be given to applicants with the ability to broaden the School’s research expertise and/or potential for translation in areas such as infection and immunity, molecular physiology and biomedical technologies .   Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team SBS is a large, lively, and interdisciplinary School delivering excellence in research and teaching, with well-developed research facilities, including controlled environment plant growth facilities, access to shared equipment centres (such as those for genomics and mass spectroscopy), and strong international and community links. We are in the heart of Auckland/Tāmaki Makaurau, a harbour-side city with rich cultural diversity, beaches, islands, iconic volcanoes, and lush forests. Auckland is consistently ranked in the top tier in global quality of life surveys.   SBS is home to the University of Auckland’s first and oldest Tuākana programme (established in 1991), that focuses on enhancing the academic learning success of Māori and Pacific students. We are committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential in an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our links with diverse communities. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of  5thSeptember 2026 to be considered. Shortlisting and interviews are scheduled to take place in November 2026. In accordance with our commitment to equitable recruitment, you need not tick every box to be considered. The University welcomes applications from all suitably qualified and experienced candidates. The application must include:  A Cover Letter and CV, as well as information on your approach to teaching and proposed research programme.  Please also include evidence of qualifications and contact information, including email addresses, of three references. Please note that letters of reference may be requested for longlisted candidates. Please reach out to Associate Professor David Goldstone (Head of School -Biological Sciences) via hod.biologicalsciences@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Te Whare Pūtaiao | Faculty of Science   is the leading science faculty in Aotearoa New Zealand and one of the largest in Australasia. We undertake world-class research across the physical, biological, and computational sciences. More than 9,000 students study across our ten schools and departments, learning from internationally recognised educators and researchers. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including  Tuākana ,  Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network  and our  Waipapa Marae .",
      "department": "Faculty of Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000141599068": {
      "title": "Pouako|Lecturer - Te Kura Tatauranga|Department of Statistics/ Te Kura Mātauranga Koiora – School of Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Shape the future of Statistics education and research in Aotearoa New Zealand Te Kura Tatauranga | Statistics  and Te Kura Mātauranga Koiora | Biological Sciences are seeking an early-career academic to join us as a jointly appointed Lecturer on a permanent contract. This is an exciting opportunity to build your academic career in a high-performing, globally recognised department known for excellence in both teaching and research. As a Lecturer, you will play a key role across teaching, research, and service. You’ll contribute to a collaborative academic environment while developing your own research profile and supporting student success at all levels. Ngā Haepapa Matua | Key Responsibilities (not limited to): Deliver high-quality teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels Coordinate courses and develop innovative curriculum and learning materials Supervise Honours, Masters, and PhD students Establish and grow a strong research programme with high-quality publications and external funding Collaborate with colleagues locally and internationally Contribute to service and leadership within the Department and Faculty Support a diverse, inclusive, and equitable learning environment aligned with Te Tiriti o Waitangi For more detailed information on the role, accountabilities, and requirements, please review this  position description. This is a full-time (40 hrs per week) permanent role. The remuneration range is $99,788 - $119,204 per annum, dependent on skills and experience. He kōrero mōu | About you We’re looking for emerging academics who are passionate about teaching and research, and ready to take the next step in their academic career. Essential requirements PhD in Statistics, Data Science, or a closely related field An interest in biological data Demonstrated track record of publications in high-quality journals Ability to work independently across the full research and publication process Strong organisational skills Preferred experience Experience lecturing in Statistics or Data Science Evidence of teaching excellence (e.g. student feedback, peer review, awards) Experience supervising or co-supervising postgraduate research Ability to secure or show potential for research funding Experience contributing to academic service and administration We are particularly interested in candidates who can contribute to advanced Applied Statistics teaching, including areas such as deep learning, large language models, experimental design, and time series, and who have an interest in biological data. The ability to conduct in-person and hybrid teaching in China is also advantageous. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities.  The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave Opportunity for Research and Study Leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services.  For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 4th September 2026  to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV, highlighting how you can meet the aforementioned skills and experience. Your CV should include a list of refereed publications and a list of courses you have taught, clearly indicating whether you were giving lectures or tutorials and the level of independence in your teaching. Also, if you have recent student evaluations of your teaching or other teaching reviews, please upload them. If needed, you may reach out to Beatrix Jones (incoming Head of Department) via beatrix.jones@auckland.ac.nz  for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Te Whare Pūtaiao | Faculty of Science   is the leading science faculty in Aotearoa New Zealand and one of the largest in Australasia. We undertake world-class research across the physical, biological, and computational sciences. More than 9,000 students study across our ten schools and departments, learning from internationally recognised educators and researchers. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including  Tuākana ,  Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network  and our  Waipapa Marae .",
      "department": "Faculty of Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000140381170": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Management Accounting",
      "description": "Ranked #67 in the QS World University Rankings 2027 Triple-crown accredited Business School (AACSB, EQUIS and AMBA) The Business School is a community of 6,885 EFT students across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, supported by the strength of 265 FTE academic staff and 105 FTE professional staff \n  Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. \n \nJoin New Zealand’s top-ranked and triple-crown accredited Auckland Business School, with an international reputation for shaping the future of research and teaching excellence, and be part of its continued growth as you embark on the next phase of your academic career. With strong growth in student numbers and sustained investment across the Business School, this is an exciting time to join as it continues to strengthen and expand its academic community. \n \n Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity  As the Faculty continues to grow, we are seeking an ambitious, collegial, and research-active Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Management Accounting to join the Business School's Accounting and Finance Department . We are seeking scholars with expertise in management accounting who will contribute to excellence in research and teaching. The successful candidate will teach intermediate and advanced management accounting at undergraduate and postgraduate levels, bringing the technical knowledge and professional expertise to teach management accounting, accounting for decision-making, and related topics. In addition to growing your academic profile and reputation, you will also be committed to shaping and delivering an exceptional learning experience for students, contributing to an innovative teaching environment using a variety of methods. We are particularly interested in researchers whose work explores broad areas of management accounting, including management accounting and control, accounting for decision-making, and sustainability accounting. This may encompass areas such as performance measurement, management control systems, sustainability measurement, reporting and assurance, and other contemporary issues in management accounting research and practice. All candidates will bring the potential to contribute service and citizenship within and beyond the University, as well as to actively participate in Department, Faculty and University committees and activities. The Business School attracts high-calibre students to our world-class undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. We take pride in the excellence demonstrated by our internationally recognised scholars, who lead a vibrant research ecosystem supported by interdisciplinary research centres and a global network of visiting experts who contribute to both teaching and research. \n \nThis is a  full-time  (40 hours per week)  permanent  position (subject to continuation). Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and will be aligned with our  Academic Standards policy . It will be within the following ranges: Lecturer (L): NZ$99,788 - NZ$119,204 \nSenior Lecturer (SL): NZ$126,863 - NZ$158,904 For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the  Position Description . \n \n He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a collegial academic with excellent communication skills and the ability to clearly articulate your ideas and future research plans. You will hold a PhD in Financial Accounting or a related discipline (or be nearing completion), along with: \n \nPotential (L) or proven (SL/AP) track record of: successfully delivering Under- and Postgraduate courses publications in top-ranked journals in relevant disciplines (e.g., ABDC A* or A-ranked or AJG 4*, 4 or 3-ranked journals) attracting funding from internal and external reputable funding schemes designing curriculum (part or entire course/programme) successfully supervising PhD and Masters students to completion in a relevant discipline. \n  Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes and mentoring support Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. \n \nFor more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . \n \n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by  Sunday, 27 September 2026 .  \n \nPlease include a cover letter outlining your interest and ability to relocate if required, CV, statement of your teaching philosophy, teaching evaluations and a statement of the direction and ambition of your research programme, with a full publication, PG supervision and research grant record. \n \nPlease direct enquiries to Professor Charl de Villiers ( charl.devilliers@auckland.ac.nz ). Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but applications must be made online \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Faculty of Business and Economics"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000140376669": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Financial Accounting",
      "description": "Ranked #67 in the QS World University Rankings 2027 Triple-crown accredited Business School (AACSB, EQUIS and AMBA) The Business School is a community of 6,885 EFT students across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, supported by the strength of 265 FTE academic staff and 105 FTE professional staff \n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. \n \nJoin New Zealand’s top-ranked and triple-crown accredited Auckland Business School, with an international reputation for shaping the future of research and teaching excellence, and be part of its continued growth as you embark on the next phase of your academic career. With strong growth in student numbers and sustained investment across the Business School, this is an exciting time to join as it continues to strengthen and expand its academic community. \n  Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity As the Faculty continues to grow, we are seeking an ambitious, collegial, and research-active Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Financial Accounting to join the Business School's Accounting and Finance Department . We are seeking scholars with expertise in financial accounting who will contribute to excellence in research and teaching. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching intermediate and advanced financial accounting at undergraduate and postgraduate levels, with expertise in International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS). We also welcome applicants with expertise in auditing and assurance, including the application of professional judgement and professional scepticism in the audit of financial statements. In addition to growing your academic profile and reputation, you will also be committed to shaping and delivering an exceptional learning experience for students, contributing to an innovative teaching environment using a variety of methods. We are particularly interested in researchers whose work explores areas including strategic disclosures, regulatory enforcement and compliance, ESG measurement and disclosure, investor relations, the interpretation and enforcement of accounting and auditing standards, and auditing and assurance. All candidates will bring the potential to contribute service and citizenship within and beyond the University, as well as to actively participate in Department, Faculty and University committees and activities. The Business School attracts high-calibre students to our world-class undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. We take pride in the excellence demonstrated by our internationally recognised scholars, who lead a vibrant research ecosystem supported by interdisciplinary research centres and a global network of visiting experts who contribute to both teaching and research. \n \nThis is a  full-time  (40 hours per week)  permanent  position (subject to continuation). \n \nRemuneration depends on experience and achievements, and will be aligned with our  Academic Standards policy . It will be within the following ranges: \n \nLecturer (L): NZ$99,788 - NZ$119,204 \nSenior Lecturer (SL): NZ$126,863 - NZ$158,904 \n \nFor more information about this role, please refer to the  Position Description . \n \n He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a collegial academic with excellent communication skills and the ability to clearly articulate your ideas and future research plans. You will hold a PhD in Financial Accounting or a related discipline (or be nearing completion), along with: \n \nPotential (L) or proven (SL/AP) track record of: successfully delivering Under- and Postgraduate courses publications in top-ranked journals in relevant disciplines (e.g., ABDC A* or A-ranked or AJG 4*, 4 or 3-ranked journals) attracting funding from internal and external reputable funding schemes designing curriculum (part or entire course/programme) successfully supervising PhD and Masters students to completion in a relevant discipline. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes and mentoring support Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits . \n \n Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by  Sunday, 27 September 2026 .  \n \nPlease include a cover letter outlining your interest and ability to relocate if required, CV, statement of your teaching philosophy, teaching evaluations and a statement of the direction and ambition of your research programme, with a full publication, PG supervision and research grant record. \n \nPlease direct enquiries to Professor Tom Scott ( tom.scott@auckland.ac.nz ). Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but applications must be made online. \n \n#LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz",
      "department": "Faculty of Business and Economics"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000138508019": {
      "title": "Senior Systems Security Engineer ",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We're building the future of cybersecurity at the University of Auckland. To strengthen the protection of some of the University's most sensitive information, the Cybersecurity team is establishing a brand-new Health Data Management team dedicated to safeguarding systems that process sensitive health data. As part of this investment, we are looking to appoint four Senior Systems Security Engineers to help design, implement, and govern the security controls, standards, and processes required to achieve and maintain compliance with the Health Information Security Framework (HISF) across a diverse range of critical systems. These newly created roles will work across cloud and on-premise environments to identify security risks, implement technical controls, strengthen system configurations, and ensure our health systems meet the highest standards of information security and regulatory compliance. These opportunities are ideally suited to experienced security engineers who have worked in regulated environments and understand frameworks such as HISF, Digital Health Security Framework (DHSF), HITRUST CSF, ISO 27001, or similar governance and assurance models. You'll bring extensive hands-on engineering experience across operating systems, infrastructure, networks, and enterprise platforms, together with the ability to design, validate, and continuously improve technical controls. If you're passionate about security engineering, governance, compliance, and building secure-by-design environments that protect critical information, we'd love to hear from you. Key responsibilities include: Designing, implementing, and maintaining technical security controls to support compliance across systems processing sensitive health information. Partnering with Product Owners and engineering teams to assess security risks, develop mitigation strategies, and strengthen system resilience. Conducting security assessments, validating technical controls, and producing clear risk and assurance documentation. Developing and improving security standards, governance processes, and compliance practices aligned to recognised health security frameworks. Leveraging security tooling to monitor compliance, identify risks, and improve visibility across the University's digital environment. Driving continuous improvement by strengthening security practices, technical controls, and engineering processes across Digital Services. These are full-time, 37.5-hour-per-week roles on a permanent basis . The salary range is $110,700 - $149,800 per annum, depending on experience and qualifications. For more information, please review the Position Description.   He kōrero mōu | About you You're an experienced security engineer who enjoys solving complex technical challenges while helping organisations strengthen their security posture. You thrive in environments where security, governance, and engineering come together, and you're comfortable working across operating systems, infrastructure, cloud platforms, enterprise applications, and networks. You enjoy partnering with technical teams to implement practical security solutions while ensuring compliance with recognised industry frameworks. Most importantly, you're passionate about protecting sensitive information and using your expertise to build secure, resilient systems that make a real difference.   Ngā pūkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience Extensive experience in systems security engineering, infrastructure security, or cybersecurity within complex enterprise environments. Experience working within regulated or compliance-driven environments using frameworks such as HISF, DHSF, HITRUST CSF, ISO 27001, NIST, or equivalent. Strong knowledge of Windows, Linux, cloud platforms, networks, identity management, and secure systems administration. Experience producing security assessments, technical documentation, and implementing security controls that meet governance and assurance requirements. Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence technical teams and communicate security risks clearly. The following would be highly beneficial: Industry certifications such as CISSP, CISM, CEH, OSCP, or equivalent. Experience working within healthcare, government, or other highly regulated sectors. Experience with security governance, assurance, or audit activities.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland provides a supportive, people-centred working environment where your wellbeing and professional development are genuinely valued. As part of our team, you'll enjoy: Flexible employment practices, including hybrid working. Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution. Five weeks' annual leave. Ongoing professional development and support for industry certifications. Opportunities to work with modern cybersecurity technologies, automation platforms, and enterprise-scale systems. A collaborative team environment where innovation, continuous improvement, and knowledge sharing are encouraged. Access to staff discounts, recreation facilities, wellbeing initiatives, and discounted parking. The opportunity to help shape one of the largest cybersecurity transformation programmes within New Zealand's tertiary sector.   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 17th August 2026  to be considered. Please include a cover letter and CV outlining how your skills and experience align with the requirements of the role. For a confidential conversation, please contact Michael Karich via  michael.karich@auckland.ac.nz . \nPlease note: we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Digital Services enables the technology that powers learning, teaching, research, and the student experience across the University. Our Cybersecurity team plays a critical role in protecting the University's digital ecosystem by delivering modern security capabilities that keep our people, data, and systems secure.",
      "department": "Digital Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000138507949": {
      "title": "Senior Systems Security Engineer - ISSO",
      "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We're embarking on an exciting transformation of how Digital Services and the University approach cybersecurity. We are on a mission to rapidly and holistically uplift how Digital Services and the University approach, process, and manage patching and vulnerability management. To make this change possible, we've created a brand-new team within our Cybersecurity function dedicated to transforming how patching is delivered across our technology ecosystem through automation and modern engineering practices. As part of this investment, we are looking to appoint four Senior Systems Security Engineers to help drive this transformation. These newly created roles will identify, assess, plan, implement, and manage groups of systems, servers, and devices through newly designed workflows that fully leverage automation at every stage of the patching lifecycle. Working across cloud and on-premise environments, you'll help strengthen the University's cyber resilience while improving the efficiency, consistency, and scalability of vulnerability remediation. These opportunities are best suited to experienced engineers with broad exposure to enterprise technologies, operating systems, cloud platforms, infrastructure, applications, and endpoint management. You'll bring significant hands-on engineering experience together with the ability to solve complex technical challenges, influence stakeholders, and partner closely with Product Owners as they adopt new ways of working. If you're passionate about automation, security engineering, and building modern solutions that have a lasting impact across a large and diverse technology environment, we'd love to hear from you. Key responsibilities include: Designing, implementing, and managing automated patching solutions across the University's technology environment. Identifying, assessing, and driving the remediation of security vulnerabilities across enterprise systems, servers, and devices. Partnering with Product Owners and technology teams to develop practical remediation strategies and strengthen cyber resilience. Leveraging enterprise vulnerability management and endpoint management tools to improve visibility, reporting, and remediation outcomes. Providing technical leadership and expert advice on secure system configuration, patch management, and vulnerability management. Driving continuous improvement through automation, process optimisation, and modern security engineering practices. These are full-time, 37.5-hour-per-week roles on a permanent basis. The salary range is $110,700 - $149,800 per annum, depending on experience and qualifications. For more information, please review the Position Description.   He kōrero mōu | About you You're an experienced systems or security engineer who enjoys solving complex technical challenges and making enterprise environments more secure. You thrive in large, diverse technology environments where no two days are the same, and you're comfortable working across operating systems, cloud platforms, infrastructure, enterprise applications, and endpoint technologies. You combine deep technical expertise with a collaborative approach, enabling you to influence stakeholders, mentor others, and support teams through meaningful technical change. Most importantly, you're passionate about automation and using technology to improve security outcomes at scale. You'll enjoy working in an environment where innovation is encouraged, collaboration is valued, and your expertise will directly influence how the University manages cyber risk.   Ngā pūkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience Extensive experience in systems engineering, security engineering, vulnerability management, or enterprise patch management within complex environments. Strong hands-on experience managing Windows, Linux, cloud, and enterprise technology platforms. Experience with enterprise vulnerability management, endpoint management, automation, and scripting technologies such as PowerShell or Python. Strong understanding of cybersecurity principles, secure systems administration, and modern vulnerability management practices. Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence technical teams and support organisational change. The following would be highly beneficial: Experience working with enterprise patch management platforms such as MECM, Intune, Tanium, Ansible, or equivalent. Industry certifications such as CISSP, CISM, Microsoft, Red Hat, or equivalent. Experience working within a large, complex enterprise or higher education environment.   Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland provides a supportive, people-centred working environment where your wellbeing and professional development are genuinely valued. As part of our team, you'll enjoy: Flexible employment practices, including hybrid working. Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution. Five weeks' annual leave. Ongoing professional development and support for industry certifications. Opportunities to work with modern cybersecurity technologies, automation platforms, and enterprise-scale systems. A collaborative team environment where innovation, continuous improvement, and knowledge sharing are encouraged. Access to staff discounts, recreation facilities, wellbeing initiatives, and discounted parking. The opportunity to help shape one of the largest cybersecurity transformation programmes within New Zealand's tertiary sector.   Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 17th August 2026  to be considered. Please include a cover letter and CV outlining how your skills and experience align with the requirements of the role. For a confidential conversation, please contact Michael Karich via  michael.karich@auckland.ac.nz . \nPlease note: we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Digital Services enables the technology that powers learning, teaching, research, and the student experience across the University. Our Cybersecurity team plays a critical role in protecting the University's digital ecosystem by delivering modern security capabilities that keep our people, data, and systems secure.",
      "department": "Digital Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000096715449": {
      "title": "Genetic Pathologist - Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor",
      "description": "The Precision Medicine Initiative (PMI) and Grafton Clinical Genomics (GCG) at Waipapa Taumata Rau, University of Auckland, are seeking to appoint a Genetic Pathologist to support the clinical accreditation and delivery of next-generation sequencing (NGS) tests in both germline and somatic contexts. This role is a joint opportunity in partnership with Te Toka Tumai Auckland Hospital (Health New Zealand/Te Whatu Ora) . The Diagnostic Genetics department at Te Toka Tumai has established strong links with the University, and this position will work across both organisations to ensure integration of research, clinical service delivery, and teaching:  Job Health NZ/Te Whatu Ora .  Appointments will be made at Senior Lecturer or Associate Professor level , depending on the successful candidate’s experience and academic profile. You will: Provide clinical oversight for somatic and germline genomic testing, including test validation and sign-out. Partner with GCG to support ISO15189-accredited workflows and accreditation of new assays. Act as the clinical interface with Te Whatu Ora, contributing to patient-centred diagnostic genomics. Contribute to the academic mission of the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, including teaching, postgraduate supervision, and curriculum development. Engage in translational research through the Precision Medicine Initiative, advancing by-New-Zealand-for-New-Zealand approaches to genomics in healthcare. This is an outstanding opportunity to shape the future of genomic medicine in Aotearoa New Zealand, working at the interface of research, clinical practice, and education. Although this position is currently fixed term, there is potential opportunity for tenure with this academic position. You will be a vocationally registered Genetic Pathologist (or eligible for registration with the Medical Council of New Zealand) and hold FRCPA in Genetic Pathology (or equivalent qualification) . Essential requirements: Experience in both somatic and germline genetic pathology . Expertise in NGS test design, validation, and implementation . A strong academic track record in research and/or teaching. Ability to contribute to Molecular Tumour Boards and genetic multidisciplinary team meetings . Excellent communication and collaboration skills, with the ability to work across research, clinical, and academic environments. This is a full-time, 40 hours per week, 24-month fixed term contract. Salary range from $126,863-$158,904 for SL and $165,808 for AP. For more detailed information, please refer to the  Position Description .   Grafton Clinical Genomics (GCG) is an ISO15189-accredited genomics laboratory hosted by the University of Auckland, operating as a hybrid clinical-research platform under the Auckland Academic Health Alliance. GCG partners closely with Te Toka Tumai Auckland Hospital, ensuring alignment between academic innovation and clinical service. The Precision Medicine Initiative (PMI) is a university-led strategic programme with the goal of enabling genomics and precision medicine in New Zealand. PMI focuses on translational genomics, clinical trials, workforce capability and infrastructure. The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences is a diverse faculty with more than 4,000 students across six schools, led by internationally recognised researchers. \nThe University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities.  The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit  Staff Benefits.   \nApplications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 18 January 2026  to be considered.  Please include your cover letter, your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above and contact details of three referees. Please reach out to  Professor Peter Browett via p.browett@auckland.ac.nz  for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n We employ more than 5,400 academic and professional staff to support over 47,000 students, making us one of NZ’s largest employers. Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on both our student body and our scholarly community, and we work with both community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the peoples of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to pursuing sustainability.",
      "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000044404786": {
      "title": "Expression of Interest - Note takers - All Faculties",
      "description": "Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please apply online with a cover letter detailing, your current level of study and courses enrolled for in for the current/next semester, an example of your own notes from a class that you have recently taken and your unofficial transcript. We are looking for causal note takers for this coming semester.   #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to  www.equity.auckland.ac.nz The University is committed to providing a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment where everyone can thrive. For further information see Te Ara Tautika | the Equity Policy and visit www.equity.auckland.ac.nz   \n\n Student Disability Services requires note-takers to have demonstrable note-taking skills, excellent communication skills (written and oral) and to have a reasonable level of personal academic achievement. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity To provide the student with a disability a comprehensive set of typed notes within 24 hours of the lecture.  These notes are intended for the student to use for their own purpose of study. Content must be factual as per what is presented by the Lecturer and does not contain the authors opinion or comments of the material. Hourly rate is $25.73 per hour and you also accrue 8% holiday pay on a casual employment agreement based on hours submitted for payment. The Note taker must: Be Punctual, reliable, and attend all lectures in person (note taking from recordings are only at the discretion and prior approval from Student Disability staff). Be organised and have the ability to summarise information effectively. Respect the  privacy  of the student.  Do not disclose who you are note taking for. Type notes in a word document, any exceptions to this must be approved by Student Disability Services Students are encouraged to leave feedback on your notes and you may need to adjust your note taking to meet those requirements Provide notes that are factual and cover the verbal content given by the lecturer, not just re-typing power points or providing a transcript of the lecture. Provide notes that are accessible Clearly structured and well referenced notes are easier to read and support the students learning better than a verbatim record. The benefit to you of being a note taker is that it can improve your own note taking and be an additional skill to add to your CV. Position Description   Nga Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills To be considered for this role you need to be currently enrolled as a student at the University of Auckland. You will ideally be in your 2nd year of study or higher, and hold a GPA of 6 or greater.",
      "department": "Equity"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149235249": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Admissions Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Admissions Manager | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Admissions Manager Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55558516 UoA Department Name: Admissions Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity An opportunity has arisen to join our Admissions leadership team within Student Operations. We are recruiting 3x Admissions Managers , comprising of two permanent opportunities and one fixed-term/secondment opportunity (12-month term), each fulltime and with responsibility for a distinct admissions portfolio. As an Admissions Manager, you will provide strategic and operational leadership across a large and complex admissions function, ensuring a high-quality applicant experience while supporting the University's recruitment and enrolment objectives. You will lead high-performing teams, build strong stakeholder relationships, and drive continuous improvement across admissions services. The three portfolios are: Postgraduate and Doctoral Portfolio Postgraduate taught and research admissions Doctoral admissions Oversight of international admissions Undergraduate, Training and Quality Assurance Portfolio Undergraduate admissions and special cohorts including school leaver processing Training, quality assurance and web content Credit transfer oversight Selection and Interview Portfolio Programmes requiring interviews, selection or ranking processes including clinical selection Document processing and verification activities Oversight of Oracle Service Cloud Key responsibilities Lead and develop high-performing admissions teams to deliver excellent service outcomes. Implement operational strategies that support admissions, enrolment and conversion targets. Build and maintain collaborative relationships across faculties, service divisions and external stakeholders. Drive service enhancements, process improvements and systems optimisation initiatives. Monitor performance, analyse data and provide insights to support informed decision-making. Ensure compliance with University regulations, policies and relevant sector requirements. The salary range for this role is $98,700 – $121,900 depending on skills and experience. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Placement within the salary band will consider skills, experience, and internal equity. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are a people-centred leader who brings energy, sound judgement, and a commitment to continuous improvement. You have a natural ability to bring people together, foster collaboration, and create clarity in a fast-paced environment. Comfortable working with both detail and the bigger picture, you take a proactive approach to solving problems and driving meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience in a management role, leading a large operational team supporting high-volume transactional activity with critical deadlines, within a large, complex organisation. Demonstrable experience in the analysis of complex datasets, including reporting and providing insights. Demonstrable experience in developing and managing relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders and cross-functional teams to support maximum impact for your portfolio, high-quality delivery for internal teams, and a culture of collaboration. Confidence working with senior leaders in complex environments, beyond pure advising to a valued partnership, while managing competing priorities and projects across your collaborating partners A solid understanding of the tertiary admission landscape to translate the needs of your portfolio into actionable outcomes. Experience developing and implementing processes, systems and services. Excellent facilitation, communication and influencing skills. Proven problem-solving skills and service-oriented mindset. Curiosity, adaptability and comfort working within evolving environments. Commitment to and understanding of Te Ao Māori, and Te Tiriti o Waitangi. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 29 September and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. In your covering letter, please advise if you are interested in the permanent or fixed-term/secondment position, as well as your preferred portfolio. If you have any questions, please email Anne-Sophie Adelys via anne-sophie.adelys@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Functional Analyst Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149230349": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Functional Analyst Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Functional Analyst Manager | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Functional Analyst Manager Full-time Campus: Newmarket Campus Position Number: 55563666 UoA Department Name: Technical Analyst Services Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa’s leading university with 9,000 staff supporting more than 50,000 students to achieve their ambitions. As one of New Zealand’s most enduring brands, the University leads the way in teaching excellence and research positively impacting the lives of its people, communities and society at large. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Join Digital Services | Connect as the Functional Analyst Practice Manager, leading a large community of analysts who help support, shape and improve the University's digital products and services This is a key leadership role focused on building capability, driving best practice, and ensuring analysis services enable successful delivery across a complex and evolving technology environment. Lead and develop the Functional Analyst practice, fostering a high-performing and collaborative culture. Partner with Product Engineering, Product Managers, and business stakeholders to understand current and future capability requirements for the delivery of effective digital solutions. Champion best practice problem solving and analysis across the product and application lifecycle. Drive continuous improvement, knowledge sharing, and professional development across the practice. Provide people leadership, including coaching, performance development, and workforce planning. This position is a full-time (37.5 hours/week) permanent opportunity. For more information, please see Position Description . This role will be based at our modern offices at the Newmarket Campus, with some work across the City Campus as required. Flexible working is supported depending on business needs. He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced people leader with a strong background in analysis, technology delivery, or digital products. You bring a practical, customer-focused approach, build trusted relationships quickly, and enjoy developing both people and capability. Proven experience leading and developing large teams (20+), with at least 3+ years' experience managing sizeable functions or teams of comparable scale and complexity. Strong understanding of business and functional analysis practices across complex systems and platforms. Experience working in Agile environments and supporting successful product delivery. Excellent collaboration and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to bring people together and influence across diverse technical and non-technical audiences. A strategic thinker who can also navigate operational priorities and competing demands. Strong communication, coaching, and problem-solving skills. Relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent experience in Information Systems, Computer Science, Software Engineering, or a related field. Experience working in large, service-based organisations or higher education environments would be advantageous. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The primary objective of Connect is to provide technology services that support the University’s core functions, including Administration, Teaching, Learning, and Research, while enhancing the student experience. As part of Digital Services, the Functional Analyst practice sits within the Product Engineering Department at our Newmarket Campus. We are committed to delivering high-quality digital outcomes in an Agile IT environment. What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary ($145,000 - $160,000pa) reflecting experience with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits Additional Information Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted on our website, before Sunday 27th September, 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend Posted by Joshua Chapman share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Student Hub Assistant Auckland, New Zealand Academic Operations Team Leader Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Functional Analyst Manager Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149229440": {
      "title": "Human Health Research Coordinator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Human Health Research Coordinator | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Human Health Research Coordinator Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60021962 UoA Department Name: Liggins Institute Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. The Human Health Research Services Platform is hosted by the Liggins Institute and provides a comprehensive range of services to support human health research across the University of Auckland, including data services, statistical support, health economics, research coordination, follow-up research coordination, digital support, and clinic space and nursing support. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Human Health Research Services Platform is hosted by the Liggins Institute and provides a comprehensive range of services to support human health research across the University of Auckland, including data services, statistical support, health economics, research coordination, follow-up research coordination, digital support, and clinic space and nursing support. The Human Health Research Coordinator (HHRC) is responsible for effectively coordinating human health research studies, collaborating closely with PIs, research nurses, midwives, and other research staff. The HHRC will ensure the delivery of project objectives by coordinating all aspects of human health research activity, monitoring timelines, regulatory compliance, and reporting to the Team Lead Human Health Research Coordinator. The HHRC will also provide research support, assisting with research methods, protocols, data acquisition, report generation, participant engagement, and other tasks related to the research. This is a part time (30 hours per week), fixed term position for 12 months . The remuneration range for this role is between $68,101 - $79,300 , depending on skills and experience. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You will bring experience coordinating or supporting human health research studies, with a strong understanding of Good Clinical Practice (GCP), research ethics, and data management. You’ll be confident managing project timelines and research protocols, and have excellent communication skills - including the ability to engage warmly and professionally with study participants. You will have a relevant tertiary qualification in a field such as nursing, public health, science, allied health, social work or education, at least 1-2 years research coordination experience, and you’ll be comfortable working independently while contributing to a collaborative and multidisciplinary team. You will be able to manage multiple priorities and tasks simultaneously, work effectively under pressure, and adapt to changing demands. A high level of accuracy and ethical awareness is essential. Hands on experience with REDCap, and knowledge of nicotine/tobacco policy and/or research, would be an advantage. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 28 September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. If you have any questions about this role please get in touch with Elisa Tam for a confidential discussion via [email protected] . Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149208759": {
      "title": "Student Hub Assistant",
      "description": "University of Auckland Student Hub Assistant | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Student Hub Assistant Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 59033998 UoA Department Name: Student Hub Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university. We employ more than 5400 academic and professional staff to support over 41,000 students, making us one of NZ’s largest employers. Job Description Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Student Hubs team is the physical gateway to the Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. We provide a welcoming, friendly, and mana-enhancing service for future and current students across their student journey. At Student Hubs, our work is guided by the following values that reflect our commitment to collaboration, leadership, learning and community: Mana Enhancing: We’re a place for uplifting interaction Service Excellence: We’re professional, accurate and responsive Manaakitanga: We listen, respect, understand and care Whanaungatanga: We connect, partner and strengthen relationships Kaitiakitanga: We guide, support and see things through We deliver a wide range of services to support students on their journey at Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. Our primary function is to deliver high-quality in-person and online services that support students holistically. Our services include: guidance and advice on being a student navigating the University systems, spaces and processes exploring study options application and enrolment support Library & Student IT services He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for highly motivated individuals to join our team of Student Hub Assistants who: Are collaborative team players who build positive relationships and contribute to a supportive team environment Take a proactive approach and work effectively both independently and with others Demonstrate reliability and a strong, positive work ethic aligned with our values Show empathy and genuine care in supporting tauira | students and colleagues Thrive in fast-paced, dynamic environments and adapt to changing needs Bring a positive, energetic approach to engaging with tauira | students in a high-volume, complex work setting Work Patterns: Location: City Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Saturday and Sunday 9am–5pm and Monday - Wednesday 8am–4pm - Fixed term - from 9 Nov 2026 – 1 Sep 2027 Part Time, 24 hours per week - Thursday 2pm–10pm, Friday, Saturday and Sunday 2pm–8pm Start date - 9 Nov 2026 - Permanent Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Monday - Friday 12pm–8pm - Fixed term - 9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Monday - Friday 10am–6pm - Fixed term - 9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Monday - Friday 11am–7pm - Fixed term - 9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Part Time, 20 hours per week - Monday - Friday 10am–2pm  - Fixed term -.9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Location: Grafton Part Time, 20 hours per week - Monday- Friday 10am–2pm - Fixed Term -.09 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 We also have a number of casual roles available. The salary range is $55,600-$57,000 per annum (pro-rated for part-time positions), depending on experience and qualifications. We will also be recruiting for casual staff to support our peak periods across all our Student Hub sites, so if you are a current University of Auckland student, this may be a great opportunity for you. For more detailed information on the role responsibilities and requirements, please review this Position Description. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply If you are strong team player who is excited about working with people and passionate about leading, then we want to hear from you! Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 24/9/26 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the abovementioned skills and experiences. Note that we may close the advert earlier if we find a suitable candidate. If you have questions or would like a confidential conversation, please reach out to the hiring team via [email protected] . Please note we are happy to answer questions, but we do not accept applications by email . Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149206129": {
      "title": "Timetabling and Enrolment Coordinator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Timetabling and Enrolment Coordinator | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Timetabling and Enrolment Coordinator Contract Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021998 UoA Department Name: Enrolment Experience Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for two Timetabling and Enrolment Coordinator to join our Student and Scholarly Services team and help deliver a smooth, supportive and student-focused enrolment experience. This role offers the chance to work at the heart of the student journey, supporting how classes are timetabled, rooms are allocated, and enrolments are managed across the University. You will work with detailed information and systems, collaborate with colleagues across faculties and service teams, and play an important part in ensuring students can confidently navigate their academic pathway. If you enjoy bringing order to complexity, working with systems and data, and being part of a team that values collaboration and continuous improvement, this could be a great fit. These roles are full-time, 37.5 hours per week. one is permanent and the other on a 16-month fixed term contract. The salary range for this role is $63,600 - $79,300 per annum depending one experience and qualifications. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Timetabling and Enrolment team is part of Student and Scholarly Services and plays a vital role in supporting students at key moments in their academic experience. The team is known for its collaborative, supportive culture and its commitment to delivering accurate, reliable, and student-centred services. You will join a team that values shared learning, open communication, and taking pride in work that directly supports student success. He kōrero mōu | About you You are organised, detail-focused, and enjoy working behind the scenes to make things run smoothly. You bring experience in administrative coordination and feel comfortable working with systems, data, and multiple priorities. You will bring: Experience in an administrative or coordination role, ideally within a large or complex organisation Confidence working with databases, systems, or scheduling tools, with strong attention to accuracy The ability to manage competing deadlines while remaining calm and solutions-focused Clear and friendly communication skills, and a collaborative approach to working with others A genuine commitment to providing high-quality service and supporting positive student experiences Experience in a tertiary environment, or with timetabling or enrolment processes, is helpful but not essential -  training and support will be provided. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer At the University of Auckland, we want our people to feel supported and able to thrive. We offer: Flexible working arrangements, including flexible hours and working from home options Five weeks’ annual leave Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Ongoing learning and professional development opportunities You’ll also have access to discounted parking, generous parental leave, childcare options, and a wide range of staff discounts and wellbeing benefits. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 27th September 2026 be considered.  Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. We may close this advert early if we have a large number of applications. Please get in touch with (Team Lead -Timetabling & Enrolments) Liana Ball via [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149204870": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Exam Supervisor",
      "description": "University of Auckland Exam Supervisor - Special | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Exam Supervisor - Special Casual Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 59022525 UoA Department Name: Student and Academic Services Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers. Job Description The opportunity | Te Whiwhinga mahi We are seeking to recruit exam room invigilators to provide supervision during end of semester exams, for students who have approved Special Conditions. These are students who have either a: Temporary disability Permanent disability Condition that affects their ability to sit exams under the prescribed conditions. You will be responsible for supervising and ensuring compliance during exam sessions to ensure the security and integrity of the exam environment and you will play an integral role in assisting students during a particularly stressful part of their studies. You will hold integrity in high regard, pay close attention to detail and be able to remain vigilant for prolonged periods. You will be able to adapt to mixed modes of exam delivery, including on campus digital exams, traditional paper-based exams and remote, online invigilation. You will be responsible for: Setting up the exam room by placing exam papers, stationery and equipment at desks in accordance with procedures and spacing requirements. Implementing exam rules and regulations and remaining attentive throughout the exam, monitoring student behaviour. Assisting before, during and after the exam by directing students to their seats, advising them about possessions permitted in the exam room, and handling queries raised. Supervising carefully, making sure that students do not communicate with one another during the exam, and responding to any irregularities immediately. Checking attendance during exams and recording details of late arrivals. Escorting candidates on washroom breaks as required. Detecting any unauthorised materials inside the exam room and ensuring that academic integrity is upheld at all times. Delivering and collecting exam scripts carefully at the start and end of the exam in accordance with procedures. Be able to adapt to different modes of exams throughout the day and/or exam period. Support exam processes You will need to be available as needed throughout the exam period. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 22/9/2026 to be considered. However, do not wait to submit your application as we may begin hiring sooner. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Anjali Patel - patel.anjali@auckland.ac.nz for more information. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Student Hub Assistant Auckland, New Zealand Timetabling and Enrolment Coordinator Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Exam Supervisor - Special Auckland, New Zealand Casual I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149204189": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Academic Operations Team Leader",
      "description": "University of Auckland Academic Operations Team Leader | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Academic Operations Team Leader Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021971 UoA Department Name: Group Services Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity An exciting opportunity has arisen for a capable, collaborative and people-centred leader to join the Faculty of Law as Academic Operations Team Leader. Working closely with the Academic Operations Manager, this role will provide both visible day-to-day leadership and practical operational support to ensure the Business and Law Group Services team delivers high-quality, responsive and consistent academic and administrative services. This is a hands-on leadership role within a team of 26 staff, with direct responsibility for four team members: one Group Services Administrator, two Academic Administration Coordinators and one Group Services Coordinator. The successful candidate will set clear expectations, support performance, coach and develop staff, coordinate workload, resolve operational issues and step in where needed to maintain service continuity during business-as-usual activity, periods of increased demand and times of change. The Academic Operations Team Leader will play an important role in strengthening Academic Operations / Group Services capability across the Faculty. The role requires someone who can lead through influence as well as action: supporting change, embedding improved ways of working, contributing to new initiatives and ensuring practical, consistent approaches are translated into effective service delivery for schools across both faculties, including teaching and learning, research administration and operational activity. We are seeking someone who brings sound administrative or coordination experience, strong judgement and a genuine commitment to people leadership, service excellence and continuous improvement. The ideal candidate will be approachable, resilient and solutions-focused, with the ability to balance strategic awareness with practical delivery. They will be confident leading people, managing competing priorities, building trust with stakeholders and working alongside the team to support high-quality outcomes. Provide confident, empathetic and visible people leadership, setting clear expectations while coaching, motivating and supporting staff to perform at their best. Take a hands-on approach to operational delivery, stepping in to support work, resolve issues and maintain service continuity when priorities shift or demand increases. Plan, prioritise and coordinate work effectively, using sound judgement to balance competing demands, allocate resources and meet agreed service expectations. Build trusted and constructive relationships with staff, students and schools, bringing a professional, customer-focused and solution-oriented approach to service delivery. Lead and contribute to continuous improvement by identifying opportunities to simplify processes, improve consistency and support positive change across the team. This is a fulltime, permanent position. The salary range for this role is $73,400 – $90,700 depending on skills and experience . For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are a proactive and collaborative leader with experience in tertiary operations or academic administration. You thrive in a dynamic environment, guiding teams to deliver efficient, consistent, and student- and staff-centred administrative services, and are confident in managing complex operational challenges while supporting strategic priorities. You will bring: A Bachelor’s degree (postgraduate qualification desirable). Proven experience leading and developing teams in a tertiary or educational environment. Demonstrated experience in academic operations, administration, or service delivery, with an understanding of key functional areas such as finance, recruitment, curriculum management, or teaching & learning administration. Ability to build strong relationships, influence outcomes, and act as a point of escalation for complex operational challenges. Strong problem-solving and organisational skills, with experience contributing to operational planning and continuous improvement initiatives. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 4 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Chrissy Bretherton via c.bretherton@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Functional Analyst Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Academic Operations Team Leader Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148918709": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Assessment Services Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Assessment Services Manager | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Assessment Services Manager Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 00014278 UoA Department Name: Student and Academic Services Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an experienced leader to join Student and Scholarly Services as our Assessment Services Manager. This pivotal role leads the University’s examination operations, ensuring high-quality, equitable and future-focused assessment services that support student success and academic excellence. Working across a diverse portfolio that includes examinations, assessment equity, academic integrity, student discipline processes and digital assessment platforms, you will partner with academic and professional leaders to drive innovation, continuous improvement and service excellence. This is a unique opportunity to influence institutional assessment strategy while leading a high-performing team in one of New Zealand’s leading universities. Key responsibilities: Lead the delivery and continuous improvement of the University’s centrally managed exam and assessment services. Provide strategic leadership for assessment operations, systems and technologies, including the University’s digital assessment platform. Lead and develop a high-performing team while fostering a collaborative and inclusive culture. Partner with senior academic and professional leaders to support learning and teaching priorities. Drive assessment transformation initiatives, service enhancements and operational excellence. Manage strategic projects, risk planning and business continuity activities across the portfolio. Build strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders to enhance assessment practices and services. This is a fulltime, permanent position . The salary range for this role is $114,400 - $140,000 depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are a people-focused leader with significant experience leading operational teams within a large and complex organisation. You enjoy building relationships, influencing stakeholders and creating a strong supportive culture and environment where teams can thrive. You will bring excellent communication and analytical skills, demonstrated experience leading change and continuous improvement initiatives, and a commitment to delivering exceptional service outcomes. An understanding of learning, assessment, equity and student-centred service delivery will enable you to make an immediate impact in this role. You'll also value te ao Māori, diversity and inclusion, and be motivated by the opportunity to contribute to meaningful outcomes for both tauira and staff. Success in this role will require a balance of strategic thinking and strong leadership. You will lead a team of experienced professionals, navigate a complex environment and ensure University priorities are delivered through strong planning, collaboration and execution. Our ideal candidate will bring: Significant leadership experience within a large and complex organisation. Experience leading high-volume operational services. Experience leading service, systems and process improvement initiatives. Strong stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills at all levels of the organisation. Demonstrated ability to analyse complex data and provide strategic insights. Experience leading teams through transformation and change. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 25 September 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Mel Cross via mel.cross@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Assessment Services Manager Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148878479": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Student Support Advisor",
      "description": "University of Auckland Student Support Advisor | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Student Support Advisor Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60017198 UoA Department Name: Te Kupenga Hauora Maori Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences is a diverse faculty with more than 4,000 students studying across six schools, led by world-leading researchers, teachers, and practising health professionals.  Our Vision is to lead and advance Hauora across generations in Aotearoa courageously.  As such, we are committed to leading and advancing the mana of Aotearoa as a global centre of Health learning, research, and innovation. Te Kupenga Hauora Māori (TKHM) is located at the Grafton Campus. TKHM represents the Department of Māori Health and is responsible for the coordination of Māori health teaching across the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, the Vision 20:20 initiative responsible for Indigenous health workforce development, and the Tōmaiora Research Unit is responsible for conducting Kaupapa Māori health research and training services. Located on the Grafton Campus, the school is fully self-contained, in a purpose-built building. The building has been designed to maximise interaction and collaboration. Our knowledge, expertise and teaching are our taonga. We value our students, alumni and donors. We work closely with partners such as Te Whatu Ora | Health NZ and Te Aka Whai Ora | Māori Health Authority, as well as communities, organisations, industry and international partners to advance the well-being of the people of Aotearoa, with a clear commitment to sustainably regenerate people & place. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | About the Role To fulfil our commitment to the Vision 20:20 initiative, which aims to increase the number of Māori and Pacific health professionals through the Māori and Pacific Admission Scheme (MAPAS). TKHM seeks to recruit an inspiring individual to support our Māori and Pacific students through their cultural and educational journey within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences (FMHS) as the Student Support Advisor The kaupapa of this role is: coordinating academic and pastoral support for Māori and Pacific students who are studying in either foundation, undergraduate or postgraduate programmes responsible for the administration, management and coordination of admission, academic, and pastoral support for our students. This is an excellent opportunity to undertake responsibility and be innovative in a stimulating position that demands critical thinking as well as a practical approach. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team Our team pride themselves on our positive working environment, where we work collaboratively to develop innovative solutions. We work closely and are always committed to giving and accepting feedback to ensure we provide the best service possible to our students. He kōrero mōu | About you The successful candidate will have an well-developed/in-depth understanding of Māori and Pacific culture to provide support, advice and mentorship to students throughout their journey with us and will have strong connections to Māori and/or Pacific communities. In addition, you will: Understand the challenges Māori and Pacific students encounter while studying at university Have a relevant undergraduate degree Be able to work independently as well as within a team Have a high competency in administration and communication skills Have a demonstrated ability to build effective relationships Have an ability to coordinate and manage events Be able to collaborate and work in partnership with key academic staff Have excellent time management and organisational skills Be flexible and able to work evenings and weekends This position is fulltime , fixed term for 18 months and the salary range is $63,600 - $79,300 (pro rata), dependent on skills and experience. For further information please refer to the Position Description Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 26/09/2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the above-mentioned skills and experiences. Please reach out to Sue Reddy via sue.reddy@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note that we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Student Support Advisor Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148698754": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Performance and Digital",
      "description": "University of Auckland Associate Director, Performance and Digital | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Associate Director, Performance and Digital Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019615 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Job Description Auckland | Permanent | Full-time Hybrid working environment (mix of on-site / work from home) Marketing & Recruitment, Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland $134,900 - $202,500 per annum (Band J), based on experience Own the strategy, governance and performance of a University-wide digital and performance marketing capability Purpose over products – ours are creating knowledge, transforming lives, shaping futures Balance ambitious work with flexibility - hybrid work, 37.5-hour week, and inclusive benefits that recognise life beyond work Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is a senior leadership role for someone who can set the strategic and operational agenda for performance marketing and digital at university scale. Someone who still wants to be close enough to the work to know how it actually gets delivered. Reporting to the Director of Marketing and Recruitment, you'll set the strategic direction, governance and operating framework for the University's performance marketing and digital capability, and lead three specialist managers; Web, Digital and Content, and Digital Marketing, through to delivery. You'll own significant media and technology budgets, shape a connected digital experience across every channel and touchpoint in the prospective student journey, and manage the agency and platform partnerships that support it. You'll be part of the Marketing and Recruitment senior leadership team, sharing accountability for outcomes that go beyond your own portfolio; driving the digital and performance maturity of the wider University. Find out more about our reimagining of digital, web and performance, and our broader Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions. Key areas of focus include: Setting and leading the University's performance marketing strategy across all paid digital channels Driving acquisition, conversion and pipeline quality across the full student recruitment funnel Providing strategic leadership across web, digital platforms and marketing automation Building advanced measurement, attribution and ROI frameworks, and translating them into decisions senior stakeholders can act on Establishing enterprise-level governance for digital and performance investment, risk and compliance Leading and growing a high-performing team, and lifting digital capability and ways of working across the University Running experimentation, CRO and customer-journey optimisation programmes Managing agency and partnerships while building in-house capability over time Championing data-informed decision-making and performance transparency across the function This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $134,900 - $202,500 per annum (Band J). We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Salary decisions for individual appointments are made based on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you You could be leading performance marketing, digital or growth for a large, complex organisation, or leading a portfolio of clients at an agency. Either way, you understand how modern acquisition ecosystems work end to end, and you know how to lead the people and functions that run them. You've likely built acquisition programmes from the ground up, scaled marketing technology platforms, led digital optimisation initiatives, or grown high-performing performance marketing teams. What matters most is that you understand how digital performance actually works, and that you can build a function that's sustainable, ethical and built to keep improving. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Significant experience leading performance marketing at scale, in a large or complex organisation Deep expertise across paid search, paid social, programmatic and digital acquisition Experience owning a marketing technology, automation and analytics stack Strong commercial and analytical judgement A track record of leading digital transformation and capability uplift Proven people leadership, including through change A track record of using data to sharpen marketing performance and investment decisions A genuine appetite for continuous improvement and innovation Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Few organisations can offer the scale, complexity and opportunity available in this role. This is a chance to join at the beginning of a significant transformation and shape the future of digital marketing for one of New Zealand's most recognised organisations. You'll have the opportunity to influence strategy, technology, capability and ways of working while building a function designed for long-term success. For the right person, this is an opportunity to achieve in a few years what may take considerably longer within a more mature organisation. You'll help define the future state, build capability, develop talent and leave a lasting impact on an organisation that influences thousands of people every day. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of Sunday 27th September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Faith Pallant, Recruitment Manager on [email protected] with any questions. Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148676639": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Student Support Advisor (Pacific)",
      "description": "University of Auckland Student Support Advisor (Pacific) | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Student Support Advisor (Pacific) Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55567920 UoA Department Name: Student Support and Engagement Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa  New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. The Faculty of Law is internationally recognised for excellence in legal education and research and is committed to supporting staff to achieve their full potential. Joining our team means contributing to meaningful outcomes for students while developing your own career in a supportive environment. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Faculty of Law is seeking a Student Support Adviser - Pacific to join our dedicated student support team. This is a rewarding opportunity to make a meaningful difference by supporting Pacific students throughout their academic journey, providing guidance, advocacy, and pastoral care that helps students thrive both personally and academically. In this highly relationship focused role, you will work directly with students in the Pacific community, building trusted relationships and providing tailored support to enhance engagement, retention, wellbeing, and academic achievement. You will work with students who may be experiencing challenges, connect them with appropriate support services, and help create an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Key responsibilities include: Providing one-to-one advice, guidance, and pastoral care to Pacific students. Supporting initiatives that improve Pacific student recruitment, retention, engagement, and success. Working closely with academic staff, Course Directors, student support services, and Pacific student networks across the University. Supporting the Faculty's Moana Oceanic initiative and Undergraduate Targeted admissions (UTA) Scheme. Coordinating appointments, responding to student enquiries, maintaining accurate records, and supporting student-focused events and activities. This is a permanent, full-time role (37.5 hours per week) position. This is a salary range for this position is $63,600 to $79,300 depending on skills and experience. This position offers a fantastic opportunity to develop your experience in higher education while contributing directly to Pacific student success within one of New Zealand's leading law schools.  You will also have the opportunity to learn from and work alongside Pacific leaders and advocates who are committed to fostering excellence and belonging. For more detailed information on the role, please review the position description . Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team You'll be joining a collaborative and supportive team committed to student success.  Based within the Faculty of Law, this role sits within a professional services team that works across both the Faculty of Law and the Faculty of Business and Economics, creating opportunities to build relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders and contribute to a connected student experience.  We are committed to fostering an inclusive environment where Pacific students feel supported, connected, and empowered to achieve their goals. He kōrero mōu | About you You are passionate about supporting Pacific learners and enjoy building meaningful relationships that help others succeed. You bring empathy, strong communication skills, and the confidence to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds. To be successful in this role, you will have: A tertiary qualification and/or relevant experience working in student support, Pacific support services, community engagement, education, or a related field. Experience providing pastoral care, mentoring, advising, or wellbeing support. An understanding of equity and the factors that contribute to Pacific student success. The ability to build effective relationships with students, whānau, academic staff, and professional colleagues. Strong organisational and administrative skills, including managing appointments, coordinating activities, and maintaining accurate records. Strong computer literacy, including confidence using Microsoft Office applications and Excel. The ability to manage competing priorities in a busy and sometimes high-pressure environment. Knowledge of Pasifika values, and experience working with Pacific communities or students would be highly advantageous. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home up to one day per week during peak periods and up to two days during quieter periods, where operational requirements allow) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme. A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave. In addition, we offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 18 September to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how your skills and experience align with this opportunity For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Ashley Flavell on a.flavell@auckland.ac.nz .  Please note that whilst we are happy to answer questions, we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Student Support Advisor Auckland, New Zealand Media & Communications Partnering Lead (Education, Faculty) Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Student Support Advisor (Pacific) Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148668358": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Research Portfolio Lead",
      "description": "University of Auckland Research Portfolio Lead – University Research Centres x 2  | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Research Portfolio Lead – University Research Centres x 2 Full-time Campus: Newmarket Campus Position Number: 60020445 UoA Department Name: Research Operations, Ethics, and Integrity Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Join a newly established portfolio management team to help shape the future of the Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland’s leading research centres. Working alongside the Directors of the University's Research Centres, you will help translate strategic ambition into well-governed, high-performing programmes of work. As an experienced programme or project leader, you will provide expert portfolio management, drive strategic planning and implementation, strengthen governance and reporting, and enable the successful delivery of complex research initiatives with significant institutional and external impact. As Research Portfolio Lead, you will be responsible for: Overseeing the administrative and financial management of University Research Centres, and maintain oversight of performance, risk and opportunities. Working with Directors to develop the URC Forward Plans, and to implement these. Ensuring budgets are compliant and monitored throughout contract lifecycle, and provide forecasting to support decision making. Working closely with the Directors and Academic Leads to coordinate the preparation, consolidation and submission of reports, briefings and governance papers. Identification of risks and work with URC Directors on mitigation planning. Liaising with internal stakeholders to ensure seamless delivery Embedding cultural safety and protocols within interactions with Māori and Pacific stakeholders For more information, please view the position description. We have two fixed-term opportunities available until 31 December 2028: one full-time (37.5 hours per week, 1.0 FTE) and one part-time (18.75 hours per week, 0.5 FTE). Salary: Band G – $87,000 to $107,500 per annum depending on skills, experience and qualifications. Location: Depending on the successful candidate's area of expertise and the portfolio they will support, this role may be based at either the University of Auckland's City Campus or Grafton Campus. The successful candidate will also be expected to spend regular time with the Research & Innovation Office (RIO) team at the Newmarket Campus to support collaboration and programme delivery. He kōrero mōu | About you Experienced in managing complex projects, ideally within a university, research or public sector environment Exceptional project management proficiency, ability to identify and manage risks as they arise Ability to synthesise information in the development and review of high-level documentation and produce effective reports. Working knowledge of research management systems and processes within a University or similar research-intensive organisation. Good teamwork abilities, particularly in a multicultural and multi-disciplinary context. Excellent organisational skills and attention to detail. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team Reporting to the Senior Manager – Research Services Improvement, you will be part of a team providing process improvement and post-award support to strategic initiatives at the University. Additional Information What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou We offer you more than just a job. We offer you the opportunity to be part of a dynamic, world-class educational institution. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits, including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave. Superannuation/KiwiSaver matching up to 6.75% Leading and inclusive parental leave options. Ongoing professional development opportunities and access to a range of discounted on campus services and amenitites. Hear more about our partnership with Māori, our Te Ao Māori principles , our impact, our staff, and life in Auckland on our career’s website . For further information please visit our staff benefits page. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Tuesday 22nd of September 2026 (NZST) to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the above-mentioned skills and experiences and indicate whether you are applying for the full-time position, the part-time position, or would like to be considered for both opportunities. Please reach out to Carmella Lee (Hiring Manager) at carmella.lee@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Research Engagement Manager - 4 positions (Aligned to the University's Signature Research Areas) Auckland, New Zealand Innovation Hub and Operations Specialist Auckland, New Zealand Research Grants and Contracts Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Research Portfolio Lead – University Research Centres x 2 Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
