    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148611545": {
      "title": "Innovation Hub and Operations Specialist",
      "description": "University of Auckland Innovation Hub and Operations Specialist | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Innovation Hub and Operations Specialist Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021688 UoA Department Name: Innovation & Entrepreneurship Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is a varied, hands-on 12-month fixed-term opportunity to play an important role in the day-to-day operation of the Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE), while contributing to a key area of development over the coming year. As Innovation Hub & Operations Specialist, you’ll help ensure CIE’s innovation hubs, operational systems and activities run effectively and provide a great experience for the diverse communities who use our spaces. You’ll work across the Centre, supporting the operational environment that enables our programmes, curricular teaching, workshops, events, stakeholder activities and wider innovation community to thrive. During this parental leave cover, a particular focus will be the continued development of CIE’s data and reporting capability. CIE has established data systems and dashboards, and you’ll help us improve and evolve these, strengthening the quality and usefulness of our data and ensuring our approaches develop alongside wider University systems and practices. The role is broad and practical, spanning innovation hub operations, systems and processes, programme and event logistics, data and reporting, and continuous improvement. You’ll need to be comfortable moving between detailed work and practical problem-solving in a busy environment, building strong relationships and finding better ways to get things done. Key responsibilities include: Support the effective day-to-day operation and utilisation of CIE’s innovation hubs, including Unleash Space. Coordinate operational systems, resources and logistics that enable CIE programmes, curricular teaching, workshops, events and stakeholder activities to run effectively. Identify and implement improvements to operational processes, systems and ways of working. Support the ongoing improvement of CIE’s existing data systems, dashboards and reporting approaches. Maintain and improve the quality, consistency and accessibility of data across CIE activities. Produce and enhance reporting and dashboards that support planning, decision-making and understanding of CIE’s reach and performance. Work with CIE colleagues and relevant University teams to ensure data and reporting approaches are practical, sustainable and appropriately aligned with wider University practices. Build effective working relationships across CIE and with University service teams, suppliers and other stakeholders. Contribute to a collaborative, responsive and entrepreneurial operating culture across CIE. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), 12-month fixed-term role covering Parental Leave.  It is a band G position with a salary range from $87,000 to $107,500 pa depending on skills, experience and qualifications. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is Waipapa Taumata Rau’s platform for developing the innovators, entrepreneurs and changemakers of the future. We work across the University to help students, researchers and staff build innovation and entrepreneurial capability, turn ideas into action, develop ventures, and create social, cultural, environmental and economic impact for Aotearoa and the world. CIE is entering an exciting new chapter. With an ambitious new strategy, we are significantly increasing the reach and impact of innovation and entrepreneurship across the University, helping realise Waipapa Taumata Rau’s ambition to be a global powerhouse of innovation, creativity and entrepreneurship, and strengthening the University’s contribution to a more innovative and prosperous Aotearoa. We are building a team of people who are curious, collaborative, ambitious for impact and committed to turning ambition into action. If you want to help shape how a world-class research-led university develops innovative and entrepreneurial people, we would love to hear from you. He kōrero mōu | About you You’re a practical, organised and proactive person who enjoys making things work well. You’re comfortable working in a busy and varied environment, can move easily between hands-on operational responsibilities and more detailed systems and data work, and enjoy finding better ways of doing things. You’ll bring strong operational and coordination skills, alongside confidence working with data, dashboards and reporting. You’ll be comfortable improving how information is captured and organised, analysing data and translating it into clear and useful insights that support decision-making. You’ll also be confident working with a wide range of people. CIE’s innovation hubs host everything from programmes and curricular teaching to workshops, events and senior stakeholder activities, so you’ll be responsive, professional and comfortable adapting to different needs and situations. You’ll bring: Experience in operations, project coordination, programme support or a similar role within a busy and complex environment. Strong organisational and problem-solving skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and follow work through to completion. Experience working with data and reporting, including analysing information, identifying patterns and translating data into clear and useful insights. Strong digital capability, including advanced Excel skills and experience developing and improving data visualisation, dashboards and reporting tools. Experience improving systems, processes or ways of working, with an eye for both efficiency and user experience. Strong interpersonal and relationship-management skills, with confidence working with people at different levels across an organisation. A collaborative, service-oriented approach and willingness to contribute wherever needed in a varied, hands-on role. Strong attention to detail and a commitment to maintaining accurate, reliable information and systems. The ability to work independently, use sound judgement and respond constructively when priorities or circumstances change. A relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent professional experience. Experience within a tertiary education, innovation or similarly complex organisational environment would be advantageous. The nature of CIE’s programmes and events means the role requires flexibility to work outside normal business hours, including regular evening work and occasional weekend work. Additional Information What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland - is New Zealand's leading university and offers an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices Competitive employer superannuation contribution Five weeks' annual leave pro rate Career development and professional learning opportunities Excellent campus facilities Access to a range of staff benefits, wellbeing initiatives, and discounts For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 27th of September 2026 to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter highlighting how you meet the skills and experience outlined above, what attracts you to this opportunity, and what you would bring to CIE, particularly in strengthening our operational, data and reporting capability. Please reach out to (Kimberley Ansell) at [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148353287": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Assessment Integrity Support",
      "description": "University of Auckland Assessment Integrity Support  | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Assessment Integrity Support Casual Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021953 UoA Department Name: Student and Academic Services Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking two Casual Assessment Integrity Support staff members to join our team within Assessment Services to support one of the University's busiest assessment periods. Working as part of a small and dedicated team, you will play an important role in supporting the University's assessment integrity processes. You will review recordings and supporting data from online examinations, identify potential breaches of examination rules and procedures, and prepare accurate, objective reports for further review. This role requires a high level of attention to detail, professionalism, and discretion. You will work with large volumes of information and sensitive data, ensuring that established procedures and guidelines are applied consistently while maintaining confidentiality at all times. This is an excellent opportunity for individuals who enjoy analytical work, can exercise sound judgement, and are comfortable working independently in a fast-paced environment. This is a casual position designed to support short-term operational requirements. Hours will vary based on business needs and will be agreed with the successful candidates. The hourly rate for this role is $29 per hour. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team Assessment Services plays a critical role in supporting assessment delivery and academic integrity across the University. Our team works closely with staff and students to ensure assessment processes are fair, consistent, and aligned with University regulations and procedures. We are committed to upholding the integrity of the University's assessment framework while providing high-quality support services that contribute to student success and academic excellence. He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a highly organised and detail-oriented individual with strong analytical and written communication skills. To be successful in this role, you will bring: Excellent attention to detail and analytical skills. Strong written communication and report-writing abilities. Confidence working with digital systems and technology. The ability to handle sensitive and confidential information with professionalism and discretion. Strong judgement and decision-making capabilities. The ability to quickly learn new information and processes. Excellent organisational skills and the ability to manage competing priorities. The ability to work independently while maintaining accuracy and consistency. A commitment to following established procedures and maintaining high standards of quality. Previous experience reviewing documentation, conducting investigations, quality assurance activities, compliance work, or administrative support would be advantageous but is not essential. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment and meaningful opportunities to contribute to the success of New Zealand's leading university. We offer: Flexible casual work based on operational requirements. A supportive and collaborative team environment. Valuable experience within a large and complex organisation. The opportunity to contribute to maintaining academic integrity and assessment quality. Ongoing learning and development within a professional higher education environment. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the 15th of September 2026 to be considered. Please include a current CV and any supporting information outlining your suitability for the role. For more information, please contact Freya McCourt - freya.mccourt@auckland.ac.nz Please note that we may close this advertisement early if suitable candidates are identified. Applications sent directly by email will not be accepted. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Exam Supervisor - Special Auckland, New Zealand Assessment Services Manager Auckland, New Zealand Inspera Casual Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Assessment Integrity Support Auckland, New Zealand Casual I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148331399": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Full Stack Developer",
      "description": "University of Auckland Full Stack Developer | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Full Stack Developer Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022002 UoA Department Name: Liggins Institute Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and health researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Join the Human Health Research Services Platform at the Liggins Institute and help shape innovative digital solutions that support world-leading health research. As a Full Stack Developer, you will play a key role in designing, developing, testing and supporting secure, scalable web, mobile and cloud-based applications that enable researchers to make a meaningful impact. Working across the full software development lifecycle, you will collaborate with a diverse range of stakeholders to deliver high-quality digital products, contribute to continuous improvement initiatives, and help ensure applications are secure, accessible and fit for purpose Key responsibilities: Develop and maintain websites, web applications, Progressive Web Apps (PWAs), mobile applications and AWS cloud-based solutions. Design and build RESTful APIs and integrations with third-party systems. Implement responsive, accessible and user-friendly interfaces. Support software testing, troubleshooting and defect resolution activities. Ensure applications meet quality, security and accessibility standards. Produce clear technical documentation and user guides. Collaborate with project stakeholders and contribute to continuous improvement initiatives. This is a fulltime position for fixed term of 24 months The salary range for this role are $87,000 - $107,500 depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are a capable developer who enjoys solving complex problems and creating technology that delivers real-world impact. You thrive in a collaborative environment, communicate effectively with both technical and non-technical stakeholders, and bring a strong commitment to quality, security and continuous learning. You will be comfortable working across both front-end and back-end development, enjoy taking ownership of your work, and be motivated by the opportunity to contribute to innovative health and research-focused projects. Our ideal candidate will bring: 3 to 5 years' experience in a Full Stack Development role. Strong experience with JavaScript/TypeScript, HTML and CSS. Experience with modern frameworks such as React or similar. Knowledge of cloud-based development, ideally within AWS environments. Experience with relational databases, SQL and RESTful APIs. Familiarity with Agile development methodologies and version control tools such as Git. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Human Health Research Services Platform is hosted by the Liggins Institute and provides a comprehensive range of services to support human health research across the University of Auckland and beyond, including data services, statistical support, health economics, research coordination, follow-up research coordination, digital support, and clinic space and nursing support Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 23 September 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Elaine Umali via e.umali@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Functional Analyst Manager Auckland, New Zealand Student Hub Assistant Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Full Stack Developer Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148151408": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "University of Auckland Research Fellow - Department of Physics | Te Kura Mātai Ahupūngao – fixed term - 26 months  | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Research Fellow - Department of Physics | Te Kura Mātai Ahupūngao – fixed term - 26 months Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021677 UoA Department Name: Physics Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science at the University of Auckland is the leading science faculty in New Zealand and one of the largest in Australasia. It undertakes world-class research across the physical, biological and computational sciences. More than 8,000 students' study in our 10 schools and departments, learning from internationally renowned lecturers. We have excellent library and computing facilities. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tuākana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Department of Physics is seeking a Research Fellow to lead the operational research programme at Taiaho Observatory, the University of Auckland’s free-space optical communications ground station at Ardmore Field Station. Taiaho Observatory combines a fast-tracking 30-centimetre DLR telescope with the DLR Simple Optical Ground Station Focaloptics Assembly, together with supporting control, environmental-monitoring and data-acquisition systems. It has been developed to acquire and track satellites and receive laser-based communications from space. The immediate priority for this position is to maximise the number and scientific value of satellite optical downlink opportunities undertaken from Taiaho. The successful candidate will become a principal operator of the Observatory, maintain campaign readiness, prepare for suitable satellite passes, conduct time-critical observations and analyse the resulting communications links. The Research Fellow will also coordinate increasingly sophisticated experiments with other members of the Australasian Optical Ground Station Network. These activities will progress from rehearsals and single-station observations to synchronised multi-site campaigns and network demonstrations involving researchers and ground-station operators in Australia and New Zealand. This operational work supports the wider Phase B objective of moving the AOGSN from independently operated facilities towards a coordinated and interoperable optical ground-station network. The position has a strong experimental and operational focus. It is not primarily a software-development role, although the successful candidate will be expected to use scientific analysis software, undertake modest scripting where necessary, and communicate operational requirements to software and engineering collaborators. Ngā Haepapa Matua | Key Responsibilities (not limited to): Operating the Taiaho telescope, receiver instrumentation and associated observatory systems. Maintaining operational readiness through calibration, testing, rehearsals, checklists and documented procedures. Working with satellite mission teams and research partners to identify and assess potential optical downlink opportunities. Planning and conducting satellite-pass campaigns, including pre-pass preparation, real-time operation, data capture and post-pass review. Coordinating experimental activities with researchers and operators at other AOGSN node sites. Analysing acquisition and tracking performance, received optical power, signal-to-noise ratio, link availability, throughput, packet or frame loss and bit error rate. Relating measured link performance to observing geometry, atmospheric conditions, telescope performance and receiver configuration. Producing pass reports, technical reports, research publications and presentations. Developing and refining common operational procedures for coordinated optical ground-station experiments. Training students and additional Observatory operators. The work will include regular activity at Ardmore Field Station. Satellite passes do not always occur during standard working hours, and the successful candidate must therefore be willing to undertake occasional evening, night, early-morning or weekend observations. Some travel within New Zealand and internationally may also be required. This is a full-time (40 hrs per week), 26-month fixed-term role The remuneration is from $99,788 to $106,259 per annum and will be dependent on skills and experience. For more detailed information review this position description He kōrero mōu | About you We are seeking a practical, organised and collaborative researcher who is motivated by the challenge of turning advanced scientific infrastructure into a routinely used experimental capability. Essential Applicants should have: A PhD in physics, astronomy, optical science, electrical or communications engineering, aerospace engineering, space systems, or another closely related discipline. Hands-on experience operating, commissioning or supporting complex scientific or engineering instrumentation. Experience planning and conducting experimental observations, field campaigns, communications tests or other activities with fixed and time-critical operating windows. Experience collecting, quality-controlling and analysing quantitative experimental data. Strong organisational and time-management skills, including the ability to prepare thoroughly for events that occur at predetermined times. The ability to coordinate activities involving multiple researchers, organisations or facilities. The ability to diagnose practical problems involving interacting hardware, instrumentation, control and data-acquisition systems. Competence with scientific data-analysis tools such as Python, MATLAB or equivalent. Excellent oral and written communication skills. The ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a multidisciplinary and geographically distributed team. A careful, reliable and methodical approach to experimental operations and record keeping. The willingness and ability to work at Ardmore Field Station outside normal working hours when required by satellite-pass opportunities. Desirable Experience or knowledge in one or more of the following areas would be advantageous: Operating an astronomical telescope, robotic observatory, optical ground station or satellite ground station. Acquiring and tracking satellites or other rapidly moving targets. Free-space optical communications, laser communications, satellite communications or optical receiver systems. Communications-system metrics such as received power, signal-to-noise ratio, throughput, packet loss and bit error rate. Telescope pointing, tracking, calibration or optical alignment. Satellite-orbit prediction and pass planning. The effects of cloud, atmospheric extinction, turbulence, background light and pointing error on optical links. Linux-based observatory or scientific systems. International or multi-institutional research collaborations. Laser safety and the safe operation of optoelectronic equipment. Training students, researchers or facility operators. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer This position provides an opportunity to work at the intersection of experimental physics, astronomy, photonics, satellite communications and space systems. The successful candidate will have a central role in operating a distinctive New Zealand research facility and in developing practical international optical-ground-station collaborations. The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytical facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices where compatible with the operational requirements of the position. Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contributions. A competitive salary and five weeks’ annual leave. Career-development and professional-training programmes. Generous parental-leave provisions. Childcare services and a range of discounts on internal and external services. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 8th October 2026 to be considered. Applications should include: A cover letter explaining your interest in the position and your suitability for its operational and research responsibilities. A curriculum vitae. A brief statement addressing the essential skills and experience listed above. Please contact Associate Professor Nicholas Rattenbury - Department of Physics via [email protected] for a confidential conversation about the role. We are happy to answer questions, but applications cannot be accepted by email. We sincerely appreciate your interest in joining Waipapa Taumata Rau | the University of Auckland. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000148000742": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Physiology/Biomedical Science",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Physiology/Biomedical Science | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Physiology/Biomedical Science Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60021695 UoA Department Name: Physiology Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The Department of Physiology at the University of Auckland is a global leader in biomedical research and education, consistently ranked within the Top 50 worldwide in the QS World University Rankings by Subject (Anatomy & Physiology). Based in Grafton within the School of Medical Sciences, the Department maintains an outstanding research portfolio spanning basic to translational physiology in cardiovascular and respiratory systems, fetal and perinatal physiology and neuroscience, and sensory to neurophysiology fields, with strong interdisciplinary connections across the University—including the Auckland Bioengineering Institute (ABI), clinical departments, and advanced imaging centers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a motivated and ambitious academic at the Lecturer or Senior Lecturer level to join our dynamic department. In this role, you will significantly contribute to the Department’s and School’s academic and research programmes, including mentoring the next generation of biomedical leaders, driving high-impact funding and research initiatives, and contributing to a culture of academic excellence and equity. You will teach into the University's new Bachelor of Biomedical Science degree, the Physiology major, and the Medical programme, delivering high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate teaching. You will hold a PhD in physiology or a closely related biomedical discipline. You will be expected to establish an independent, high-impact research program that complements and expands departmental strengths. In line with evolving national science funding priorities, we also welcome candidates who demonstrate an interest or track record in research translation, commercialisation, industry engagement, or clinical collaboration. The successful candidate will be a collaborative scholar capable of building interdisciplinary networks, securing contestable grant funding, supervising postgraduate research students, and engaging with broader community and health stakeholders. This is a fulltime (40h per week) permanent position. The salary range for this role is as follows (skills and experience dependent): Lecturer: $99,788 - $119,204 Senior Lecturer: $126,863 - $158,904 For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You will hold a PhD in Physiology or a closely related biomedical discipline and have an established or emerging track record in research aligned with physiology, biomedical science, or a related field. You will be able to demonstrate research outputs appropriate to your career stage, along with the potential to develop and sustain an independent research programme. You will be committed to delivering high-quality teaching and supporting student success across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Experience teaching in physiology, biomedical science, neuroscience, or related disciplines, and supervising postgraduate students, will be advantageous. Our ideal candidate will bring: A track record of research achievement Evidence of, or potential for, securing competitive research funding. Experience supervising or mentoring postgraduate research students. The ability to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching. Strong communication and relationship-building skills. A collaborative approach and interest in interdisciplinary research. Experience or interest in research translation, commercialisation, industry engagement, or clinical collaboration. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 9 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions about this role, please email Justin Dean via [email protected] Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147994089": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Medical Lead",
      "description": "University of Auckland Medical Lead | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Medical Lead Contract Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021163 UoA Department Name: Student Health and Counselling Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Within Te Kāhu Tauira | Student & Scholarly Services, the University Health and Counselling Service (UHCS) provides high-quality, accessible healthcare and wellbeing support to students across the University. Our multidisciplinary team is committed to delivering exceptional care, promoting wellbeing, and helping students thrive throughout their university journey. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an experienced and inspiring Medical Lead to provide clinical leadership and strategic direction within our University Health and Counselling Service. This is a unique opportunity to combine clinical excellence with leadership in a purpose-driven environment that places student wellbeing at the heart of everything we do. As Medical Lead, you will play a key role in shaping the delivery of primary healthcare services for a diverse student population while supporting a team of dedicated clinicians to provide outstanding patient-centred care. Working closely with the General Manager and senior leadership team, you will contribute to service planning, clinical governance, quality improvement initiatives, and the ongoing development of a positive, collaborative, and high-performing clinical culture. This role offers the opportunity to make a meaningful impact on student health outcomes while helping shape the future of healthcare delivery within one of New Zealand’s leading universities. Key responsibilities include: Providing clinical leadership and support to medical practitioners within UHCS Promoting excellence in clinical practice, patient safety, and quality improvement initiatives Supporting clinical governance activities and ensuring compliance with relevant professional standards and legislation Contributing to service planning, operational decision-making, and strategic initiatives Working collaboratively with multidisciplinary teams including nurses, counsellors, psychologists, and administrative staff Supporting workforce development, mentoring, and professional growth within the medical team Maintaining a high standard of clinical care through direct patient consultations Supporting the development and implementation of evidence-based healthcare practices Building strong relationships with internal stakeholders and external healthcare providers Contributing to a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, and student-centred service delivery This role combines clinical leadership with hands-on practice, allowing you to continue delivering care while helping drive service excellence and positive health outcomes for students. This is a full-time , 37.5 hours per week role on a permanent contract. For more information, please review the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced and respected medical practitioner who is passionate about delivering high-quality healthcare and supporting the development of others. You bring strong clinical leadership skills, sound judgement, and the ability to foster positive relationships across multidisciplinary teams. You are committed to continuous improvement, patient-centred care, and creating an inclusive and supportive environment where both students and colleagues can thrive. Most importantly, you are motivated by the opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to student wellbeing while helping shape the future direction of a trusted and highly valued health service. Ngā pūkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience Current registration with the Medical Council of New Zealand and a current practising certificate Significant experience working in General Practice or primary healthcare Demonstrated clinical leadership experience and the ability to support and mentor clinicians Strong understanding of clinical governance, quality improvement, and patient safety principles Excellent communication and relationship-building skills Experience working effectively within multidisciplinary healthcare teams A commitment to culturally responsive practice and equitable health outcomes The following would be beneficial: Vocational registration in General Practice Experience working with young adults or within tertiary health services Experience leading service improvement and healthcare innovation initiatives Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland provides a supportive, people-centred working environment where your wellbeing and professional development are genuinely valued. As part of our team, you’ll enjoy: Flexible employment practices Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Five weeks’ annual leave A wide range of professional development opportunities Generous parental leave provisions Access to staff discounts, recreation facilities, wellbeing initiatives, and discounted parking The opportunity to combine clinical practice with leadership in a rewarding university healthcare environment The chance to make a lasting impact on the health, wellbeing, and success of thousands of students Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online to be considered. Please submit your application by 20 September 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV outlining how your skills and experience align with this role. For a confidential conversation about the role, please contact Tania Abernethy-Jones - tania.abernethy-jones@auckland.ac.nz. Please note that applications sent via email will not be accepted. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Functional Analyst Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Medical Lead Auckland, New Zealand Contract I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147991639": {
      "title": "Associate Professor in Medicine",
      "description": "University of Auckland Associate Professor in Medicine - Cardiology | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Associate Professor in Medicine - Cardiology Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60021674 UoA Department Name: Medicine Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa, New Zealand, and the Asia-Pacific. Our lecturers and health researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world and passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an Associate Professor in Medicine (Cardiology) to join the Department of Medicine within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences. This senior academic role offers an opportunity to make a significant contribution to cardiology through clinical teaching, research, supervision, service, and leadership. You will play a critical role in advancing undergraduate and postgraduate medical education while developing and leading a research programme aligned with the strategic priorities of the Department of Medicine. Working closely with colleagues across the University and Health New Zealand-Te Toka Tumai Auckland, you will contribute to the development and delivery of high-quality teaching programmes, supervise postgraduate research students, and pursue innovative research that enhances the profile and impact of the Department nationally and internationally. The successful candidate will be expected to build collaborative research partnerships, secure external funding, contribute to curriculum development, and provide academic leadership within cardiology and the wider Department of Medicine. This is an outstanding opportunity to join a leading academic and clinical environment dedicated to excellence in education, research, and patient outcomes. This is a part-time , 4 hours per week, on a permanent contract. The salary for this role is $165,808 + $57,798 medical loading per annum (pro rata). For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review this Position Description . Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Department of Medicine is one of New Zealand’s leading academic medicine departments, bringing together clinicians, researchers, educators, and students to improve health outcomes through world-class research, teaching, and clinical practice. As part of the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, the Department works closely Health New Zealand and other health sector partners to deliver exceptional medical education and research that has meaningful impact for patients and communities throughout New Zealand and internationally. He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for an accomplished academic cardiologist with a strong commitment to teaching, research, and clinical excellence. To be successful in this role, you will bring: A medical qualification registrable in New Zealand. Fellowship of the Royal Australasian College of Physicians (FRACP) or an equivalent cardiology qualification. Vocational registration with the Medical Council of New Zealand, or eligibility to obtain registration. A PhD and/or MD qualification. Eligibility to be employed within the Cardiology Service at Health New Zealand-Te Toka Tumai Auckland. Demonstrated excellence in undergraduate and postgraduate teaching. Experience supervising research students, including doctoral candidates. A strong track record of research achievement, including publications and successful research activity. Experience developing collaborative research programmes and partnerships. The ability to attract research funding and build a sustainable research programme. Strong organisational, leadership, and stakeholder engagement skills. A commitment to equity in education and healthcare delivery. A passion for advancing cardiology through teaching, research, and service. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 20/09/2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the abovementioned skills and experiences. Please reach out to Professor Nicola Dalbeth via [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147987462": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Media & Communications Partnering Lead (Education, Faculty)",
      "description": "University of Auckland Media & Communications Partnering Lead (Education, Faculty) | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Media & Communications Partnering Lead (Education, Faculty) Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019472 UoA Department Name: Communications Job Description With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. As part of our newly established Communications function, we've created the role of Media and Communications Partnering Lead (Education and Faculty) to head up a new team of embedded Media and Communications Partners.  This is a rare opportunity for an experienced and people-focused communications leader, to help shape the future direction of communications at New Zealand's leading University. Find out more about how we’re reshaping how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Reporting to the Director of Communications, you’ll lead a team of five trusted Media and Communications Partners. Together you’ll create a new partnering model to deliver high-quality strategic advice and communications support for the University’s faculties (Law, Business & Economics, Arts & Education, Science, Engineering & Design and Medical and Health Sciences). As a trusted advisor to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Education and the Education Office, you’ll support strategic communications planning and delivery, stakeholder engagement and reputation-sensitive activity relating to the University’s education portfolio. With a strong media focus, your team will proactively tell the University’s most compelling stories, shaping reputation and contributing to our social licence. We want to talk about not only what the University is good at, but what it’s good for. Key areas of focus: Lead, coach and develop a team of communications professionals, embedding a future-focussed partnering model built on expertise and collaboration. Provide strategic communications counsel and reputation management expertise to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Education, Education Office and other senior leaders. Oversee communications planning, messaging, and delivery, helping identify the audiences that matter most, the channels to reach them and the stories that will resonate. Support communications responses during emerging issues and crisis situations. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $114,400 - $169,800 per annum. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $122,501 – $155,000 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you We're particularly interested in people who bring: Significant experience in strategic communications, media relations, communications business partnering or public relations within a large and complex organisation. Proven people leadership experience, including coaching, mentoring, performance development and leading teams through change. Experience managing reputationally sensitive communications issues and supporting organisational priorities in dynamic environments. Sound understanding of New Zealand’s changing media landscape and the impacts of AI on how audiences are engaging with content. Cultural competency and experience in shaping communications for diverse audiences, with a strong understanding of Te Ao Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi. Strong stakeholder engagement and influencing skills, with the ability to build trusted relationships across all levels of an organisation. Excellent planning, prioritisation and problem-solving abilities. Confidence operating in ambiguity and balancing strategic oversight with hands-on support when required. A commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi, equity, inclusion and culturally responsive practice. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you'll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of 1 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Verity Collins verity.collins@auckland.ac.nz with any questions. Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Internal Communications Lead Auckland, New Zealand Media & Communications Partners Auckland, New Zealand Editor – Digital Content Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Media & Communications Partnering Lead (Education, Faculty) Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147789868": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Research Grants and Contracts Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Research Grants and Contracts Manager | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Research Grants and Contracts Manager Full-time Campus: Newmarket Campus Position Number: 55572705 UoA Department Name: Research Operations, Ethics, and Integrity Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university. We employ more than 9000 academic and professional staff to support over 50,000 students, making us one of NZ’s largest employers. Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on both our student body and our scholarly community, and we work with both community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the peoples of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to pursuing sustainability. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a skilled and collaborative Research Grants & Contracts Manager to join our team at Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. In this role, you will lead the negotiation and execution of complex contracts, subcontracts, and variations for research and consulting projects with external clients, funders, and partners. You’ll work closely with researchers, Faculty and Institute Research Services Teams (FIRST), UniServices teams, and commercial stakeholders to deliver optimal contracting outcomes within the University’s delegations framework and risk appetite. Key responsibilities include: Manage the drafting, negotiation and execution of complex agreements. Build and maintain strong stakeholder relationships across the University and external partners. Provide clear and timely communication on contract progress and outcomes. Review contracts with a high level of attention to detail and risk awareness. Support colleagues with contractual advice, training and peak-period workloads. Contribute to continuous improvement and accurate contract management reporting. This position is currently based at the University of Auckland's Newmarket Campus. We offer flexible hybrid working arrangements that support a balance of working from home and on campus, subject to business requirements. This is a full time, permanent employment agreement, working 37.5 hours per week.  This is a salary band G, offering a salary range of $87,000 - $103,000 per annum. depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the position description. About the team | Mō tō mātou rōpū The Research Grants & Contracts team manages a high volume of commercial and research agreements that help enable world-class research and innovation across the University. It's a collaborative, high-performing team that takes pride in delivering quality outcomes and supporting researchers to make a meaningful impact. With a strong \"one team\" culture, team members work closely together, share knowledge freely and support each other through changing priorities and busy periods. The team enjoys the satisfaction of knowing that the work they do every day contributes to research, partnerships and initiatives that create positive outcomes for the University and wider society. He kōrero mōu | About you You are a detail-oriented contracts professional with a strong ability to draft, review and manage complex agreements. You combine excellent written communication skills with sound commercial judgement and enjoy working collaboratively with a diverse range of stakeholders to achieve positive outcomes. You are organised, adaptable and solutions-focused, with the ability to manage multiple priorities while maintaining a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. To be successful in this role, you will bring: Demonstrated experience drafting, reviewing and managing complex contracts and agreements. Strong written communication skills and the ability to interpret and clearly explain contractual terms. Excellent attention to detail, with the ability to identify risks and ensure contractual accuracy. Proven stakeholder management skills, with the ability to build collaborative and trusted relationships with both internal and external partners. Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage competing priorities in a busy environment. A collaborative and customer-focused approach, with a willingness to support colleagues and contribute to a positive team culture. Experience working in a tertiary education, research, legal, commercial or professional services environment would be advantageous. A Bachelor of Laws (LLB) qualification or equivalent experience in contract management and drafting would be highly regarded. Additional Information What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou We offer you more than just a job. We offer you the opportunity to be part of a dynamic, world-class educational institution. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits, including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave. Superannuation/KiwiSaver matching up to 6.75% Leading and inclusive parental leave options. Ongoing professional development opportunities and access to a range of discounted on campus services and amenitites. Hear more about our partnership with Māori, our Te Ao Māori principles , our impact, our staff, and life in Auckland on our career’s website . For further information please visit our staff benefits page. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of Friday 18th of September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. For any further questions related to the role, please contact Aimee Carr on aimee.carr@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Research Portfolio Lead – University Research Centres x 2 Auckland, New Zealand Research Engagement Manager - 4 positions (Aligned to the University's Signature Research Areas) Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Physiology/Biomedical Science Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Research Grants and Contracts Manager Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147746445": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Team Leader, Student Hubs and Client Services",
      "description": "University of Auckland Team Leader, Student Hubs and Client Services | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Team Leader, Student Hubs and Client Services Contract Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021986 UoA Department Name: Student Hub Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Te Kahu Tauira I Student & Scholarly Services is looking for a skilled Team Leader to join the Student Hub leadership team. Your role forms part of a wider Student Hubs Leadership group across our 6 locations. This role will be responsible for the team and operations of our Davis Law Library on City Campus. To ensure alignment with the wider leadership team the role will be split across the City Student Hub and Davis Law Library as below: Two days at Davis Law Library (days TBC) Two days at City (Tuesdays and one other day) One day Work from Home (day TBC) This fixed term role is covering for a secondment, and the contract dates are likely to be from October 2026 to the end of April 2027. It is a full-time, 37.5 hours per week, role with a Monday-Friday work pattern (start and end times negotiable) and the salary range is  $88,000 - $98,000 per annum. For more detailed information on the role responsibilities and requirements, please review the position description . Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Student Hubs team is the physical gateway to the Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. We provide a welcoming, friendly, and mana-enhancing service for future and current students across their student journey. At Student Hubs, our work is guided by the following values that reflect our commitment to collaboration, leadership, learning and community: Mana Enhancing: We’re a place for uplifting interaction Service Excellence: We’re professional, accurate and responsive Manaakitanga: We listen, respect, understand and care Whanaungatanga: We connect, partner and strengthen relationships Kaitiakitanga: We guide, support and see things through We deliver a wide range of services to support student on their journey at Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. Our primary function is to deliver high quality in-person and online services that support students holistically, and these include: guidance and advice on being a student navigating the University systems, spaces and processes exploring study options application and enrolment support Library & Student IT services He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for a highly motivated individual who: Has proven leadership experience Is an innovative problem-solver and champions continuous improvement Is comfortable leading in an environment of change Has excellent communication skills Is highly organised and able to manage a variety of work simultaneously Has the ability to see the bigger picture, but not forget the detail Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply If you are strong team player who is excited about working with people and passionate about leading, then we want to hear from you! Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Thursday 17 September to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the abovementioned skills and experiences. If you have questions or would like a confidential conversation, please reach out to Kelly Duffus ( k.duffus@auckland.ac.nz ).  Please note we are happy to answer questions, but we do not accept applications by email . Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education Auckland, New Zealand Event Technician Auckland, New Zealand Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Team Leader, Student Hubs and Client Services Auckland, New Zealand Contract I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147738310": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60020825 UoA Department Name: Education and Social Practice Company Description Job Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Education and Social Practice within Te Pūtahi Mātauranga | Faculty of Arts and Education is seeking a Lecturer in Sport, Health and Physical Education to join our collegial academic team. This is an opportunity to contribute to teaching, research and service across the discipline, including the education of future teachers. You will contribute to the delivery and development of our programmes, supervise postgraduate students and develop an active programme of research, while building collaborative relationships across the School, Faculty and wider community. We are particularly interested in applicants whose teaching and research expertise will complement the School's existing strengths in areas such as anatomy, exercise physiology, Health and Physical Education curriculum, games education or sport pedagogy. Key responsibilities include: Delivering high-quality teaching, contributing to curriculum development and supervising postgraduate students. Developing and maintaining an active programme of research, including scholarly publications, research collaborations and external funding opportunities. Building collaborative relationships across the School, Faculty and wider community to support teaching, research and service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week) permanent position (subject to continuation). The workload split is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. The salary range for this role is $99,788 - $119,204 per annum. Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and is aligned with our Academic Standards policy. For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description. He kōrero mōu | About you You are a collaborative academic with expertise in Sport, Health and Physical Education and a passion for educating future teachers. You bring a thoughtful, inclusive approach to your work and are committed to making a positive contribution through teaching, research and engagement. To be successful in this role, you will bring: A PhD in Sport, Health or Physical Education. Experience teaching Sport, Health and Physical Education at the tertiary level. A developing or established research profile, with high-quality research outputs and the potential to contribute to collaborative research and external funding. Experience applying socially critical, inclusive and culturally responsive approaches within teaching, research or professional practice. Strong communication, collaboration and relationship-building skills. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Sunday 20 September to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter outlining your interest in and suitability for the role. Please direct enquiries to Associate Professor Alan Ovens (Discipline Leader, Sport, Health and Physical Education) via [email protected] . Please note while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications via email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147699351": {
      "title": "University of Auckland 3 x Health & Safety Adviser",
      "description": "University of Auckland 3 x Health & Safety Adviser – Science and Engineering  – 12-month fixed term  | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . 3 x Health & Safety Adviser – Science and Engineering  – 12-month fixed term Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021491 UoA Department Name: Health and Safety Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for three Health & Safety Advisers to join our Science and Engineering faculties at the University of Auckland: One Health & Safety Adviser – Science One Health & Safety Adviser - Medical and Health Sciences One Health & Safety Adviser – Engineering These are broad, hands-on roles where you will work alongside academics, researchers, technicians, professional staff, and students to help create safe, compliant, and well-managed environments across complex teaching, research, laboratory, and engineering settings. This is much more than a compliance role. You will be a trusted adviser who understands the needs of the business, provides practical advice, challenges when required, and helps people find solutions that enable teaching and research to happen safely. Working closely with the relevant Health, Safety, and Wellbeing Manager and Faculty stakeholders, you will provide specialist advice and guidance across a broad range of health Nga Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Provide practical, health and safety advice and guidance across complex environments Support the identification and management of hazards and risks, including reviewing risk assessments, implementing appropriate controls, and supporting higher-risk laboratory and engineering activities. Lead and support incident response and investigations, corrective actions, and organisational learning, while identifying opportunities for continuous improvement. Undertake safety walks, inspections, and audits, while coordinating health and safety documentation, training, records, reporting, and other compliance requirements Build strong relationships with managers, academics, technicians, researchers, and students, providing training and guidance Using health and safety systems and data to identify emerging risks and contribute to practical improvements that strengthen safety across the Faculty. Supporting compliance with relevant legislation, standards, and University requirements. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), 12-month fixed-term position. The salary range is $87,000 to $118,00 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for someone who combines sound technical health and safety knowledge with strong interpersonal skills and a practical, people-focused approach. You will bring: A relevant tertiary qualification and demonstrated experience in health and safety, preferably within a complex, technical or higher-risk environment. For the Science positions: a degree in Science, Environmental Science, Occupational Health & Safety or a related discipline. A Chemistry qualification or background would be advantageous . For the Engineering position: a degree in Engineering, Mechanical Engineering or a related discipline. Approximately 3–4 years’ relevant health and safety experience, ideally within a complex operational, laboratory, research, engineering, or other technical environment Strong knowledge of risk management, hazard identification, incident management and health and safety compliance. Experience supporting incident investigations, corrective actions and continuous improvement, as well as developing, reviewing and maintaining risk assessments, procedures and health and safety documentation. Experience undertaking audits, inspections, safety walks and other assurance activities, with strong attention to detail and the ability to manage multiple compliance and administrative requirements. Excellent communication, relationship-building and influencing skills, with the confidence to work with people at all levels, challenge constructively, provide appropriate pushback and build practical, solutions-focused relationships. The ability to work autonomously, exercise sound judgment, and take ownership, supported by strong organisational and administrative skills, Proficiency in Microsoft Excel and experience using large or complex health and safety management systems - Sidetrack and/or Ideagen (previously Damstra) would be advantageous. If you have a background in science, engineering, manufacturing or laboratory environments, and a passion for health, safety and wellbeing, we’d love to hear from you. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and several other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 24th September 2026, and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. Please indicate your preferred position – Science, Engineering, or either – in your cover letter If you have any questions about this role, please get in touch with Sharon Kishore (Recruitment Consultant) via sharon.kishore@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential discussion. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information All your information will be kept confidential according to EEO guidelines. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Services Adviser - part-time (18.75 hrs pw) Auckland, New Zealand STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. 3 x Health & Safety Adviser – Science and Engineering  – 12-month fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147584090": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Marketing Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Marketing Manager - Campus experience | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Marketing Manager - Campus experience Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019621 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Job Description Auckland | Permanent | Full-time Hybrid working environment – mix of on-site and work from home Marketing & Recruitment, Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland $114,400 - $154,800 per annum (Band I) depending on experience Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity A university is more than a place of study. It's where students build friendships, discover new interests, access support, and develop a sense of belonging. These experiences shape how students choose a university, how connected and supported they feel, and ultimately how they thrive throughout their time at university. We're looking for a Marketing Manager who can bring that experience to life. Leading marketing for our Campus Experience portfolio, you'll use audience insight, strategic thinking, and compelling storytelling to showcase our campuses, accommodation, facilities, services, events, and student opportunities. You'll be comfortable working across a broad portfolio, translating diverse experiences into clear, engaging marketing that helps future and current students understand what makes our University experience distinctive. This new role and focussed portfolio is part of a broader transformation of how we engage and connect with our audiences, amplify our stories and create connected and meaningful experiences. Find out more about our reimagining of our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $114,400 - $ 154,800 - Band I, per annum. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $122,501 - $140,000, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you We're seeking a collaborative and results-driven marketing professional with experience delivering integrated marketing campaigns, content and events within a complex, stakeholder-rich environment. You'll have a track record of successfully managing multiple priorities, working across diverse portfolios, and building strong relationships with internal stakeholders, external suppliers and senior leaders to achieve shared goals. You'll combine strong people leadership and communication skills with a data-informed approach to decision making, using insights and performance metrics to optimise outcomes and inform recommendations. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Experience delivering end-to-end marketing campaigns, content and events, from strategy and planning through to execution and evaluation. Strong strategic marketing capability, with the ability to translate organisational objectives into impactful audience engagement activities. A data-informed approach to decision-making, using insights and performance metrics to optimise outcomes. An innovative mindset and commitment to continuous improvement, with experience enhancing processes, services or ways of working. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer This is a rare opportunity to contribute to a new function, vision and culture while working within an organisation that operates at significant scale. This new function gives you access to internal expertise and resources commensurate with our scale and ambition, including an in-house design studio, media production teams, audience specialists, web and digital teams, AI and automation expertise, and dedicated Māori and Pacific specialists across brand, media, comms and engagement. Together, these teams help bring ideas to life through integrated campaigns, content and experiences. As a comprehensive university, our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. You'll have the chance to contribute across a wide range of projects and strategic priorities while continuing to grow your own career. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Friday, 18 September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Fiona Badger, Recruitment Consultant on f.badger@auckland,ac,nz with any questions. Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Associate Director, Performance and Digital Auckland, New Zealand Marketing Manager – Postgraduate & lifelong learning Auckland, New Zealand Media Adviser Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Marketing Manager - Campus experience Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147576929": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Marketing Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Marketing Manager – Postgraduate & lifelong learning | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Marketing Manager – Postgraduate & lifelong learning Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019619 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Job Description Auckland | Permanent | Full-time Hybrid working environment – mix of on-site and work from home Marketing & Recruitment, Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland $114,400 - $154,800 per annum (Band I) depending on experience Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Every decision to study starts with a question about the future. Whether someone is advancing their career, changing direction, or returning to learning, they need confidence that their next step is the right one. We're looking for a Marketing Manager who can help make those decisions easier. Leading marketing for our postgraduate and lifelong learning portfolio, you'll use audience insight, strategic thinking, and compelling storytelling to engage prospective students and help them understand the opportunities available to them. You'll be comfortable working across a broad portfolio, translating complexity into clear, relevant marketing that supports informed decision-making and meaningful engagement. This new role and focussed portfolio is part of a broader transformation of how we engage and connect with our audiences, amplify our stories and create connected and meaningful experiences. Find out more about our reimagining of our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $114,400 - $ 154,800 - Band I, per annum. We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $122,501 - $140,000, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you We're seeking a collaborative and results-driven marketing professional with experience delivering integrated marketing campaigns, content and events within a complex, stakeholder-rich environment. You'll have a track record of successfully managing multiple priorities, working across diverse portfolios, and building strong relationships with internal stakeholders, external suppliers and senior leaders to achieve shared goals. You'll combine strong people leadership and communication skills with a data-informed approach to decision making, using insights and performance metrics to optimise outcomes and inform recommendations. We're particularly interested in people who bring: Experience delivering end-to-end marketing campaigns, content and events, from strategy and planning through to execution and evaluation. Strong strategic marketing capability, with the ability to translate organisational objectives into impactful audience engagement activities. A data-informed approach to decision-making, using insights and performance metrics to optimise outcomes. An innovative mindset and commitment to continuous improvement, with experience enhancing processes, services or ways of working. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer This is a rare opportunity to contribute to a new function, vision and culture while working within an organisation that operates at significant scale. This new function gives you access to internal expertise and resources commensurate with our scale and ambition, including an in-house design studio, media production teams, audience specialists, web and digital teams, AI and automation expertise, and dedicated Māori and Pacific specialists across brand, media, comms and engagement. Together, these teams help bring ideas to life through integrated campaigns, content and experiences. As a comprehensive university, our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. You'll have the chance to contribute across a wide range of projects and strategic priorities while continuing to grow your own career. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa New Zealand's most diverse and complex organisations Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Friday, 18 September 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Fiona Badger, Recruitment Consultant on f.badger@auckland,ac,nz with any questions. Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Associate Director, Performance and Digital Auckland, New Zealand Marketing Manager - Campus experience Auckland, New Zealand Associate Director, Postgraduate Recruitment Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Marketing Manager – Postgraduate & lifelong learning Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
