    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147407329": {
      "title": "Lecturer in European Studies, with Gender Studies",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in European Studies, with Gender Studies | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in European Studies, with Gender Studies Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021668 UoA Department Name: Humanities Job Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ranked #47 in the QS Ranking for Arts & Humanities Ranked 5th in Oceania and 28th globally for sustainability (QS Sustainability Rankings 2026) Shape teaching and research in European literatures and visual cultures, with expertise in Gender Studies The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Situated within Te Pūtahi Mātauranga | The Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna Aronui | Humanities is Aotearoa’s leading centre for humanities scholarship, home to the largest concentration of humanities academics in the country. The School includes English, Drama and Comparative Literature alongside Art History, Asian Studies and Languages, History, Philosophy, Media and Screen Studies, and more. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Lecturer in European Studies, specialising in European literatures and visual cultures, with expertise in Gender Studies. This is a permanent position commencing January 2027, or by negotiation. This is an opportunity to contribute to the development and delivery of European Studies across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, bringing expertise in transnational European contexts across any period from the medieval to the present. You will contribute to core European Studies curriculum, alongside teaching in areas such as French and/or Spanish literatures, comparative literature and drama, film and media. You will also contribute to the Faculty’s cross-disciplinary Gender Studies programme, including teaching the core second-year course in feminist and gender theory on a biennial basis. The role provides scope to develop research and teaching across a broad range of European literary and visual cultures, while contributing to the intellectual life of the School and Faculty. Key areas of contribution include: Teaching and curriculum development across European Studies, French and/or Spanish literatures, comparative literature and drama, film and media, at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Contributing to the Gender Studies programme, including its core second-year feminist and gender theory course. Developing a productive, high-quality research profile and contributing to the wider research culture of the School. Supervising PhD and other postgraduate students across a broad range of periods and topics. Contributing to academic service, leadership and citizenship within the School, Faculty and wider University. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research and 20% service. Remuneration will be commensurate with experience and achievements and aligned with the University's Academic Standards policy . It will be in the range of Lecturer (L): NZ$99,788 - NZ$119,204 per annum. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a collegial academic with expertise in European literatures and visual cultures, and a strong commitment to high-quality research and teaching. You will have excellent communication and collaboration skills and be able to work effectively across disciplinary areas and engage students from diverse cultural backgrounds. You must hold a PhD by the time of appointment in European literatures, comparative literature, film or media studies, languages, cultural studies, Gender Studies or a related field, with: Competency in at least two Romance European languages and cultures represented in our curriculum, preferably French and Spanish. A record of effective tertiary teaching, research and publication in relevant fields. The ability to teach in Gender Studies, with experience in, or readiness to undertake, postgraduate supervision and advising. Expertise in European diasporas, colonialisms and postcolonialisms, with experience adapting and innovating curricula and courses in response to evolving needs, would be desirable. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest-ranked and most comprehensive research-intensive university, we offer an environment that supports academic excellence and career development, including: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research and Study Leave Research, teaching and leadership development programmes, mentoring and professional development opportunities Flexible employment practices, including hybrid working and flexibility in how and where you work Up to 6.75% employer superannuation scheme Gender-neutral parental leave, including up to nine weeks’ full pay On-site, discounted childcare centres Access to active staff networks and a range of other staff benefits Located in the heart of Auckland, the University offers the opportunity to live and work in one of Aotearoa New Zealand’s most vibrant and diverse cities, with easy access to beaches, harbours, native bush and a temperate climate. For more information, please visit our Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by Sunday 04 October, 2026 (11.59pm NZT). Please include a cover letter outlining your interest and suitability for the position, your curriculum vitae, and the names and contact details of three academic referees. For a confidential discussion about the position, including teaching requirements for 2027, please contact Associate Professor Lisa Bailey, Interim Head of School, at [email protected] . Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but applications must be made online. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147406649": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Procure to Pay Administrator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Procure to Pay Administrator | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Procure to Pay Administrator Contract Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55560200 UoA Department Name: Shared Transaction Centre Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is a great place to work! We have a collaborative and inclusive environment and offer many benefits not offered by other organisations. The Shared Transaction Centre (STC) is the University of Auckland’s centralised ‘Procure to Pay’ and ‘Order to Cash’ centre. It is responsible for all purchasing, billing, and revenue collection activity for faculties and most service divisions.  We perform an operational function, consolidating high volume finance transactional activities relating to the purchasing and supply of goods and services. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity 6-Month Fixed Term Secondment Opportunity We are looking for an Accounts Payable Administrator to join the Shared Transaction Centre on a fixed term contract. Support the Accounts Payable Team Leader to work with internal and external stakeholders, maximise automation financial processing, administrative efficiency and improve service delivery across the organisation. Key accountabilities for this position include, but not limited to: Large supplier account reconciliations Requisition requests High volume invoice processing Match exceptions resolutions Receipting escalations Supplier maintenance, onboarding and set-up Co-ordination and execution of foreign and domestic pay runs, working with Treasury, signatories, and the banks. Identify technical roadblocks and opportunities, and suggesting improvements Financial Delegations/Sensitive Expenditure Processing Travel/expenses and reimbursements Accurately matching invoices to Purchase Orders Escalation of issues to the advisory team for internal/external customers Managing the accounts payable function for your portion of the ledger Accurately matching invoices to Purchase Orders Weekly and monthly payment runs Other Ad Hoc duties This is a full-time role (37.5 hours per week, Monday to Friday). Salary range is $62,100 - $70,200pa plus benefits. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You’ve worked in a complex, high‑volume environment, thrive under time pressure, and stay current with accounts payable/P2P best practice. You’re a people person with a can‑do attitude who balances accurate, high‑throughput processing with excellent service to students and corporate customers (transactions, refunds, invoice payments). Ngā Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Excellent organisational and analytical skills. Experience using an ERP (e.g., PeopleSoft) and strong Excel proficiency; quick to learn new, industry‑specific systems. Experience in Accounts Payable and Pay to Purchase within a large complex organisation Proven performance in a customer‑focused environment with strong problem‑solving abilities. A clear commitment to policy, control, and compliance in every transaction. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer We offer an opportunity within New Zealand’s leading University that will have a positive impact on the lives of students. We also offer: Five weeks’ annual leave per annum Flexible working – Hybrid model (blend of in-office and remote) Discounted parking and various leisure and recreation services A diverse and inclusive working environment For more information please visit Staff Benefits . Qualifications Additional Information Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 13th September , 2026 to be considered. All candidates must have , or be able to obtain, the legal right to work in New Zealand . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend Posted by Joshua Chapman share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Facilities & Services Team Leader Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer in European Studies, with Gender Studies Auckland, New Zealand STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Procure to Pay Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Contract I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147393785": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Research Engagement Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Research Engagement Manager - Māori | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Research Engagement Manager - Māori Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019819 UoA Department Name: Research Development and Impact Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We have an exciting opportunity for a Research Engagement Manager – Māori to join the newly established Research Engagement function within Te Puna Tiketike | Research and Innovation Office. Supporting the James Henare Research Centre (JHRC) and aligned Signature Research Areas, this role will play a critical part in strengthening relationships between the University, iwi, hapū, Māori organisations, communities, researchers, and strategic partners. Working within a kaupapa Māori framework, you will lead strategic engagement activities, develop trusted partnerships, and facilitate opportunities for collaboration that support Māori research aspirations and contribute to meaningful research impact. As a key connector and advisor, you will help shape research engagement approaches that align community priorities with the University's research strengths and strategic objectives. Key responsibilities include: Providing specialist advice to the James Henare Research Centre Academic Director, Signature Research Area (SRA) research leads and senior leaders on Māori research engagement strategy, partnership development, and co-design practice grounded in kaupapa Māori. Developing and coordinating engagement plans that reflect iwi, hapū and Māori community priorities, align with strategic objectives, and contribute to a seamless and coherent partner experience. Building and stewarding long-term, mutually beneficial relationships with iwi, hapū, Māori organisations, Māori businesses, industry, funders, government agencies, civil society organisations and international partners. Acting as a strategic broker and wayfinder, connecting Māori partners with University expertise and innovation capability while ensuring Māori aspirations help inform research agendas and priorities. Convening and facilitating co-design processes, including hui and wānanga in tikanga-led and marae-based settings, bringing together Māori communities, researchers and interdisciplinary teams to develop shared research opportunities. Collaborating with Marketing and Communications teams to ensure outreach activities, events, digital engagement and thought leadership initiatives are aligned with broader partnership and engagement strategies. This is a Permanent full‑time, 37.5‑hour‑per‑week contract.  This is a band H position with the salary band from $98,700 pa to $121,900 pa depending on skills, qualifications and experience. This position will be based in the James Henare Research Centre, City Campus For more information on the role, please view the Position Description. About the Research Engagement Team We're establishing a new Research Engagement function within Te Puna Tiketike | Research and Innovation Office to strengthen the University's ability to build meaningful, long-term partnerships that enable excellent research and deliver impact. Working alongside researchers, communities, iwi, industry, government, and other partners, the team will help shape shared research agendas, foster transdisciplinary collaboration, and support co-designed research that addresses challenges of national and global importance. Rather than responding to opportunities as they arise, the team will take a strategic, relationship-centred approach to engagement, helping position the University as a trusted partner while making it easier for external organisations to connect with our research expertise. Aligned to the University's Signature Research Areas, the team will work closely with researchers to develop enduring partnerships, build engagement capability across the University, and embed research engagement and impact as core components of research excellence. As the Research Engagement Manager – Māori , you will play a pivotal role in building and strengthening relationships with iwi, hapū, Māori organisations and communities, helping to ensure Māori aspirations and priorities are reflected in the University's research partnerships and impact agenda He kōrero mōu | About you You are a strategic relationship builder with a passion for connecting people, ideas and research to create meaningful outcomes for Māori communities. You bring significant experience in research engagement, strategic partnerships, knowledge mobilisation, science communication, business development or a related field, along with a demonstrated ability to build and sustain trusted relationships across diverse stakeholder groups. You have experience engaging effectively with Māori communities, iwi and hapū and can confidently apply kaupapa Māori principles and tikanga in your professional practice. You also bring a strong understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its application within research engagement and partnership development. Skills and experience that are required: A minimum of five years' experience in research engagement, knowledge mobilisation, science communication, business development, or a related strategic partnership role. Demonstrated success in building and sustaining high-trust relationships across diverse stakeholder groups, including government agencies, industry, iwi and hapū, community organisations, and policy leaders. Proven experience engaging with Māori communities, iwi and hapū in research or partnership contexts, with the ability to apply kaupapa Māori principles and tikanga appropriately in professional practice. Deep understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles and their strategic application within research engagement and partnership practice. Additional Information Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland - is New Zealand's leading university and offers an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Five weeks' annual leave Career development and professional learning opportunities Generous parental leave provisions Excellent campus facilities Access to a range of staff benefits, wellbeing initiatives, and discounts For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Friday 18th of September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter outlining your interest in the role and relevant experience and your current curriculum vitae (CV) For any questions related to the role, please reach out to the Recruitment Team on recruitment.team@auckland.ac.nz #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Editor – Digital Content Auckland, New Zealand Media & Communications Partnering Lead (Education, Faculty) Auckland, New Zealand Media Adviser Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Research Engagement Manager - Māori Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149502249": {
      "title": "Community Engagement Coordinator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Community Engagement Coordinator - SPARX | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Community Engagement Coordinator - SPARX Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022103 UoA Department Name: Liggins Institute Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Join the Human Health Research Services Platform at the Liggins Institute and help increase awareness, engagement and uptake of SPARX (www.sparx.org.nz), an evidence-based digital mental health programme designed to support the wellbeing of young people. As Community Engagement Coordinator, you will help connect SPARX with communities across Aotearoa New Zealand through outreach, partnerships, events, communications and digital promotion. Working closely with the SPARX project and research teams, you will identify and coordinate opportunities to promote SPARX through community events, schools, libraries, youth organisations, healthcare providers and digital channels. You will build strong stakeholder relationships, coordinate engagement activities and help us understand what is working so we can continue to improve our reach and impact. Key responsibilities: Bring SPARX into communities through events, expos and local initiatives. Build strong relationships with schools, youth organisations, health providers and community groups. Coordinate engaging SPARX events, activations and promotional resources. Create engaging social content and support digital campaigns. Track engagement and campaign performance to understand what works. Work with designers, suppliers and partners to bring promotional ideas to life. Spot new opportunities to grow SPARX awareness, reach and uptake. This is a part-time position (15 hours per week) for a fixed term of 15 months The salary range for this role are $ 63,600 - $79,300 pro rata depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are a confident and organised communicator who enjoys connecting with people and turning ideas into practical engagement activities. You are comfortable building relationships with diverse communities and stakeholders, coordinating multiple activities at once, and representing a programme in community and public settings. You will bring a creative and proactive approach to community engagement and promotion, with an interest in using both in-person and digital channels to reach young people and the communities that support them. You will be motivated by the opportunity to contribute to an evidence-based programme with real-world impact. Our ideal candidate will bring: A relevant tertiary qualification in communications, marketing, health promotion or a related discipline, or equivalent relevant experience. Experience coordinating events, community engagement activities or outreach initiatives. Experience building and maintaining relationships with a range of internal and external stakeholders. Experience planning and managing social media content across multiple platforms. A good eye for design and visual communication, with experience creating polished, engaging content in Canva or similar tools and applying brand guidelines consistently. Strong organisational, planning and project coordination skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities. Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Human Health Research Services Platform is hosted by the Liggins Institute and provides a comprehensive range of services to support human health research across the University of Auckland and beyond, including data services, statistical support, health economics, research coordination, follow-up research coordination, digital support, and clinic space and nursing support. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 29 September 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. Please also include links in your CV to examples of relevant work, or provide a portfolio showcasing work such as social media content, campaigns, events, community engagement, digital content or other promotional projects If you have any questions, please email Elaine Umali: [email protected] Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
