    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149461794": {
      "title": "Student Hub Assistant",
      "description": "University of Auckland Student Hub Assistant | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Student Hub Assistant Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 59033998 UoA Department Name: Student Hub Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university. We employ more than 5400 academic and professional staff to support over 41,000 students, making us one of NZ’s largest employers. Job Description Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Student Hubs team is the physical gateway to the Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. We provide a welcoming, friendly, and mana-enhancing service for future and current students across their student journey. At Student Hubs, our work is guided by the following values that reflect our commitment to collaboration, leadership, learning and community: Mana Enhancing: We’re a place for uplifting interaction Service Excellence: We’re professional, accurate and responsive Manaakitanga: We listen, respect, understand and care Whanaungatanga: We connect, partner and strengthen relationships Kaitiakitanga: We guide, support and see things through We deliver a wide range of services to support students on their journey at Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. Our primary function is to deliver high-quality in-person and online services that support students holistically. Our services include: guidance and advice on being a student navigating the University systems, spaces and processes exploring study options application and enrolment support Library & Student IT services Our recruitment Process As we do not use AI for screening or psychometric testing, we will be using your cover letter to assist us in longlisting, so we encourage you to be as authentic as possible and be mindful when using AI. Phone Screening : To assist us with shortlisting for interviews, we may be giving you a quick call. If you receive our call during an inconvenient time, please let us know and we will be happy to call you back. Group & Individual Interviews : The interviews for these positions will consist of Group and Individual Interviews. They will be conducted on the same day, either on Tuesday 6 October (9 am to 12 pm) or Wednesday 7 October (9am to 12 pm). You need to be available on either of these interview dates and attend both parts of the interview. As we are working within a short recruitment timeframe, we recommend having your references ready early in the process. The start date for all roles will be Monday, 9 November, and there will be a two-week, full-time training and induction programme that will be based primarily at the City Campus. He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for highly motivated individuals to join our team of Student Hub Assistants who: Are collaborative team players who build positive relationships and contribute to a supportive team environment Take a proactive approach and work effectively both independently and with others Demonstrate reliability and a strong, positive work ethic aligned with our values Show empathy and genuine care in supporting tauira | students and colleagues Thrive in fast-paced, dynamic environments and adapt to changing needs Bring a positive, energetic approach to engaging with tauira | students in a high-volume, complex work setting Work Patterns: Location: City Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Saturday and Sunday 9am–5pm and Monday - Wednesday 8am–4pm - Fixed term - from 9 Nov 2026 – 1 Sep 2027 Part Time, 24 hours per week - Thursday 2pm–10pm, Friday, Saturday and Sunday 2pm–8pm Start date - 9 Nov 2026 - Permanent Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Monday - Friday 12pm–8pm - Fixed term - 9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Monday - Friday 10am–6pm - Fixed term - 9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Full Time, 37.5 hours per week - Monday - Friday 11am–7pm - Fixed term - 9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Part Time, 20 hours per week - Monday - Friday 10am–2pm  - Fixed term -.9 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 Location: Grafton Part Time, 20 hours per week - Monday- Friday 10am–2pm - Fixed Term -.09 Nov 2026 – 28 Mar 2027 We also have a number of casual roles available. The salary range is $55,600-$57,000 per annum (pro-rated for part-time positions), depending on experience and qualifications. We will also be recruiting for casual staff to support our peak periods across all our Student Hub sites, so if you are a current University of Auckland student, this may be a great opportunity for you. For more detailed information on the role responsibilities and requirements, please review this Position Description. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply If you are strong team player who is excited about working with people and passionate about leading, then we want to hear from you! Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 24/9/26 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the abovementioned skills and experiences. Note that we may close the advert earlier if we find a suitable candidate. If you have questions or would like a confidential conversation, please reach out to the hiring team via [email protected] . Please note we are happy to answer questions, but we do not accept applications by email . Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149455104": {
      "title": "University of Auckland STC Finance Administrator",
      "description": "University of Auckland STC Finance Administrator | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . STC Finance Administrator Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60006664 UoA Department Name: Shared Transaction Centre Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is a great place to work! We have a collaborative and inclusive environment and offer many benefits not offered by other organisations. The Shared Transaction Centre (STC) is the University of Auckland’s centralised ‘Procure to Pay’ and ‘Order to Cash’ centre. It is responsible for all purchasing, billing, and revenue collection activity for faculties and most service divisions.  We perform an operational function, consolidating high volume finance transactional activities relating to the purchasing and supply of goods and services. Job Description Do you have experience in accounts receivable processes and do you consider yourself a administrative whiz? Join our busy Finance Shared Transaction Centre as an STC Administrator (O2C). You’ll work with internal and external stakeholders to ensure all accounts receivable/O2C transactions are delivered accurately, on time, and in line with service levels. We’re after exceptional attention to detail, strong problem‑solving, and confidence navigating multiple systems and procedures—while helping reduce exceptions, drive automation, and improve the customer experience. Your responsibilities will involve: Interpreting stakeholder requests and providing clear, expert advice to ensure policy compliance and positive outcomes. Managing customer setup and maintenance, processing invoice requests and credit notes, and ensuring accurate, efficient reconciliation. Investigating and resolving complex billing and payment issues, including re‑education of users to reduce repeat errors. Completing daily banking reconciliations across multiple sources; clearing unidentified payments; processing receipt files, dishonours, and transfers to meet critical month‑end timeframes (including related journals). Supporting the Order to Cash Team Leader to execute operational plans and meet agreed SLAs. Building effective relationships with key stakeholders and consistently delivering reliable, efficient, and accurate service. Contributing to process improvements and automation that lift quality, speed, and control. This is a fulltime (37.5 hours per week), 6.5 -month fixed term position. The remuneration is $62,000 - $67,000 per annum (pro rata), depending on skills and experience. He kōrero mōu | About you You’ve worked in a complex, high‑volume environment, thrive under time pressure, and stay current with accounts receivable/O2C best practice. You’re a people person with a can‑do attitude who balances accurate, high‑throughput processing with excellent service to students and corporate customers (transactions, refunds, invoice payments). Ngā Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Excellent organisational and analytical skills. Experience using an ERP (e.g., PeopleSoft) and strong Excel proficiency; quick to learn new, industry‑specific systems. Proven performance in a customer‑focused environment with strong problem‑solving abilities. A clear commitment to policy, control, and compliance in every transaction. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer We offer an opportunity within New Zealand’s leading University that will have a positive impact on the lives of students. We also offer: Five weeks’ annual leave per annum Flexible working – Hybrid model (blend of in-office and remote) Over 300 different career development courses and programmes Discounted parking and various leisure and recreation services A diverse and inclusive working environment For more information please visit Staff Benefits . Additional Information Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Wednesday 23rd Septembe r , 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend Posted by Joshua Chapman share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Academic Operations Team Leader Auckland, New Zealand Postdoctoral Fellow - Te Kura Mātauranga Pāngarau | Department of Mathematics  - 12 months fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Student Accommodation Night Support Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. STC Finance Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149447099": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Kaihōpara Raukura",
      "description": "University of Auckland Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55572820 UoA Department Name: Talent and Recruitment Compensation: up to NZD 70,145 - yearly Job Description Find your path. Build your future. Explore before you specialise Permanent employment from day one Grow professionally, personally and culturally To our alumni, graduates and future leaders, nau mai, hoki mai. The future needs Māori leaders, innovators, specialists and professionals and so do our universities. At Waipapa Taumata Rau, we believe talent grows when people have the opportunity to explore, learn and discover where they can contribute most. That's why we created Waipapa Raukura Rau: a graduate programme designed for Māori graduates to build meaningful professional careers while creating pathways into the breadth of opportunities across the University. Most graduates know the University as a place to study. Few realise it's also one of New Zealand's largest and most diverse employers, with more than 6,000 staff working across technology, data, marketing, communications, project management, student success, finance, innovation, libraries and many other professional fields. Through tailored rotations, mentoring, development opportunities and permanent employment from day one, you'll gain real-world experience, broaden your horizons and discover where your strengths can take you. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity A personalised pathway within one of New Zealand's most diverse workplaces This 12-month programme combines exploration, personalised development and meaningful work experience with the security of permanent employment. Rather than asking you to choose a career pathway immediately, we'll give you the opportunity to experience different possibilities before deciding where you want to focus. As a Raukura, you'll: Explore professional career pathways through four tailored rotations. Build experience across one of Aotearoa New Zealand's largest organisations. Develop your skills through real projects and meaningful mahi. Connect with mentors, colleagues and fellow graduates. Build confidence in your strengths, interests and future direction. Every pathway is different. Potential rotation areas are available across our professional departments, offering access to career paths, skills and directions few employers can offer. From student success, to strategy, to sustainability, and everything in-between. See more detail on our rotation areas and programme on our web page . This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. There is a fixed starting salary for this role - $70,145 per annum with fixed increases after 6 months and at the beginning of your destination role (end of the programme). The salary for this programme is based on a structured salary band system the University uses for all professional roles, considering capabilities, experience and equity with other internal roles. This salary information is provided as part of our commitment to gender and ethnic pay equity . Why we created Waipapa Raukura Rau Waipapa Taumata Rau, University of Auckland is committed to creating a diverse future workforce, one that reflects our students and communities, and brings diverse minds and world-views to the challenges we seek to solve. Central to the University’s aspirations are a commitment to indigenisation, going beyond inclusion to offer transformative possibilities for the whole institution, while ensuring Māori students, staff, knowledge systems and ways of being and doing are normalised. The University seeks to be a place where te reo Māori can flourish and mātauranga Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi are valued, fostered and protected. To reach these lofty goals, we need kaimahi Māori across all levels and sectors of the organisation, and Waipapa Raukura Rau was created to enable this. Find out more about our strategic plan – Taumata Teitei , Kawea Ake - our indigenising initiatives, and Toitū Waipapa – our enduring framework. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Grow professionally, personally and culturally. Waipapa Raukura Rau is designed to help graduates flourish, supported by mentors, career development specialists, experienced colleagues, Kaimahi Māori networks and your fellow graduates. This supportive programme empowers Raukura to: •             Build confidence through hands-on experience. •             Develop technical, professional and leadership skills. •             Strengthen your connections and networks across the University. •             Deepen your connection to Te Ao Māori and grow as a Māori professional. •             Reflect on your strengths, aspirations and future direction. You don't need to have everything figured out before you start. This programme exists to help you discover what's possible. Beyond the programme itself, you'll be joining one of New Zealand's largest and most diverse employers. Few organisations offer the same breadth of professional pathways, communities, expertise and opportunities for growth. Alongside permanent employment and a structured development journey, we offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa’s most diverse and complex organisations Most importantly, you'll have the opportunity to build a meaningful future with a community invested in your growth and success. He kōrero mōu | About you We're looking for curious, motivated graduates who are excited about exploring possibilities and discovering where their strengths can make the greatest contribution. You'll: Have recently graduated, or be due to graduate, with a bachelor's degree or higher from Waipapa Taumata Rau. Whakapapa Māori. Be open to new experiences and opportunities. Enjoy learning, collaborating and trying new things. Be interested in professional, personal and cultural growth. Bring initiative, curiosity and a willingness to contribute. Fluency in Te Reo Māori is not required. What matters most is an openness to learning, growth and continuing your journey as a Māori professional. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Find your path. Build your future. Applications are now open. Please apply online with your CV and a covering letter telling us a bit about you, your aspirations and motivations for applying. Closing date Sunday October 4th . Interview Dates are scheduled for October 9th, with morning and afternoon sessions offered. If you have any questions, please contact recruitment.team@auckland.ac.nz Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland 3 x Health & Safety Adviser – Science and Engineering  – 12-month fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Services Adviser - part-time (18.75 hrs pw) Auckland, New Zealand Postdoctoral Fellow - Te Kura Mātauranga Pāngarau | Department of Mathematics  - 12 months fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149765638": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "University of Auckland Postdoctoral Fellow - Te Kura Mātauranga Pāngarau | Department of Mathematics  - 12 months fixed term  | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Postdoctoral Fellow - Te Kura Mātauranga Pāngarau | Department of Mathematics  - 12 months fixed term Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021513 UoA Department Name: Mathematics Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Te Whare Pūtaiao | Faculty of Science is the leading science faculty in Aotearoa New Zealand and one of the largest in Australasia. More than 9,000 students study across our ten schools and departments, learning from internationally recognised educators and researchers. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tuākana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae The Department of Mathematics is the largest and strongest Mathematics Department in New Zealand, with expertise spanning pure mathematics, applied mathematics and mathematics education. You’ll join a department with a strong research culture and the opportunity to contribute to teaching across a broad range of mathematical disciplines. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We’re looking for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the Marsden-funded research project, “Quantifying energy dependence phenomena for rough scatterers.” This project works lies in the pure/applied mathematics interface and aims to quantify the dependence of scattering properties on energy using semiclassical analysis. The successful candidate will work with principal investigator Melissa Tacy on the project at the University of Auckland and interact with the international associate investigators Simon Chandler-Wilde (Reading) and Julie Rowlett (Chalmers). Candidates are encouraged to contact Tacy directly to learn more about the project before applying. This position comes with NZD10,000 travel allocation, and there are further opportunities to apply for more travel support within the department. There is a possibility of extension of the period of employment to take up teaching roles within the Department of Mathematics. Ngā Haepapa Matua | Key Responsibilities (not limited to): This is an opportunity to build your research profile within a supportive and internationally connected mathematics environment. You’ll: Undertake high-quality research in mathematics, with a focus on analysis. Develop and contribute to research outputs, including publications in reputable journals and conference proceedings. Contribute to the wider research environment through activities such as seminars, events, peer review and academic service. Develop your own research portfolio, with the potential to attract future external funding and postgraduate students. Contribute to teaching activities, which may include lectures, tutorials, seminars, teaching coordination, assessment and co-supervision of students. You’ll work closely with Melissa Tacy on the project and have opportunities to engage with researchers internationally. For more detailed information, review this position description This is a full-time (40 hrs per week) 12-month fixed-term role The remuneration is $92,213 per annum He kōrero mōu | About you We’re looking for someone who has: Completed, or is very close to completing, a PhD in Mathematics with a research focus in analysis , with your thesis examined before the fellowship start date. Research experience in analysis. The ability to work independently and contribute positively to a collaborative research environment. Experience in one or more of the following would be advantageous: Semiclassical analysis Numerical analysis Spectral theory Theory of partial differential equations (PDEs) This opportunity is open to researchers within 0–5 years of PhD award , allowing for career breaks. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave Opportunity for Research and Study Leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 16th October 2026 to be considered. The application must include: Cover Letter and CV, including a list of publications and preprints, a summary of teaching and service contributions, and the names of some people who can provide academic references. Please reach out to Melissa Tacy (Senior Lecturer - Mathematics) via [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Applications are strongly encouraged from under-represented groups, such as women, Māori, and Pacific mathematicians. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149734979": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Student Accommodation Night Support",
      "description": "University of Auckland Student Accommodation Night Support | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Student Accommodation Night Support Part-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55571871 UoA Department Name: Accommodation Halls Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking someone to join us as a Student Accommodation Night Support (internally known as Night Supervisor) to work rostered night shifts in our student residences. This vital role ensures our students feel safe, supported, and part of a positive community. You will be the first point of contact for residents needing pastoral care or well-being support during the night. This isn’t a traditional security role; you’ll lead on health, safety, and security, making sure protocols are followed, emergencies are handled, and first aid is provided when needed. When not responding to incidents or patrolling, you’ll be responsible for completing general administrative and coordination tasks. Our Student accommodation team provides housing for over 4,500 students across a variety of accommodation styles, from first-year halls to self-catered apartments for senior students. We aim to create a safe, supportive, and enjoyable community that feels like home for our students. For more information about this role, please refer to the full position description . This is a part‑time, permanent position. Hours of work will vary week to week and are on a fixed roster between 11:00 pm and 9:00 am , Monday to Sunday . You’ll work an average of between 30 and 35 hours per week (up to 70 hours per fortnight). Shifts alternate weekly, and weekend work is required . The salary range for this role is $ $55,000 to $69,000 , negotiable depending on skills and experience. Training We provide full training, including first aid, mental health event management, and basic security. This paid training will take place during regular business hours before your night shifts begin. He kōrero mōu | About you We’re looking for candidates who are empathetic, passionate about student well-being, and capable of handling challenging situations independently. While you’ll be working alone during shifts, remote support from the team will be available. This role is ideal for those night shifts to fit their lifestyle, and we’ve found it to be a great fit for people with experience in pastoral care, student-focused roles, mental health services, social work, youth work, residential care or hospitality, or even those considering a career as a police officer. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking Be available for night shifts, across Monday to Sunday, including Public Holidays. Ideally, have experience providing pastoral care or support. Possess strong communication skills, able to guide and support others with a gentle, non-enforcement approach. Have some knowledge of security systems, CRMs, databases, and Building Health & Safety compliance (training will be provided). Be physically fit (a medical test by our approved provider is required). This position required successful applicants to pass NZ police vetting checks and vulnerable children’s Act requirements. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on‑site amenities Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Thursday, 01 October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Stanislav Ivanov, Night Manager at stan.ivanov@auckland.ac.nz #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Associate Director, Performance and Digital Auckland, New Zealand Editor – Digital Content Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Student Accommodation Night Support Auckland, New Zealand Part-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
