e Tiriti o Waitangi are valued, fostered and protected. To reach these lofty goals, we need kaimahi Māori across all levels and sectors of the organisation, and Waipapa Raukura Rau was created to enable this. Find out more about our strategic plan – Taumata Teitei , Kawea Ake - our indigenising initiatives, and Toitū Waipapa – our enduring framework. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Grow professionally, personally and culturally. Waipapa Raukura Rau is designed to help graduates flourish, supported by mentors, career development specialists, experienced colleagues, Kaimahi Māori networks and your fellow graduates. This supportive programme empowers Raukura to: •             Build confidence through hands-on experience. •             Develop technical, professional and leadership skills. •             Strengthen your connections and networks across the University. •             Deepen your connection to Te Ao Māori and grow as a Māori professional. •             Reflect on your strengths, aspirations and future direction. You don't need to have everything figured out before you start. This programme exists to help you discover what's possible. Beyond the programme itself, you'll be joining one of New Zealand's largest and most diverse employers. Few organisations offer the same breadth of professional pathways, communities, expertise and opportunities for growth. Alongside permanent employment and a structured development journey, we offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa’s most diverse and complex organisations Most importantly, you'll have the opportunity to build a meaningful future with a community invested in your growth and success. He kōrero mōu | About you We're looking for curious, motivated graduates who are excited about exploring possibilities and discovering where their strengths can make the greatest contribution. You'll: Have recently graduated, or be due to graduate, with a bachelor's degree or higher from Waipapa Taumata Rau. Whakapapa Māori. Be open to new experiences and opportunities. Enjoy learning, collaborating and trying new things. Be interested in professional, personal and cultural growth. Bring initiative, curiosity and a willingness to contribute. Fluency in Te Reo Māori is not required. What matters most is an openness to learning, growth and continuing your journey as a Māori professional. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Find your path. Build your future. Applications are now open. Please apply online with your CV and a covering letter telling us a bit about you, your aspirations and motivations for applying. Closing date Sunday October 4th . Interview Dates are scheduled for October 9th, with morning and afternoon sessions offered. If you have any questions, please contact recruitment.team@auckland.ac.nz Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland 3 x Health & Safety Adviser – Science and Engineering  – 12-month fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Diversity, Equity and Inclusion Services Adviser - part-time (18.75 hrs pw) Auckland, New Zealand Researcher Development Adviser – Maori Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
