    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000149952216": {
      "title": "Researcher Development Adviser",
      "description": "University of Auckland Researcher Development Adviser – Maori | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Researcher Development Adviser – Maori Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60016418 UoA Department Name: Research Development and Impact Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a passionate and culturally grounded Researcher Development Adviser - Māori to join Te Puna Tiketike | Research and Innovation Office. Embedded within the James Henare Research Centre, this role will lead the development and delivery of Māori researcher development programmes, learning opportunities, and mentoring initiatives that strengthen research capability across Waipapa Taumata Rau. The position plays a key role in supporting Māori researchers, fostering collaboration, and contributing to a thriving Māori research ecosystem. Key responsibilities: Design and develop Māori researcher development programmes, workshops, and learning opportunities. Identify researcher capability gaps and develop initiatives that respond to evolving development needs. Collaborate with subject matter experts and stakeholders to create culturally appropriate training and resources. Support initiatives that enhance Māori researcher capability and research funding success. Maintain and promote researcher development resources and learning opportunities. Evaluate the effectiveness of programmes and provide insights to inform continuous improvement. Build strong relationships across the University to support a connected and sustainable Māori research ecosystem This is a fulltime, permanent position . The salary range for this role are $73,400 - $90,700 depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You will bring a strong commitment to kaupapa Māori and a genuine passion for empowering researchers to succeed. You are experienced in building trusted relationships and working collaboratively with Māori communities, researchers, organisations, hapū, and iwi. You enjoy creating engaging learning experiences, can navigate complexity with confidence, and are motivated by work that delivers meaningful impact for Māori research and scholarship Our ideal candidate will bring: Experience working in kaupapa Māori contexts and building enduring relationships with Māori communities. Strong understanding of tikanga Māori and culturally appropriate engagement practices. Experience designing, facilitating, or delivering learning and development programmes. Ability to apply Te Ao Māori perspectives and Māori-centred approaches in professional practice. Strong stakeholder engagement, collaboration, and relationship management skills. Understanding of, or commitment to, Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles and their application within a tertiary environment. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 1 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Heidi Collins via [email protected] Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150552379": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60020825 UoA Department Name: Education and Social Practice Company Description Job Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Education and Social Practice within Te Pūtahi Mātauranga | Faculty of Arts and Education is seeking a Lecturer in Sport, Health and Physical Education to join our collegial academic team. This is an opportunity to contribute to teaching, research and service across the discipline, including the education of future teachers. You will contribute to the delivery and development of our programmes, supervise postgraduate students and develop an active programme of research, while building collaborative relationships across the School, Faculty and wider community. We are particularly interested in applicants whose teaching and research expertise will complement the School's existing strengths in areas such as anatomy, exercise physiology, Health and Physical Education curriculum, games education or sport pedagogy. Key responsibilities include: Delivering high-quality teaching, contributing to curriculum development and supervising postgraduate students. Developing and maintaining an active programme of research, including scholarly publications, research collaborations and external funding opportunities. Building collaborative relationships across the School, Faculty and wider community to support teaching, research and service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week) permanent position (subject to continuation). The workload split is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. The salary range for this role is $99,788 - $119,204 per annum. Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and is aligned with our Academic Standards policy. For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description. He kōrero mōu | About you You are a collaborative academic with expertise in Sport, Health and Physical Education and a passion for educating future teachers. You bring a thoughtful, inclusive approach to your work and are committed to making a positive contribution through teaching, research and engagement. To be successful in this role, you will bring: A PhD in Sport, Health or Physical Education. Experience teaching Sport, Health and Physical Education at the tertiary level. A developing or established research profile, with high-quality research outputs and the potential to contribute to collaborative research and external funding. Experience applying socially critical, inclusive and culturally responsive approaches within teaching, research or professional practice. Strong communication, collaboration and relationship-building skills. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Sunday 4 October to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter outlining your interest in and suitability for the role. Please direct enquiries to Associate Professor Alan Ovens (Discipline Leader, Sport, Health and Physical Education) via [email protected] . Please note while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications via email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150269170": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Employment Relations Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Employment Relations Manager | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Employment Relations Manager Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 00015607 UoA Department Name: HR Advisory Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau is New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to advancing understanding, transforming lives and shaping futures. We have an exciting opening for an Employment Relations Manager /Legal Counsel to join our team. You will work within the supportive and well respected HR Advisory / Ranga Pūmanawa Tangata team, who provide a professional partnership with academic faculties and service divisions to enable the University to deliver on its people and culture priorities. We are family-friendly, flexible and encourage a balance of on campus and remote working. You will be supported by a large team of specialist HR colleagues. Reporting to the Associate Director - Advisory, the Employment Relations Manager & Legal Counsel leads the University’s ER strategy in support of the University’s vision and strategic plan, Taumata Teitei .  With a collaborative and engaging working style, you will manage the relationship with the unions and oversee all employment relations issues. Success will be measured through your ability to coach and influence senior stakeholders and build capability within HR to mitigate risk and achieve results.  Working strategically and operationally, you will provide legal expertise across all areas of employment relations. There will be one Employment Relations Specialist reporting into you. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $155,200 - $210,000 per annum. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more information, please see the position description Qualifications Nga Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills The ideal candidate will hold a Bachelor of Laws (LLB), have extensive knowledge of relevant employment legislation and proven experience of working effectively with leaders, unions and staff on issues affecting a large complex organisation. Outstanding stakeholder management and strong analytical, conceptual and thinking skills are key requirements of the job as is a confident, proactive decision making style. The ideal candidate will be digitally savvy and take a coaching approach to building ER capability. Additional information What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave and the option to buy an additional week, or cash in a week 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information please visit our Staff Benefits page. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 1 October 2026 If you have any questions about this role, please contact Verity Collins – verity.collins@auckland.ac.nz The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive and equitable environment. For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQITakatāpui+, people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other equity issues go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQITakatāpui+, people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other equity issues go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Senior Clinics Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Researcher Development Adviser – Maori Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Employment Relations Manager Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
