    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150230209": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Senior Clinics Administrator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Senior Clinics Administrator | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Senior Clinics Administrator Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 00016361 UoA Department Name: Technical Services Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an enthusiastic Senior Clinic Administrator to support the University of Auckland Optometry Clinic within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences. You will play a vital role in the day-to-day operations of our student-led teaching clinic. This role is responsible for patient support, administration, reception, and the daily activities that keep our busy and diverse clinic running smoothly. Supporting our Optometry Clinic, you'll enjoy a variety of tasks, work with a diverse patient population, and be part of a unique environment that delivers high-quality clinical services while helping students achieve the clinical competencies required for course completion. In essence, you'll be helping to support the training and development of the future allied health workforce. Key responsibilities include Front-of-house and reception responsibilities. Liaising with patients and providing information about our services. Appointment scheduling and diary management across multiple user groups. Invoice processing and payment collection. Processing external claims. Managing Healthlink referrals to Health NZ and other external agencies. Ensuring patient confidentiality protocols and requirements are adhered to. Obtaining and maintaining appropriate consent and documentation prior to service delivery. Supporting clinical teams with the preparation and distribution of clinical correspondence. Maintaining Health and Safety standards in line with University and legislative requirements. This is a fulltime, permanent position The salary range for this role is $61,701 - $71,000 depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You'll have experience in a busy administration, reception or customer-facing role and enjoy working in a fast-paced environment where no two days are the same. In addition, you'll bring: Previous experience in the health sector would be advantageous. Experience processing financial transactions. Experience using patient management systems or similar databases. Experience supporting multiple Optometry services or comparable clinical environments. Strong proficiency across the Microsoft Office suite. A professional and confident phone manner. Excellent organisational skills and a strong commitment to providing outstanding service. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 2 October and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. In your covering letter, please advise if you are interested in the permanent or fixed-term/secondment position, as well as your preferred portfolio. If you have any questions, please email Clivenn Naepi via Clivenn.naepi@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Casual Study Doctor – Neurogenetics Auckland, New Zealand Student Accommodation Night Support Auckland, New Zealand Admissions Manager Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Senior Clinics Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150223079": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Casual Study Doctor",
      "description": "University of Auckland Casual Study Doctor – Neurogenetics | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Casual Study Doctor – Neurogenetics Casual Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022021 UoA Department Name: Medicine Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Neurogenetic Research Clinic, part of the Centre for Brain Research, is a leading centre for clinical research in rare neurological disorders. Our work spans international clinical trials, natural history studies and translational research aimed at improving diagnosis, treatment and quality of life for people living with inherited neurological conditions. Our multidisciplinary team is led by Associate Professor Richard Roxburgh, Consultant Neurologist, and includes neurophysiologists, research nurses, physiotherapists, genetic counsellors and study coordinators. Participant assessments are conducted at Clinical Research Centre (Building 507, Grafton) for research in collaboration with international partners and pharmaceutical sponsors. Current projects include: Phase 3 clinical trials for focal epilepsy (tablets) and myotonic dystrophy (infusion). Observational studies to validate clinical outcome measures, characterise phenotypes and identify biomarkers. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a motivated doctor to join our team as a Sub-Investigator on a casual basis. This is a varied and engaging role that offers the opportunity to advance your clinical research career by contributing to cutting-edge clinical research, improving the lives of people living with rare disorders, and developing expertise in neurological assessment. Following training in specific neurological tests, the role will involve conducting assessments following study protocols, including (but not limited to): Obtaining informed consent and conducting participant eligibility assessments. Taking medical histories and reviewing concomitant medications. Performing protocol-specific physical and neurological examinations. Conducting and documenting study assessments in accordance with Good Clinical Practice (GCP). Monitoring participant safety, including review of laboratory results and ECGs. Communicating clinically relevant findings with participants and their healthcare providers. Performing protocol-specific procedures where trained and required including (but not limited to) venepuncture, rating scales (requiring certification) and common/bespoke study software. Collaborating with study coordinators, allied health professionals and international research partners. Maintaining GCP qualifications and any other qualifications required for studies. Responding to queries raised by study monitors. For clinical trials, additional tasks include: Checking investigational products (IPs) and supervising administration where required. Responding to concerns raised by the Research Nurse that arise during IP infusion including being available to attend emergency calls. Judging whether events are Adverse Events or Serious Adverse reactions and taking appropriate management decisions to ensure participant’s wellbeing. He kōrero mōu | About you Our ideal candidate will bring: Essential Current registration and practising certificate with the Medical Council of New Zealand. Minimum three years' postgraduate clinical experience. Excellent clinical assessment and communication skills. Strong organisational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Current professional indemnity insurance and up to date CPR. Desirable Experience in General Practice or Neurology. Interest in or experience with clinical research. Current GCP certification. Experience with neurological examination and assessment. Experience working with people living with chronic neurological disorders. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 30 September 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Sarah Nagar, via sarah.nagar@auckland.ac.nz , for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Senior Clinics Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Student Support Advisor Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Physiology/Biomedical Science Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Casual Study Doctor – Neurogenetics Auckland, New Zealand Casual I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150216969": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Kaihōpara Raukura",
      "description": "University of Auckland Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55572820 UoA Department Name: Talent and Recruitment Compensation: up to NZD 70,145 - yearly Job Description Find your path. Build your future. Explore before you specialise Permanent employment from day one Grow professionally, personally and culturally To our alumni, graduates and future leaders, nau mai, hoki mai. The future needs Māori leaders, innovators, specialists and professionals and so do our universities. At Waipapa Taumata Rau, we believe talent grows when people have the opportunity to explore, learn and discover where they can contribute most. That's why we created Waipapa Raukura Rau: a graduate programme designed for Māori graduates to build meaningful professional careers while creating pathways into the breadth of opportunities across the University. Most graduates know the University as a place to study. Few realise it's also one of New Zealand's largest and most diverse employers, with more than 6,000 staff working across technology, data, marketing, communications, project management, student success, finance, innovation, libraries and many other professional fields. Through tailored rotations, mentoring, development opportunities and permanent employment from day one, you'll gain real-world experience, broaden your horizons and discover where your strengths can take you. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity A personalised pathway within one of New Zealand's most diverse workplaces This 12-month programme combines exploration, personalised development and meaningful work experience with the security of permanent employment. Rather than asking you to choose a career pathway immediately, we'll give you the opportunity to experience different possibilities before deciding where you want to focus. As a Raukura, you'll: Explore professional career pathways through four tailored rotations. Build experience across one of Aotearoa New Zealand's largest organisations. Develop your skills through real projects and meaningful mahi. Connect with mentors, colleagues and fellow graduates. Build confidence in your strengths, interests and future direction. Every pathway is different. Potential rotation areas are available across our professional departments, offering access to career paths, skills and directions few employers can offer. From student success, to strategy, to sustainability, and everything in-between. See more detail on our rotation areas and programme on our web page . This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. There is a fixed starting salary for this role - $70,145 per annum with fixed increases after 6 months and at the beginning of your destination role (end of the programme). The salary for this programme is based on a structured salary band system the University uses for all professional roles, considering capabilities, experience and equity with other internal roles. This salary information is provided as part of our commitment to gender and ethnic pay equity . Why we created Waipapa Raukura Rau Waipapa Taumata Rau, University of Auckland is committed to creating a diverse future workforce, one that reflects our students and communities, and brings diverse minds and world-views to the challenges we seek to solve. Central to the University’s aspirations are a commitment to indigenisation, going beyond inclusion to offer transformative possibilities for the whole institution, while ensuring Māori students, staff, knowledge systems and ways of being and doing are normalised. The University seeks to be a place where te reo Māori can flourish and mātauranga Māori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi are valued, fostered and protected. To reach these lofty goals, we need kaimahi Māori across all levels and sectors of the organisation, and Waipapa Raukura Rau was created to enable this. Find out more about our strategic plan – Taumata Teitei , Kawea Ake - our indigenising initiatives, and Toitū Waipapa – our enduring framework. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Grow professionally, personally and culturally. Waipapa Raukura Rau is designed to help graduates flourish, supported by mentors, career development specialists, experienced colleagues, Kaimahi Māori networks and your fellow graduates. This supportive programme empowers Raukura to: •             Build confidence through hands-on experience. •             Develop technical, professional and leadership skills. •             Strengthen your connections and networks across the University. •             Deepen your connection to Te Ao Māori and grow as a Māori professional. •             Reflect on your strengths, aspirations and future direction. You don't need to have everything figured out before you start. This programme exists to help you discover what's possible. Beyond the programme itself, you'll be joining one of New Zealand's largest and most diverse employers. Few organisations offer the same breadth of professional pathways, communities, expertise and opportunities for growth. Alongside permanent employment and a structured development journey, we offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme, Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa’s most diverse and complex organisations Most importantly, you'll have the opportunity to build a meaningful future with a community invested in your growth and success. He kōrero mōu | About you We're looking for curious, motivated graduates who are excited about exploring possibilities and discovering where their strengths can make the greatest contribution. You'll: Have recently graduated, or be due to graduate, with a bachelor's degree or higher from Waipapa Taumata Rau. Whakapapa Māori. Be open to new experiences and opportunities. Enjoy learning, collaborating and trying new things. Be interested in professional, personal and cultural growth. Bring initiative, curiosity and a willingness to contribute. Fluency in Te Reo Māori is not required. What matters most is an openness to learning, growth and continuing your journey as a Māori professional. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Find your path. Build your future. Applications are now open. Please apply online with your CV and a covering letter telling us a bit about you, your aspirations and motivations for applying. Closing date Sunday October 4th . Interview Dates are scheduled for October 9th, with morning and afternoon sessions offered. If you have any questions, please contact recruitment.team@auckland.ac.nz Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Employment Relations Manager Auckland, New Zealand 3 x Health & Safety Adviser – Science and Engineering  – 12-month fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer in Sport Health & Physical Education Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Kaihōpara Raukura - Māori Graduate Programme Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150867516": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Criminology",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Criminology | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Criminology Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021556 UoA Department Name: Social Sciences Company Description Join New Zealand's world-ranked Faculty of Arts and Education (#61 QS Ranking) and contribute to the development of our successful Criminology programme Inspire our outstanding students and collaborate with highly reputed colleagues Live and work in Aotearoa New Zealand’s largest, most cosmopolitan and culturally diverse city Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. Situated within The Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna Mārama | School of Social Sciences engages critically with studies of culture, politics and issues confronting humanity and society in the past, the present and into the future. Our academics are regarded as experts in their fields. They are at the forefront of Aotearoa New Zealand’s academic presence in anthropology, archaeology, communication, criminology, gender studies, global studies, linguistics, international relations, politics, public policy, and sociology. Job Description We are seeking a Lecturer in Criminology to join our team to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, supervision of sub-doctoral and PhD students, and to undertake high quality and impactful research. This position offers the opportunity to build your academic career within a dynamic and successful Criminology programme. Criminology has grown significantly over the last ten years and represents one of the most popular majors in the Bachelor of Arts alongside growing postgraduate programmes. Researchers and educators in Criminology at the University of Auckland take broadly critical approaches to the discipline, including an emphasis on Indigenous perspectives, restorative justice, hate crime, cybercrime, penology, gender, policing, prisons, cultural criminology, victimology and justice policies. The successful candidate will have teaching and/or research expertise in one or more of the follow areas: state crime and state harm; white collar crime and/or corporate crime; or green/environmental criminology. A track record of or capacity to engage in public and/or community activism is also desirable. The School of Social Sciences is seeking to grow the number of staff with methodological and theoretical expertise in Indigenous, Kaupapa Māori, Pacific and/or Asian epistemologies. We are looking for a colleague who can develop successful pedagogical approaches to teaching large undergraduate classes as well as teaching into our growing postgraduate offerings in Criminology. Existing experience in postgraduate supervision or mentoring will be an advantage. The successful candidate will be familiar with or open to learning about Te Tiriti o Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi) and be prepared to build or extend relationships with Māori and Pacific communities in Aotearoa New Zealand. The University of Auckland occupies a unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia- Pacific. It is New Zealand’s largest university and an international centre of learning and academic excellence. Auckland is a vibrant, cosmopolitan, and rapidly growing city with a population of 1.8 million, and is the commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including stunning harbours and accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us to retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world in which to live. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical, and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The University is committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential in an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications from Māori and Pacific candidates, as well as other applicants that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our links with diverse communities. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week), permanent employment agreement (subject to continuation). The full remuneration range is Lecture step 1: $99,788 – Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . We are committed to reflecting the diversity of our city and therefore welcome applications from all suitably qualified candidates. Additional Information Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 18 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV including research and teaching statement, full publication record, teaching record and summarised evaluations, and research grants received. Please identify three referees (who will not be contacted without your express permission). Please reach out to Francis Collins, [email protected] (Head of School) or Carisa Showden, [email protected] ) (Deputy Head of School) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150803080": {
      "title": "Associate Finance Business Partner",
      "description": "University of Auckland Associate Finance Business Partner | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Associate Finance Business Partner Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55564831 UoA Department Name: Finance Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking as Associate Finance Business Partner to join the Faculty of Arts and Education. You will partner with academic schools, that include Creative Arts, Education, Humanities, Social Sciences and Te Wananga o Waipapa,  and operational teams to provide high quality reporting, analysis and advice that drives performance and supports strategic goals.  This role combines technical expertise with collaboration, helping shape budgets, forecasts and long term plans while improving financial understanding across the Faculty. Key responsibilities include: Support budgeting, forecasting and long term planning processes Deliver accurate financial insights, reports and analysis to support strategic goals and KPIs Build strong relationships and understanding of key business drivers Collaborate on operational and strategic initiatives across faculties Improve financial literacy and enable informed decision making Ensure compliance with University finance policies and contribute to continuous improvement This is a permanent, full-time role (37.5 hours per week, Monday to Friday). For more information about this role, please refer to the position description. We are committed to reflecting the diversity of our city and therefore welcome applications from all suitably qualified candidates. He kōrero mōu | About You You are a trusted finance professional who combines strong technical expertise with the ability to influence, challenge and partner effectively with senior stakeholders. You understand complex organisations and are comfortable balancing local priorities with broader organisational goals. You bring strong commercial judgement, a collaborative mindset and a genuine interest in helping leaders make informed decisions. You will bring: Previous experience in Management Accounting or experience in a similar role where you have had engagement with senior stakeholders and led continuous improvement projects while demonstrating knowledge of accounting best practices. Demonstrated knowledge of accounting best practice. Experience using Power BI or similar performance reporting tools Be solutions focussed, have strong initiative, and a collaborative approach. Thrive on analysis and financial business modelling to support senior management. Advanced excel skills are a must, with confidence using large ERP systems such as PeopleSoft and Workday. Being a Chartered Accountant (member of CAANZ, CPA or equivalent) is preferred but not essential. What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou We offer you more than just a job. We offer you the opportunity to be part of a dynamic, world-class educational institution. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home if feasible, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . The full salary range for this role is $87,000 - $118,000 per annum (Band G). Additional Information Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before Sunday 4th October, 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Bevan Iles, [email protected] (Director of Faculty Finance) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150791268": {
      "title": "Research Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Research Operations Coordinator | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Research Operations Coordinator Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60021344 UoA Department Name: FMHS Research Support Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Pūtahi Manawa | Healthy Hearts for Aotearoa New Zealand is a Centre of Research Excellence (CoRE) hosted at Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland. It brings communities together with research scientists, clinicians, and educationalists to disrupt the status quo and take action against heart health inequities. Our vision is to achieve heart health equity for Māori and Pacific Peoples. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is so much more than a coordination position, “Business not as usual” is our ethos, so you’ll be a mover and shaker, ready to work effectively and efficiently by harnessing the University processes and systems and leveraging the fantastic relationships you’re able to build both internally and externally. Ideally, you will bring with you an understanding of, or prior experience working with tangata whenua and/or Pacific Peoples and will be able to bring your own experiences to help to fine tune what and how we do things. Our team’s strengths are in its diversity, interdisciplinary /inter-institutional collaborations, and the culturally safe space we have created. This is a great opportunity to kick start your career in research support with a clear career pathway for a talented Research Operations Coordinator to work towards a senior and even ultimately a manager research support/operations role. You will have the support of the wider team across the CoRE and the host faculty to gain experience and develop your career in research support. Key accountabilities include: Support the Pouwhakarite | Māori Strategy Support Adviser with the organisation of our Māori summer research programme. Liaise with relevant stakeholders and coordinate events including outreach and education activities with our Māori and Pacific communities. This may include attending outreach events across the country. Track and manage expenditure for events to keep them within budget Organisation of travel and accommodation for approved internal and external stakeholders. Provide administrative support to the Operations Manager and team, including the organisation of governance, advisory group and other meetings, document production, diary management Support with purchasing, receipting, set-up, and activation of suppliers, ensuring alignment with financial policy and Centre values. This is a part-time (22.5 hours per week, 0.6 FTE), fixed term role until 31/12/2028, in the first instance. Subject to confirmation of ongoing funding, the fixed term period may be extended. The working pattern is flexible and may be agreed with the manager to fit in with family or other commitments. The remuneration is $68,101 - $79,300 per annum, pro rata, dependent on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description . Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team You will be working with the Research Operations Manager, Centre Co-Directors, Pouwhakarite, Pou Tikanga, Pacific Portfolio Coordinator and Senior Research Operations Coordinator at Pūtahi Manawa | Healthy Hearts for Aotearoa New Zealand Centre of Research Excellence. We are all passionate about the mission and values of our Centre and working together to really make a difference. You will also be a part of the Research Services community at the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences with opportunities to participate in all our training and social events. He kōrero mōu | About you You’ll have the ability to quickly pick up University systems, and you’ll be comfortable working independently or with others. Innovation will be a strength of yours, and you’ll be confident to contribute to our aim to disrupt the status quo. You will have: 3-4 years’ experience in the research management sector working with several stakeholders OR the equivalent experience working for complex organisation, with project coordination or financial administration experience Proven customer service experience Culturally responsive, ideally with experience working with Māori and Pacific communities and stakeholders Excellent communication skills and spoken and written English Proficiency with Microsoft Office, including Word and Excel Your coordination and organisational skills will be imperative to your success, you’ll know what is happening, who needs to give you what and when by. You’ll have the ability to follow up but the emotional intelligence to decide when and how to do this. Naturally, with the mahi we do here, you’ll share in our vision and goals and have the health of our people at the heart of what you do. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 6 October 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Linda Fotherby via [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
