    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151342338": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Student Support Adviser",
      "description": "University of Auckland Student Support Adviser – Māori Tauira - Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Student Support Adviser – Māori Tauira - Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55569855 UoA Department Name: Student Support and Engagement Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tuākana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for a Student Support Adviser who is passionate about tauira success, wellbeing, and creating meaningful connections. Based within the Faculty of Science’s Student Support and Engagement team, you will play an important role in supporting Māori tauira throughout their university journey – helping navigate university life, connect with people and services, and access opportunities that support their success. This is a relationship-focused role where whanaungatanga and manaakitanga are central to how you work. You will build trusted relationships with tauira and become a visible and approachable source of support within the Faculty. You will work closely with Māori student networks, Tuākana, Kaiārahi, and colleagues across the University, helping strengthen connections and ensuring tauira know where to go for support, guidance, and opportunities. Nga Pūkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Build genuine and trusted relationships with Māori tauira and student groups, maintaining a visible presence across the Faculty. Work alongside Māori support networks, Tuākana, Kaiārahi and other student groups to promote support, opportunities and services. Take a holistic approach to understanding the needs of individual tauira and help connect them with appropriate wellbeing, academic and co-curricular support. Develop support plans where appropriate and work collaboratively with other University services. Proactively connect with students who may benefit from additional support. Promote and support mentoring, tutoring and study group programmes. Work collaboratively with colleagues across the University Student Wellbeing and Care team to deliver a consistent and positive student experience. Maintain accurate records and follow appropriate processes to ensure effective coordination of student support. Participate in relevant training, including the Marae Model of wellbeing, and contribute to the University’s obligations under the Pastoral Care Code of Practice. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $63,600 to $79,300 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You will bring a strong understanding of te ao Māori and demonstrated experience working with and alongside Māori communities. You will be committed to providing culturally responsive support, guidance, and mentorship to students throughout their journey, while building and maintaining meaningful relationships with Māori whānau, iwi, and community networks. You will bring: Minimum 3 years of experience working with students or young people, with an understanding of their diverse needs. Experience working in a customer-focused environment. Strong relationship-building and active listening skills. The ability to support people experiencing moderate levels of distress with empathy, confidence, and professionalism. Excellent oral and written communication skills. An understanding of the University’s responsibilities under Te Tiriti o Waitangi and equity principles. A tertiary qualification or equivalent experience in a relevant discipline, such as social work. Proficiency in te reo Māori would be an advantage Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and several other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 18th October 2026, and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions about this role, please get in touch with Serena Tiaiti (Student Support & Engagement Mgr) serena.tiaiti@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential discussion. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Postdoctoral Fellow - Te Kura Mātauranga Pāngarau | Department of Mathematics  - 12 months fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Research Fellow - Department of Physics | Te Kura Mātai Ahupūngao – fixed term - 26 months Auckland, New Zealand Pouako|Lecturer - Earth Science - Te Kura Mātai Taiao - School of Environment Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Student Support Adviser – Māori Tauira - Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151178632": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Finance",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Finance | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Finance Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55570732 UoA Department Name: Accounting and Finance Job Description Ranked #67 in the QS World University Rankings 2027 Triple-crown accredited Business School (AACSB, EQUIS and AMBA) The Business School is a community of 6,885 EFT students across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, supported by the strength of 265 FTE academic staff and 105 FTE professional staff Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Join New Zealand’s top-ranked and triple-crown accredited Auckland Business School, with an international reputation for shaping the future of research and teaching excellence, and be part of its continued growth as you embark on the next phase of your academic career. With strong growth in student numbers and sustained investment across the Business School, this is an exciting time to join as it continues to strengthen and expand its academic community. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity As the Faculty continues to grow, we are seeking an ambitious, collegial, and research-active Lecturer or Senior Lecturer specialising in Finance to join the Business School's Accounting and Finance Department . We are seeking scholars with expertise in finance who will contribute to excellence in research and teaching. The successful candidate will contribute to finance teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels, bringing strong theoretical knowledge and research expertise in their area of specialisation. We are open to candidates with expertise in a range of areas within finance and welcome applications from scholars whose research contributes to contemporary finance scholarship and practice. In addition to growing your academic profile and reputation, you will also be committed to shaping and delivering an exceptional learning experience for students, contributing to an innovative teaching environment using a variety of methods. All candidates will bring the potential to contribute service and citizenship within and beyond the University, as well as to actively participate in Department, Faculty and University committees and activities. The Business School attracts high-calibre students to our world-class undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. We take pride in the excellence demonstrated by our internationally recognised scholars, who lead a vibrant research ecosystem supported by interdisciplinary research centres and a global network of visiting experts who contribute to both teaching and research. This is a full-time (40 hours per week) permanent position (subject to continuation). Remuneration depends on experience and achievements, and will be aligned with our Academic Standards policy . It will be within the following ranges: Lecturer (L): NZ$99,788 - NZ$119,204 Senior Lecturer (SL): NZ$126,863 - NZ$158,904 For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review the Position Description. He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a collegial academic with excellent communication skills and the ability to clearly articulate your ideas, research, and future plans. You will hold a PhD in Finance, Financial Economics or a related discipline (or be nearing completion), along with: Potential (Lecturer) or proven (Senior Lecturer) track record of: conducting high-quality, independent research and contributing to publications in leading international journals successfully delivering engaging, research-informed teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels designing engaging learning activities, assessments and/or curriculum supervising Honours, Master's and, where appropriate, PhD students to completion developing research collaborations, contributing to competitive research funding applications, and engaging with industry, professional or other external partners. Industry or practical experience in finance, as well as engagement with professional networks, would be advantageous. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer As New Zealand’s largest, highest ranked and most comprehensive research intensive university, we are committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes and mentoring support Auckland is a vibrant cosmopolitan and growing city with a population of 1.8m (greater Auckland area). The University is located in the metropolitan centre and commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including four stunning harbours, accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world to live. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of Thursday 01 October 2026 (NZT) . Please include a cover letter outlining your interest and ability to relocate if required, CV, statement of your teaching philosophy, teaching evaluations and a statement of the direction and ambition of your research programme, with a full publication, PG supervision and research grant record. Please direct enquiries to Prasanna Gai, Head of Department, via [email protected] . Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but applications must be made online. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151167799": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Market Research Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Market Research Manager | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Market Research Manager Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021333 UoA Department Name: Planning and Information Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is an opportunity to shape how New Zealand’s leading university makes strategic decisions. We are establishing a new market research and insights capability to help the University anticipate change, identify growth opportunities, and respond to evolving student, industry, and research markets. Reporting within the Planning and Information Office, the Market Research Manager will lead the development of a strategic market research function that supports student recruitment, retention, brand positioning, academic portfolio development, and research and innovation opportunities. This role combines strategic leadership, advanced market research, analytics, and stakeholder engagement. You will provide insights directly to executive leaders responsible for enrolment growth, academic portfolio development, international strategy, research and innovation, and institutional planning. You will partner with senior leaders across Marketing and Recruitment, Planning, Academic Divisions, and Research and Innovation to embed insights into decision-making and planning processes. As the function evolves, you will have the opportunity to shape the team's structure, establish new ways of working, recommend tools and platforms, and create a best-practice market research capability that supports the University’s long-term success. Your responsibilities will include: • Translating insight into action • Influencing decision-making • Strategic foresight • Identifying opportunities and risks • Leading organisational change This is a full-time, permanent opportunity (37.5 hours per week). The full salary range for this role is $114,400 - $154,800 (Band I). We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective in the role (target), and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $115,000 - $135,000 , depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. A full description of your duties and the role is provided in the position description linked HERE . He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced insights or market research leader who enjoys turning data into meaningful stories that influence decision-making. You are excited by the opportunity to build something new, establish best practice, and create a market research function that delivers real value across a large and complex organisation. If you have experience working in customer insights, consumer intelligence, commercial strategy, consulting, public sector insights, higher education strategy, or research agencies, we want to hear from you. You bring a strong blend of strategic thinking, technical expertise, and stakeholder engagement. You are comfortable discussing research methodologies and predictive modelling one minute, and presenting recommendations to senior leaders the next. You enjoy challenging assumptions, identifying emerging opportunities, and helping organisations make evidence-based decisions. You are naturally curious, commercially minded, and proactive. You understand that great insights are only valuable when they lead to action, and you have a track record of translating complex analysis into practical recommendations that drive organisational outcomes. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking • Significant experience leading market research, insights, or customer intelligence functions within a large and complex organisation • Demonstrated expertise in quantitative and qualitative research methodologies, forecasting, segmentation, and advanced analytics • Experience establishing research frameworks, governance processes, tools, or platforms that support strategic decision-making • Proven people leadership experience, including building capability, leading teams, and developing high-performing functions • Exceptional stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence senior leaders and translate complex analysis into clear recommendations • Relevant tertiary qualification in marketing, data science, business, social sciences, or a related discipline, with postgraduate qualifications advantageous This role would particularly suit someone who enjoys creating new capabilities, driving organisational change, and building an insights function that helps shape the future direction of a large and influential institution. Additional Information Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer This is a rare opportunity to define a new operating model, research agenda, governance frameworks, and future capability for an entirely new function. This will give you access to internal expertise and resources commensurate with our complexity and commitments, including an in-house design studio and media production teams, audience specialist teams, web and digital teams, AI and automation expertise, and Māori and Pacific specialists across brand, media, communications, and engagement. The University is one of New Zealand's largest and most complex organisations, serving more than 50,000 students and engaging with local and global markets. Whether your interests lie in education, research, innovation, reputation building, community engagement, or student growth, you'll have the chance to contribute across a wide range of portfolios, projects, and strategic priorities while continuing to grow your own career. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives, and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute, and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5-hour working week, flexible hours, and hybrid/remote working patterns, as well as discounted public transport for permanent and long-term fixed-term staff A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% superannuation and annual salary increases with a performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with five weeks' annual leave and an additional leave buy/sell scheme Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave of up to nine weeks’ full pay, and on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people, with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching, and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, and discounted health insurance The opportunity to grow through access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership, and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao Māori and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa’s most diverse and complex organisations Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of Friday, 16 October 2026 . Please include your cover letter and CV , highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities, and relevant experience. Please reach out to Neil Gardner, Senior Recruitment Consultant, on neil.gardner@auckland.ac.nz with any questions. Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Economics Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Finance Auckland, New Zealand Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Market Research Manager Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151150828": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Schools and Community Engagement Adviser",
      "description": "University of Auckland Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019816 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Are you a confident communicator and relationship builder who knows how to engage ambitious young people? We’re looking for a Schools and Community Engagement Adviser to join our high-performing team and help strengthen the University’s connections with secondary schools, students, whānau, and school communities. This is more than an outreach role. You’ll be part of the University’s student recruitment journey, helping attract high-achieving students and positioning UoA as a university of choice. Our Schools and Community Engagement approach is grounded in Manaakitanga, Whanaungatanga, and Kaitiakitanga — caring for people, building lasting relationships, and taking responsibility for the communities we work with. What you’ll be doing You’ll take ownership of an assigned portfolio of schools and become a trusted first point of contact for students, teachers, careers advisers, and school communities. Your days will be varied. You could be: Building strong relationships with schools and careers advisers and understanding what matters to their students. Representing at school visits, information evenings, events, and other engagement opportunities. Delivering confident, engaging presentations to students, teachers, and school leaders. Supporting the attraction and engagement of high-achieving secondary students, including students considering Cambridge and IB pathways. Using application, enrolment and engagement data to identify trends, opportunities and areas of focus. Developing tailored resources, digital content and engagement activities for different student audiences. Helping organise and deliver major University events and initiatives — and being someone who is comfortable rolling up their sleeves to make them happen. Working across the University with faculties, communications and marketing, student services and other teams to create a connected prospective student experience. The University of Auckland Contributing to projects and improvements that make it easier and more engaging for students to explore their future at The University of Auckland . Providing accurate, timely and genuinely useful advice to students and their whānau as they navigate their options. The role combines relationship management, recruitment, presentations, events, project work, data and strategy **Ideal start date January 2027** This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $73,400 to $90,700 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You’re someone who can build rapport quickly, but you’re also comfortable holding your own. You’re confident, personable and strategic enough to see the bigger picture. You’ll bring: Excellent interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills. Strong presentation and facilitation skills, with the confidence to engage a room full of young people. A natural ability to build and maintain genuine professional relationships. The judgement to understand different audiences and tailor your approach accordingly. Strong organisation skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities, events and projects. A practical, solutions-focused approach — you see something that could work better and look for ways to improve it. Confidence working with data, systems and digital tools, with the ability to pick up new technology quickly. An analytical mindset and the ability to turn information and trends into useful insights. A strong customer and stakeholder focus, while maintaining appropriate professional boundaries. The confidence to work strategically and contribute ideas, rather than simply waiting for instructions. Must hold a full, clean New Zealand drivers licence Experience working within the New Zealand secondary school sector and an understanding of the tertiary environment would be valuable. Experience working with academically focused schools or students following Cambridge or IB programmes would be an advantage. An understanding of te ao Māori and confidence engaging respectfully with Māori and Pacific communities would also be highly valued. A commitment to equity and inclusion is important to how we engage with young people and their whānau. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: · Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) · Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme · A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 23rd October 2026, to be considered. We may close this advert early if we have a large number of applications. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Isabella Mory (Scholars Outreach & Engagement Manager) via isabella.mory@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist Auckland, New Zealand Associate Director, Performance and Digital Auckland, New Zealand Associate Director, Postgraduate Recruitment Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151140605": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Development Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Development Manager - Fundraising  | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Development Manager - Fundraising Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 00016813 UoA Department Name: Alumni Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Development Manager to fundraise and partner with our Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences (FMHS) and institutes, connecting world-leading research, teaching and health initiatives with individual donors, corporate partners and philanthropic organisations. Together, these organisations form a nationally significant health, research and innovation portfolio dedicated to improving health outcomes for New Zealand and beyond. Bringing together expertise across medicine, health sciences, engineering and technology, they support groundbreaking research, transformative education and collaborative partnerships that address some of society's most pressing health challenges. As part of the Alumni Relations and Development team, you will manage a portfolio of major gift prospects and supporters, building strong relationships and developing tailored engagement strategies that lead to significant philanthropic investment. Working closely with the Dean, senior academic leaders, researchers and colleagues across the University, you will help identify opportunities that inspire donors to support transformative research, student success and innovation. This is a highly relationship-focused fundraising role that combines strategy, donor engagement and major gift development. You will guide prospects through the full donor journey, from identification and cultivation through to solicitation and stewardship, building trusted relationships with high-net-worth individuals, trusts, foundations and corporate partners. Success in this role comes from curiosity, initiative, strong commercial acumen and the ability to connect donors with opportunities that create meaningful and lasting impact. Your responsibilities will include: Managing a portfolio of major gift prospects, trusts, foundations and corporate partners, developing strategies to secure significant philanthropic support Building strong relationships with donors, faculty leadership, researchers and University stakeholders to identify and advance funding opportunities Developing donor engagement plans, proposals and stewardship activities that foster long-term relationships and investment Working alongside the Dean and senior leaders to prioritise prospects and align fundraising activity with faculty priorities Supporting the development and implementation of faculty fundraising strategies, plans and performance measures Collaborating with colleagues across Alumni Relations and Development to deliver coordinated donor engagement and fundraising initiatives This is a permanent, full-time position ( 37.5 hours per week). The salary range for this role is $118,000 to $128,000 , negotiable depending on skills and experience. A full description of your duties and the role is provided in the position description linked HERE He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced fundraiser or relationship manager with a track record of building meaningful, long-term relationships that lead to significant financial investment. You are comfortable engaging with high-net-worth individuals, philanthropic trusts, foundations and senior corporate stakeholders, and understand how to move relationships from initial engagement through to successful solicitation and stewardship. You are naturally curious and enjoy understanding people's motivations, interests and aspirations. You know how to develop trust, identify alignment between donor interests and organisational priorities, and create compelling opportunities for support. Equally comfortable working independently and collaboratively, you thrive in a relationship-driven environment where success comes from strategic engagement, persistence and excellent stakeholder management. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking Demonstrated success securing major gifts, philanthropic investment or high-value commercial partnerships through relationship-based engagement Experience managing a portfolio of high-net-worth individuals, trusts, foundations, corporate partners or key strategic stakeholders Proven ability to identify, cultivate, solicit and steward supporters through a structured relationship management or moves-management process Exceptional relationship-building and influencing skills, with confidence engaging senior leaders, donors and external stakeholders Demonstrable commercial acumen, with experience developing proposals, delivering presentations and achieving revenue or fundraising targets A relevant tertiary qualification, or equivalent experience, combined with a genuine interest in supporting world-leading medical research and health education Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on‑site amenities Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday, 11 October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Neil Gardner, Senior Recruitment Consultant, via neil.gardner@auckland.ac.nz Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist Auckland, New Zealand Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Economics Auckland, New Zealand Student Support Adviser – Māori Tauira - Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Development Manager - Fundraising Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151139328": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Communication",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Communication | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Communication Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021555 UoA Department Name: Social Sciences Company Description Join New Zealand's world-ranked Faculty of Arts and Education (#61 QS Ranking) and contribute to the development of our successful Communications programme Inspire our outstanding students and collaborate with highly reputed colleagues Live and work in Aotearoa New Zealand’s largest, most cosmopolitan and culturally diverse city Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. Situated within the Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna Mārama | School of Social Sciences engages critically with studies of culture, politics and issues confronting humanity and society in the past, the present and into the future. Our academics are regarded as experts in their fields. They are at the forefront of Aotearoa New Zealand’s academic presence in anthropology, archaeology, communication, criminology, gender studies, global studies, linguistics, international relations, politics, public policy, and sociology. Job Description Te Whiwhinga Mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Lecturer in Communication to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, supervision of sub-doctoral and PhD students, and to undertake high quality and impactful research. We particularly welcome and encourage applications from communication scholars with expertise in Māori and/or Pacific epistemologies and pedagogies, cross-cultural communication, political communication as well as those with existing research and/or teaching relationships with community, public sector and/or industry organisations. This position offers the opportunity to build your academic career within a growing and successful Communication programme. The University of Auckland offers Communication within a specialist Bachelor of Communication, as a major within the Bachelor of Arts, and in Masters and PhD programmes. Researchers and educators in Communication have expertise spanning new media and communication technologies, Indigenous data sovereignty and governance, cultural labour, feminist media studies, social media and digital environments, journalism studies, social change, computational media, technology ethics, gig work and digital and platform economies. The successful candidate will have teaching and/or research expertise that adds to or extends the current strengths of the Communication programme. The successful candidate will be familiar with or open to learning about Te Tiriti o Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi) and be prepared to build or extend relationships with Māori and Pacific communities in Aotearoa New Zealand. The University of Auckland occupies a unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia- Pacific. It is New Zealand’s largest university and an international centre of learning and academic excellence. Auckland is a vibrant, cosmopolitan, and rapidly growing city with a population of 1.8 million, and is the commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including stunning harbours and accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us to retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world in which to live. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical, and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The University is committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential in an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications from Māori and Pacific candidates, as well as other applicants that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our links with diverse communities. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week), permanent role (subject to continuation).The full remuneration range is Lecture step 1: $99,788 – Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity. Preferred start date of 1 March 2027. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . We are committed to reflecting the diversity of our city and therefore welcome applications from all suitably qualified candidates. Additional Information Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Monday 26 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV including research and teaching statement, full publication record, teaching record and summarised evaluations, and research grants received. Please identify three referees (who will not be contacted without your express permission). Please reach out to Francis Collins, [email protected] (Head of School) or Carisa Showden, [email protected] ) (Deputy Head of School) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151138560": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Criminology",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Criminology | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Criminology Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021556 UoA Department Name: Social Sciences Company Description Join New Zealand's world-ranked Faculty of Arts and Education (#61 QS Ranking) and contribute to the development of our successful Criminology programme Inspire our outstanding students and collaborate with highly reputed colleagues Live and work in Aotearoa New Zealand’s largest, most cosmopolitan and culturally diverse city Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. Situated within The Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna Mārama | School of Social Sciences engages critically with studies of culture, politics and issues confronting humanity and society in the past, the present and into the future. Our academics are regarded as experts in their fields. They are at the forefront of Aotearoa New Zealand’s academic presence in anthropology, archaeology, communication, criminology, gender studies, global studies, linguistics, international relations, politics, public policy, and sociology. Job Description We are seeking a Lecturer in Criminology to join our team to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, supervision of sub-doctoral and PhD students, and to undertake high quality and impactful research. This position offers the opportunity to build your academic career within a dynamic and successful Criminology programme. Criminology has grown significantly over the last ten years and represents one of the most popular majors in the Bachelor of Arts alongside growing postgraduate programmes. Researchers and educators in Criminology at the University of Auckland take broadly critical approaches to the discipline, including an emphasis on Indigenous perspectives, restorative justice, hate crime, cybercrime, penology, gender, policing, prisons, cultural criminology, victimology and justice policies. The successful candidate will have teaching and/or research expertise in one or more of the follow areas: state crime and state harm; white collar crime and/or corporate crime; or green/environmental criminology. A track record of or capacity to engage in public and/or community activism is also desirable. The School of Social Sciences is seeking to grow the number of staff with methodological and theoretical expertise in Indigenous, Kaupapa Māori, Pacific and/or Asian epistemologies. We are looking for a colleague who can develop successful pedagogical approaches to teaching large undergraduate classes as well as teaching into our growing postgraduate offerings in Criminology. Existing experience in postgraduate supervision or mentoring will be an advantage. The successful candidate will be familiar with or open to learning about Te Tiriti o Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi) and be prepared to build or extend relationships with Māori and Pacific communities in Aotearoa New Zealand. The University of Auckland occupies a unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia- Pacific. It is New Zealand’s largest university and an international centre of learning and academic excellence. Auckland is a vibrant, cosmopolitan, and rapidly growing city with a population of 1.8 million, and is the commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including stunning harbours and accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us to retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world in which to live. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical, and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The University is committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential in an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications from Māori and Pacific candidates, as well as other applicants that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our links with diverse communities. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week), permanent employment agreement (subject to continuation). The full remuneration range is Lecture step 1: $99,788 – Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . We are committed to reflecting the diversity of our city and therefore welcome applications from all suitably qualified candidates. Additional Information Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 18 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV including research and teaching statement, full publication record, teaching record and summarised evaluations, and research grants received. Please identify three referees (who will not be contacted without your express permission). Please reach out to Francis Collins, [email protected] (Head of School) or Carisa Showden, [email protected] ) (Deputy Head of School) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000150929589": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Sociology",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Sociology | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Sociology Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021554 UoA Department Name: Social Sciences Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. Situated within the Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna Mārama | School of Social Sciences engages critically with studies of culture, politics and issues confronting humanity and society in the past, the present and into the future. Our academics are regarded as experts in their fields. They are at the forefront of Aotearoa New Zealand’s academic presence in anthropology, archaeology, communication, criminology, gender studies, global studies, linguistics, international relations, politics, public policy, and sociology. Job Description Te Whiwhinga Mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a 2-year fixed-term Lecturer in Sociology to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, supervision of sub-doctoral students, and to undertake high quality and impactful research. The successful candidate will have teaching and/or research expertise in one or more of these fields: Māori and/or Indigenous Sociology, decolonial social sciences, Asian diasporas and communities, social justice and/or applied sociology. We particularly welcome and encourage the applications of critical sociologists and related social scientists with methodological and theoretical expertise in Indigenous, Kaupapa Māori, and/or Asian epistemologies. Researchers and educators in Sociology at Waipapa Taumata Rau have expertise in social theory, Pacific sociologies, higher education, critical mental health studies, science and technology studies, urban sociology, disasters, migration and population, sports sociology, transgender studies, social movements and social justice, amongst other areas. We seek a colleague who will bring research and teaching expertise that compliments and/or extends the current Sociology staff profile and teaching programme. The successful candidate will be familiar with or open to learning about Te Tiriti o Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi) and be prepared to build or extend relationships with Māori and Pacific communities in Aotearoa New Zealand. The University of Auckland occupies a unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific region. It is New Zealand’s largest university and an international centre of learning and academic excellence. Auckland is a vibrant, cosmopolitan, and rapidly growing city with a population of 1.8 million, and is the commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including stunning harbours and accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us to retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world in which to live. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical, and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The University is committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential in an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications from Māori and Pacific candidates, as well as other applicants that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our links with diverse communities. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week), fixed-term role for 2 years. The full remuneration range is Lecture step 1: $99,788 – Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . We are committed to reflecting the diversity of our city and therefore welcome applications from all suitably qualified candidates. Additional Information Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Friday 9 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV including research and teaching statement, full publication record, teaching record and summarised evaluations, and research grants received. Please identify three referees (who will not be contacted without your express permission). Please reach out to Francis Collins, [email protected] (Head of School) or Carisa Showden, [email protected] ) (Deputy Head of School) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
